   368	### New canon minted (author's list)
   369	- **The Oxhollow slip (flag):** real paper, a careful unpractised hand, the hold's mark a horned head, broad between the horns, over three wavy strokes. *The high traverse, Oxhollow to Thornwater. To be walked with a cart, and mapped. Paid at Oxhollow, on the map: seventy marks.* No coin is left in Pike's box; the hirer pays on delivery and marks the slip.
   370	- **Why the job exists (flag):** an Oxhollow cart fetching Thornwater grain goes round by Lowmarch's ferry, eight days each way; the salt crews speak of a shelf over the high ground between, and a mule string once came over it in three days. Oxhollow is "a ford with a fence round it"; its keeper takes a copper a wheel.
   371	- **Which way it is walked (flag):** east to west. The eastern foot is at the lower of the two crossings on the Thornwater road, where a side water comes in from the north; an old cart-track, grown over, climbs it for two hours to a gorge. Thornwater is not visited and the stretch is not walked.
   372	- **The smith's terms (flag):** the cart is the party's own, sold on day 12 and cut to a mule's gauge (four feet between the wheels, five from hub to hub). Six marks laid down against its return, which is the hire; six more if it does not come back. Paid at first light on the 71st: in hand, forty-six. It is drawn by hand, two in the shafts.
   373	- **The boxes go (flag):** the ferryman is filling the loft over the drum with hay against the cold and wants all of it.
   374	- **Brom's strap:** a doubled bight of the ferryman's long rope over his head and one shoulder, made fast low on the cart's near side; he draws with his chest and keeps both hands behind him. On the bench he walks on the up-slope side and leans against it. **The ferryman's rope went into the gorge with the cart.**
   375	- **Shale-backs, as seen (flag):** as long as a cart and broader; four legs like tree-stumps; back and flanks roofed with overlapping horn plates, keeled, the largest along the spine, lichen growing on the old ones; a small head carried at knee height, ending in a beak like a tortoise's, with which they scrape lichen and moss off rock; they shed plates (grey, the size of a shield, built in leaves like a hoof). Half-grown calves have paler plates and keep to the inside. They mow a shelf in a line, a length and a half apart, and the line moves about forty yards an hour without any animal being seen to walk. Sounds: rasping beaks, and plates clattering "as a slate roof settles".
   376	- **Pike's two tales (flag):** a salt string on the cold road, under a ledge herd, whose Force shifted a fallen block with his Path, and "the hill came down, with the herd upon it"; and roofing-stone cutters who used to come up the side water with carts and one year did not come back. Pike could not say which kind of loud.
   377	- **Karis's derived page (flag), as read out:** *Shale-back. Seventy-fifth day. One: it does not answer fire at three hundred and fifty yards, nor sound, nor a blow on its own shelf. Two: it answers something given off by one of its own in fright, at once, the whole herd together, by moving away. Three: by that, and by the carters' rule, it will answer a declaration in the same way, and a declaration is a great deal larger than a frightened animal. Derived. Not tested.* Copied into Cael's log in his words, with what he saw at the west end. On the map's margin it reads as derived on the seventy-fifth day and used on the seventy-eighth, and Karis will not write that it is tested, since she could not see both ends of her line.
   378	- **The mountain (flag):** the fan (two hundred and sixty yards, a gully along its gorge-ward edge, rock islands; climbed in six stages); the bench (thirty feet at best, pitched outward a hand or two to the yard; slab, with tongues of loose plates where gullies cross); a stone dropped from the first hundred is heard at a count of four and a piece, and again off the far wall; four sleeping places under rock, each found by Seln; eight narrowings (eleven feet; nine; nine and a half; seven; eight; six and a hand; five feet nine with a crust at the rim; the eighth nine feet wide and broken clean across by a slot of two feet); the bay (two hundred yards by forty, floored with shale, a seep at its eastern corner); the pale rock, or fin (see the event list's amendment); the ledge (three hundred yards, six to eight feet wide, roofed in parts); the lip (thirty-one paces, four feet wide, tilted, forty-four scratched stones); the shoulder; a mule track down the western side; a valley road north-west to Oxhollow, a day and a half.
   379	- **The map (flag):** one long pasted leaf: two ruled lines for the shelf, with crosses for hollow places, hatched tongues for loose stone, a black bar at the eighth, and nothing drawn beyond the bay; beside it six columns (number, width, pitch, footing, water, and whether it takes a cart, with a reason); a separate column numbering the narrowings; the cart's own gauge in a box at the head. **Sixty-three lines**, sixty-two in Cael's hand and the last in Karis's. The verdict is Karis's last column and Pike's later line: carts go round.
   380	- **The stampede, as fixed:** see chapter 19 and the event list. Lira's burst is involuntary and is her own Path. Karis's line is nine points and eight channels, about sixty yards, held eight seconds; it empties her. Three or four heavy falls are heard after the cart; nobody sees them. From the western ledge on the 81st the herd counts twenty-five.
   381	- **Karis's lamp:** a small brass reading lantern that lives in the left-hand box with her pens.
   382	- **Oryn, as seen (flag):** not tall, square in the shoulder, broad in the hand, hair cropped as short as a boy's; a man's brown coat made for a bigger person and taken in ("off a peg that nobody was using any more", given by a hold in her first year); a low hoarse voice; a small brown mule with a pack-saddle, two panniers and a crossbow in an oiled cover lashed on top. She stops a stride and a half from people. Cael puts her at about ten years older than himself; no age is stated. Her right heel is rubbed raw and taped, and has been for two rounds.
   383	- **Tide, as taught (flag):** contact only. What mends is the patient's own current, which stands at a break "like water behind a board"; she finds where it stands, opens the way with her own, and holds it open while the body does six weeks' knitting in under an hour, and she pays for the six weeks. A reserve "like a purse", spent at the rate of what it mends: three mendings in a day if she has slept under a roof and been fed, two on a road. She will not touch a table until she has eaten and slept two hours. She cannot do it to herself. She mends what she can read. **The surface reading:** hands on the hurt, or two fingers on a wrist and two on the neck, for a count of ten; it costs her nothing she would notice. **The deep reading ("the long one"):** both hands, all of her, minutes, the patient still and whole, at a mending's cost; not run on a road. A person's current runs in a bed, and "the shape of the bed is their Path"; she names Karis as Ember by it. **The stall:** a hand on a wrist for a breath as the other means to declare, and the declaration does not come; it costs a day's mendings and the next day's and mends nobody; she has done it and hates it. No count of times is given.
   384	- **Tape (flag):** linen strip three fingers wide, spread with pitch, mutton fat and something from a hold that keeps bees. Five bands from breastbone to spine, laid on the out-breath. Twenty-one days from the 78th; she means to lift it herself. For Lira's ankle: an anchor turn above the joint, two strips under the heel and up the outside, and a figure of eight.
   385	- **Brom's wrist:** mended on the 80th; a cuff of plain linen "to remind the hand"; a week from the middle of that day: it takes what sits on it and nothing that hangs from it.
   386	- **Oryn's route, as told (flag):** seven holds, twenty days, "thirty-odd" rounds; late eleven times and this the twelfth, five days behind; nobody has put a coin in her hand past the Line; holds feed her and the mule, one shoes the mule, one gave the coat. She began with four holds and added the fifth in her third round, the sixth "for an old woman's sake" and the seventh because of a child. In a city she was one of six on a healing house's staff, where the morning list is kept by tier. No city is named.
   387	- **The carter's hand:** the carter who told her of the late crew, at the foot of the lip, had a split palm that she had promised to do that evening; he hauls to Thornwater.
   388	- **Oxhollow (flag):** on the north bank of a broad shallow river, in a hollow, behind a palisade of split logs with a gate on the south side toward the ford and another on the north; a ford of a hundred yards of gravel under a foot of water; a yoke of red oxen with a chain, for drawing carts through; the keeper's low stone house with a turf roof between the gate and the water; a long house; a well; the healer's table of scrubbed ash on two trestles under a lean-to against the gate-house, with a bench along the wall. The keeper is old, bent, with a notched hazel tally-stick, and cannot read; he keeps his coin under a hearthstone. He would have sent three carts over the top at the next dry spell with a Force walking behind.
   389	- **Roles (all unnamed):** the smith; the ferryman's boy; Oxhollow's keeper; the woman who keeps Oxhollow's gate; the woman with the scald, the ox-driver, the boy with the collarbone, and five others on the bench; the man with the shoulder, the nursing mother and the old herdsman at the next hold; the child and its mother at the hold after; the carter with the split hand.
   390	- **Teague (flag):** two holds' work in four weeks, a drain cut and a byre roofed; two mules. The bow's limp is an old hip that went out and seated itself wrong, ten years or more ago. Teague timed his second slip to meet the healer's round at the upper hold on the 81st and waited a day. On the hill above Oxhollow he asks her whether the hip is a thing she would do ("Not on a road. Not in a night."), and she tells him his five are in the yard ("They're not mine." / "No. They're not anybody's. I noticed that.").
