editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:151:"That's all a hirer wants to hear from me," he said. "Did they come back, and did his goods come with them. I've been asked it every day for thirty years and I've not yet been asked a second question."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:161:"Copper, Force, thirty years on the roads," he said. "Pike. Now you know less than you did."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:197:A man who had been posted from town to town for fifteen years had been written into a book in every one of them, always by some other hand, and under a grade.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:245:"Bronze," said Seln mildly. "Nobody has asked me in fifteen years. They had it on a roll."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:253:Nobody had sent him, and he was clear with himself about that, as he tried to be about most things. For fifteen years somebody had always sent him, and there had been a line on a sheet to say where, and a second line for the hour at which he was to be back. When he left the last of those houses he had expected that particular habit to be among the first to go.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:247:Something lifted in him then, soft, behind the other eight. It came as it came at every dusk, in a meadow or on a road or at the turn of a carter's ruts. It was offering to take him out of the light. He stood ten cords and more inside the line, on the far side of a floor where a Shadow of fifteen years had found nothing to lift, and it came up in him exactly as it always had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:173:"I have gone fifteen years without leaving one of those on anything," said Seln. He was looking at the strip with real interest. "It was the second thing I was taught. I find that the hand does not want to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:265:"I've stood over that valley thirty years," he said, in his dry unused voice. "I know what it sounds like when they take a cart. You can hear it from the fence, with the wind right." He turned his eyes on Lira. "I stood on that bank through the whole of this, and none of you shouted."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:61:"One: it is a thing that happens, and I have called it the Quieting. Two: five sigils of five Paths failed at one stride, in one way, and came back so. Three: the floor is older than the oldest stone that I can date. Four: the registry has put iron on the edge of it." She shut the notebook on her finger. "And there is a hypothesis, which is mine, and which is not entered anywhere."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:141:Vastin received the broker's completion report as a courtesy copy, forwarded through a registry office that had no interest in any board east of the Line, in the second folder of the morning, marked *Fallow Ring: crew returned, instruments not recovered*. He read it twice — a broker's report from a town on no registry map, on his desk with no routing note — and did not know why it had reached him, and filed that, in the register of a man who had never once in forty years pretended to knowledge he lacked, as the whole of what he knew about it. He returned the copy to its folder unannotated.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:157:*The reading turned outward. I ask first. Every time. If I ever don't, that's the day I became the instrument.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:13:"One. A bar of iron of the registry's pattern, bearing its stamp and the number thirty-one, stands on the edge of the Quieting at the Fallow Ring, a boot's width from the place where I found that edge with my feet. Two. By its rust, and by the turf grown against it, it has stood there about five years. Three. It was driven at the far end of a measure, so that whoever drove it had found the same circle that I paced, and had a cord or a chain to find it with. Four. I do not know what the number counts, or what was written down afterward, or where it is kept."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:267:She was alone on the walk, and that was new. The lean man had gone down to his supper while there was still light in the west, which Cael had never known him do; and he had left a girl with one arm sitting against the southern post, and the yard bow lying spanned on the planks beside her knee. She had the whole valley in front of her, grey going to blue, with the frost coming down on a mile of pale grass and a white stone standing in it; and it came to him that in thirty years nobody but one man had sat where she was sitting at that hour.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:153:He set the stock down on the rail of the walk. There was a place on that rail worn pale and hollow, where his own left hand had rested for thirty years, and the stock lay in it as an egg lies in a spoon.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:269:"It's older, I'll say once and not again, than [UNBOUND] — older than the word they retired, and I can't say by how much. Entered as a limit, not a finding. We explained the middle of the story in Ostrand. This is before the beginning, and I don't have a ruler for it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:193:*the Quieting*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:197:It was made out of a verb, like the other; it named a thing that went on happening, to whatever came over the line, for as long as it stayed there. It did not say how, and it did not say why. It took *the* in front, which made it one thing, and it would take a place after it without complaint: the Quieting at the Fallow Ring. She could write *inside* before it, and *the edge of*, and *a site of*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:257:"The Quieting." She made a face. "It sounds like what you do to a baby."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:279:"It is a good word for a report," said Seln. "I should have been glad of more words of that kind, in fifteen years." He considered the leaf. "There will be another name for this in the west, on the docket of a tin. I never read the docket. I do not know what they call it, and I shall not guess."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:111:"I've a knife and fifteen years. It was never most of what I do."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:131:He did not watch her go. If a one-armed man had been wrong for thirty years, the answer to it would come out of the grass on the eastern side, a quarter of a mile off, where three runs crossed a line; and somebody had to have his eyes there when the air broke. He fixed them on that place and kept them on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:207:"I said it. I'm bound to say it; twice, the day it came, to the whole grass, with the price." He turned the cream sheet a little with one finger. "The broker's clerk brought it up from the landing the morning after the last sack, sealed as you see it, with a hundred marks in a bag, counted. I've had that seal on bales for thirty years. I never had it before on a slip that sends a crew two days past Thornwater."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:213:He said that last quite mildly, and Cael understood that he had been told something about the man. Pike wrote down whatever was put in front of him and read it to anyone; but he had found, in thirty years, one paper that he would not hang up between two fences and a wheel, and he had kept it where a person must go looking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:261:He had not meant to say it aloud. It had been going round in him since the plank, a thing that he could not sort. A man had walked a road for thirty years, and each time that he came to a certain place he had gone round, and each time he had made a mark in his head. He had never once put his foot over, and he knew the number of his own refusals to the unit.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:289:"I ask first. Every time."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:57:*Filed with your record. It reads: left in good standing, undefeated on paper. I've kept this ledger forty years and never had a line like it. The session still stands. The road still runs through Ardenmere. — V.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:179:"Then we shall have walked to a ring of stones, and a one-armed man has been wrong for thirty years."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:220:He watched her do what she did at a bench. She did not guess. She went over what her hands had found, strictly, and came to the one thing that a healer must ask first.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:15:He turned that over in the dark, since he had the leisure. He had said once, at a fire in a meadow, that he kept waiting for someone to ask for his report. For fifteen years his reports had been asked for by a slot in a door; for a hundred days after that they had been asked for by nobody, and he had gone on walking the first hour of every road, alone, and saying what he found or not as it bore.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:47:The seal was a stranger matter. It guarded the place where a boy had learned that craft, and the place was a man. Seln had spent fifteen years keeping other people's secrets in drawers; he had not looked to end by being one himself, kept shut on a hillside by a person of eighteen who had weighed a friend's throat against it and had still stopped to count who was looking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:53:He had been keeping off it since noon. It had stood at the back of everything that he did that day, as a figure stands at the back of a room: a square bar, driven at the far end of a measure, with a stamp on its neck that he had seen on every door he had walked through for fifteen years.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:59:He carried what he had carried as a careful clerk carries it, in drawers, each one shut. It was how a man could bear fifteen years of other people's paper and still sleep. But one of them, at the stake, had shifted on its runners, and all day it had stood a finger's breadth open, with nothing to be seen in it but dark.

