editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:5:Cael noticed that before he noticed her. She was a broad grey-braided woman of fifty or sixty with her sleeves rolled to the elbow. She stood in the gap of her gate and counted them through it as a farmer counts sheep at a hurdle: the dark coat first, then the two of them abreast, and the mule, and the cart, and last a very large young man with a hedging bill. She moved her lips for each. When the tailboard was inside she put her shoulder to the gate and shut it, and dropped a bar as thick as his arm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:11:Inside the fence there was noise. Cael had forgotten, in a mile, that there could be. A dog was barking at the mule, and a door stood open on a lit room, and somewhere a child was being told for the last time to come in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:13:The carter took his hand off the mule's nose. He wiped the palm down his breeches, slowly, and turned to the five of them, in the lantern's light, with an expression that Cael could not at first place. It turned out to be respect, on a face that was not much used to showing it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:31:He seemed to think that covered it, and he led the mule away toward the barn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:91:She had asked nobody. She went round the inside of the posts, the whole quarter mile, flat out, with the geese scattering, and Cael saw her come down the last straight toward the gate with her head back and her arms going. Three strides short of the bowman's platform she did what she had spent a month on a gravel bar failing to do. She stopped. She did it between one stride and the next, dead, on both feet, with nothing at the end of it but herself, breathing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:121:He had waited for the hour, and he went without his Path, as he had gone everywhere since a crossroads fire; Shadow was a declaration like any other, and he had told four people to make none. Nobody had given him the reason for the hour, and he had not sought it; but a carter of twenty-two years kept that rule, and so did a hold that had stood behind this fence for longer, and he had not lived to his age by supposing himself wiser than the people of a place. The man on the platform watched him lift the bar. He did not call down. Seln had the feeling of being entered in a column, correctly, by somebody who would not trouble him about it afterward.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:139:That was the form, and it had made itself without his leave, as it did. He read the last sentence over. He had written *nothing further to report* at the foot of seven hundred returns, and he had never before written that he did not know a thing. It had always been possible to leave such matters out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:143:The first was the road they had come by, and the second went north from a wicket in the fence, up to grazing, and ended at a stone hut with nobody in it. The third left by a gate on the eastern side and went on up the valley, over a low place in the hills. He had walked two hours along that one, yesterday, with the sun on his head. It was a road like the others, with ruts that had been used, though not lately; and nobody had told him that it was different from the rest, and nobody had told him that it was the same. He turned back at the two hours because he had promised himself that he would be within a fence, any fence, well before the light began to go.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:147:He considered, walking back up the ruts to the gate, whether any part of it bore upon the day in hand. That was the rule he had made for himself, and he applied it with care, since he distrusted rules that he found convenient. They were inside a fence. They would go down this valley again behind a cart, at noon, with the sun over them. On that day there was nothing in his eight roads that five people needed in order to put one foot before the other, and so he would keep it, as he had kept the rest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:159:The carter was back on the fourth day, an hour past noon, with the mule in a lather.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:161:He had gone out at noon on the first full day of their stay, with the witnessed slip in his hat and nothing in the cart, sitting on the tailboard as he had not once sat while the grain was on it. An empty cart behind a rested mule, it seemed, could do in one day what a loaded one did in two. He had brought salt up with him, and a keg of nails, and a bolt of blue cloth that three women came out of the house to look at.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:169:*Thornwater road, the stretch. Walked ahead of one cart, one carter, one mule. Delivered at the fence. No losses. Nine marks, paid.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:171:"He's counted the mule," said Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:175:"I'm not complaining. I'd have counted it myself."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:211:He went to see to his mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:223:*Deployment since the Line: none.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:3:A mule came over the saddle at a trot, and Cael was on his feet before he knew what had woken him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:11:"You can't know a mule at half a mile."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:15:The boy rode straight down through the tents. A person coming in from a road went round the carts, by the river side, at a walk; this one came between them, and a woman snatched a pan out of his way, and not one voice was raised at him, which Cael noticed. The mule was dark with sweat to the girth and carried her head low. He saw the rider's face as they went by the ash, and he knew it. It was a brown boy of twelve or so, who had been sent for a hammer and a billet of ash on the morning a rung broke, and had come back with both at a run.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:27:There was one other man in the meadow who had not gone back to his fire. The Thornwater carter had loaded yesterday, forty sacks under a net, and he had been buckling a trace when the mule came by him. He stood now with the leather hanging from his fist, and he was looking at the boy's back as a man looks at a door behind which somebody is ill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:55:The boy had not turned round. He stood with the mule's rein in both hands, and his shoulders were up about his ears.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:79:Nobody in the meadow moved. Cael saw a big man in a new coat find something that wanted his attention in a mule's near hind foot. A woman with a red cloth round her hair would have stood up, he thought, but she had gone up the north road two days ago with her three, and there was no other line on that sheet that Pike's eyes had rested on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:115:Cael looked at the five strokes drying under a struck name. They were short, and all of a height, and each of them was a person: one who ran, and one who carried, and one who counted, and one who walked every road first. The fifth was himself, who had reached for the paper. If that river kept any of the five, the stroke would stand on the slip all the same, since Pike did not unwrite things.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:127:"Then it's a habit." He was already walking back to his mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:153:They camped cold, in the hollow they had used before, well back from the stream. The carter saw to his mule and ate what he had brought, looking at the ground between his boots. When he had done he wiped his knife on his knee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:169:*Deployment since the Line: none.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:187:They came to it in the morning of the fifty-second day, with the sun still behind the eastern hill and the grass grey with dew. The ruts ran down through a gap in the scrub onto a beach of pale stones, and went into the water, and the carter stopped his mule ten yards short and stood with his fist in the cheek-strap.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:199:Cael saw. The white stones ran out from the near bank in their row, a dozen of them, and stopped; and for the last five yards before the middle of the river there were none. One lay on the rock by itself, a little out of the line, where somebody had set it down meaning to come back to it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:201:In the middle the river had built itself an island. It was a bar of gravel, a long narrow heap of stones that sat on the ledge like a boat run aground upon a weir, with its blunt bow upstream and its stern trailing away below, between the two dark places, to a point. The road came up out of the water onto the bar and crossed it and went down into the water again on the other side.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:205:He took his cart over without another word, and it was the first time Cael had watched him do it as a thing that could go wrong. He led the mule a step at a time. Brom went at the tail-board with both hands on it, and halfway across the near channel the off wheel slid a hand's breadth toward the white stones, quite gently, as a plate slides on a wet table. Brom leaned, and the carter clicked his tongue, and it came back into its groove.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:207:On the far beach the carter stopped to let the mule blow, forty yards below the crossing, where she could drink without fouling it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:213:"I timed myself," she said. "From when the canvas went out of sight. I looked at the river again in less than a minute." She took a cord out of her coat, with knots in it. "I believe that I'm improving. Will somebody pace for me? I want it in yards, and mine aren't."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:231:Cael stood on the bar's head and made his own catalogue, as he would have made it of a floor before a bout. There was the near beach, and the cut bank behind it, as high as a man, with grass along its top. There was the far beach, wider, where the mule had stood. He counted the large stones along the far waterline, the ones too big to shift in a flood, since he wished to know that shore when he saw it again in a worse light: seventeen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:27:Pike did not wait for her to finish. Another slip lay ready under the edge of his stone, where somebody had left it for him earlier in the day. He drew it out with two fingers, and read it to himself, and read it again, and he was giving it to the meadow before the last of their silver was off the oak.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:29:"North road. Four carts of salt and iron walked to the upper holds. Delivered at the last gate. One mule lamed. Fourteen marks."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:45:"One mule lame at taking," said Pike, and dipped his pen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:57:Pike looked past her, down the length of the meadow. There were perhaps two hundred people in it that afternoon, cooking and mending and arguing over mules, and Cael watched him consider them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:69:The man after her was a big one, in a new coat, and he was pleased with himself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:81:"It rained on us the last mile. You can't call that the carrying." He had a good loud comfortable voice, and he looked round at the people by the plank as he used it, inviting them in. "Eleven bales went up on my mules and eleven came down to the landing. Eleven bales, eleven marks. That's the slip."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:121:Cael said nothing, for he knew the road that ford was on, having walked it twice now, at a mule's pace, with his mouth shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:189:It was the one word, and it had a line to itself. Below it came the rest, twelve of them, one under another, each a name with a number of people after it, or a trade where a name would not serve. He read them all, and they meant nothing to him yet, though one crew, about the middle, seemed to be entered under the name of its mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:221:"Four lines. Nobody lost, nobody hurt, nobody knows you." Pike held up his one hand and folded the fingers down as he went. "A boat. A fence. A wagon out of a ditch. A mile behind a mule. I'd hire you tomorrow, myself, for any of the four. But a man comes to me from a hold with something that's eating his stock, and asks who he ought to have. I can't give him a crew that's never yet had anything go wrong." He let the hand fall. "It's a good book. It's a short one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:279:*Deployment since the Line: none.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:285:Nobody in the room was watching him. He checked it, as he checked it everywhere, and the count came out at nothing, and for the first time it did not go round again by itself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:309:It was a week, and he had the whole of it in his head already, a stage at a time, as he would have had a load. Salt went up to the top holds before the frost shut the pass, and it went on mules, and the mules went on a track that was a ledge for two miles of its length with a wind across it. He had done it nine times, and the price was fair. The bow's leg would stand a week of that if he set the pace for the leg and gave some other reason; and the boy would carry more than his share and have to be stopped, and the spear would say nothing at all for seven days and miss nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:345:*That one first*, he thought. He ought always to have been first. The one a man finds on his second pass is the one he should have looked for on the first, and Teague entered it against himself, as a thing to be remembered on a road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:349:He was the youngest of them. He had a book in front of him, shut on a pencil, and he sat quite still in the long light from the door with his hands on the cover; and he was not staring, as boys stared who wanted a place in a crew. He was reading. Teague could see the order of it. The boots. The wrist, and the leather on the wrist. The three in the doorway behind, one at a time, left to right. He was going over a stranger as Teague himself went over ground, and he was not troubling to hide it, any more than Teague was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:357:He stayed on the sill a breath longer than he needed, all the same, and let himself be read to the end. It seemed only fair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:365:The right wrist was strapped with a band of leather four fingers wide, worn black, and laced on the inside, where a man can lace a thing himself. Nobody wears that for show; there was an old hurt under it, or one that he expected, or both.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:5:He had been awake a good while, as Cael knew by the gelding, who had been watered and picked out and now stood with a full net in front of him, and by the tailboard itself, which had been swept clean. On the clean oak Brom had set down four small heaps of grain in a row and a fifth by itself, and he waited until all of them were looking before he began.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:23:"The carts here are half the width," said Karis slowly. "I wrote that down on the sixth day and never asked myself what it meant."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:59:"There's a man over there," he said, "with a mule that can't walk in a circle."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:65:He was in the shed when they came, with the old mule's off hind foot between his knees, and he did not put it down to look at them. The shed was a ring of posts under a cone of thatch, and in the middle stood a drum of oak as thick as a barrel, with the wet rope coiled on it, and from the drum a beam ran out level, fifteen feet, with a padded collar at its end. Round the whole the earth was trodden into a perfect circle, as hard as a threshing floor.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:67:"He's done," said the ferryman, to the hoof. "I've known it since the thaw. He stops, and I shout, and he goes on because he's honest, and it's a poor trade for the both of us." He let the foot down at last. "What's that?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:75:The ferryman came out from behind the mule, wiping his hands on his thighs, and walked round the gelding once. He ran a hand down each leg in turn and lifted the feet, and then he took hold of the upper lip, not roughly, and looked in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:99:"Look at him," said Lira bitterly. "A fortnight I've hauled that animal over a continent, and talked myself hoarse at him, and all he ever wanted was to go round and round a post."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:107:They settled the price on an upturned bucket, Brom and the ferryman, and it took a good while, because Brom had counted the old mule's stops. There had been eleven in a single crossing, the day before, and he knew how many carts had stood waiting on the far bank through each one. He set that sum against the gelding's teeth and went on setting it there, patiently, until the ferryman stopped talking about teeth. Cael did not hear the figure, though he heard what went with it, for that had been his own notion: dry room for three boxes in the loft above the drum, where the ferryman's boy slept, for as long as the horse walked underneath.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:109:"And the mule?" said Brom, when they had shaken on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:123:The cart went to the smith the same afternoon. He was a square, silent man, and he wanted it for the iron on its wheels and the oak in its bed, and said he would cut the axle down to a mule's gauge and let it out by the day to anybody fool enough to want wheels on those roads. Brom emptied it first, and from under the seat board he drew out a hedging bill with a hooked blade and an ash handle worn dark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:207:So they stood, and Seln placed them without being asked: one at each forward corner, and one aft on either side, and the fifth amidships by the goods, where the casks were. He changed the fifth at every crossing, as he changed the sleeper on a watch, and Cael noticed that he never once put himself where the sun would be behind him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:309:He wrote the day's entry short. *Fourteenth day. First line: six crossings stood, nothing lost. Earned, two. In hand, less than one, and a side of bacon. The reserve is not counted here and never will be. Thirteenth of thirteen, Lira says. Deployment since the Line: none. Pressure, Storm and Wind all offered when a tree came down the river, and Brom's Path offered too, and all four were told no.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:31:"That's a mule," said Brom. He had his hand over his eyes. "An old one. He's got a bad off hind, and he's being asked to do it anyway."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:37:The carts went first, as was plainly their right, two to a crossing. A boy took a copper for each wheel and a copper for each beast, and he looked at the gelding as the courier had looked at it, and charged for it as a mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:47:The old man had gone straight to the ash. He drew his two mules into its shade as a man walks into his own kitchen, and nobody else stood within twenty feet of him, though the shade was the best in the meadow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:55:It smelled of mules, and tar, and of something frying. A woman went between the tents with a board on her head and flat loaves stacked on the board; a man sat cross-legged in the grass with a harness across his knees, driving an awl through it with the heel of his hand. Three children and a yellow dog were trying to get a goat to go somewhere it had decided against. After nine days of ridges and a river, it seemed to Cael as loud as a city.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:67:"A copper a night to stand. The grass is free for a mule." He looked at the gelding, and then up at Brom, with some sympathy. "He'll not do on the grass, will he. They don't. Hay's by the net."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:99:He had done this once before. He had been new to a district, and not long past his Kindling, and he had stood in front of a board at a crossroads in Ardenmere for the better part of a morning before he let himself go near the yard it spoke of. A girl had told him afterward that almost nobody did that. Most people either never looked, or walked straight in and were carried out the same day, and he had done a third thing, which was to stand in the road and try to understand the board first. It had served him then, and he did it again now, slowly, a slip at a time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:111:*Strayed, one mule, grey, off the cold road; half its worth to who brings it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:117:"And a strayed mule."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:119:"I saw the mule. I'm not fighting a mule."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:241:"I'd mine ready as well," said Brom. "Copper. For good, and I can say why." He turned his big hands over. "And then the man with the book's a Copper himself, and he says it the way I'd say I was tall."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-02.md:281:*Deployment since the Line: none.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:7:Nine marks earned, the book said. It did not say that Lira's palms had skinned over, or that Brom could now drop a tree at the first slip and catch it again at the second, or that Karis had held a point unlit to the count of three hundred and then put it out herself. Cael kept those in his own book, at the foot of each day, under a line that had not changed since a ring of churned turf.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:9:The carter came in at dusk with one cart and one mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:11:He was a short, thick man, burnt as brown as a nut, and his cart was loaded to the rails with sacks. He chocked his wheels, saw to the mule, and sat down in the ring with a yard of empty ground on either side of him. Cael had seen him once already that day, at noon, handing a slip across the plank; and he had watched the meadow read that slip all afternoon, crew after crew, and go back to its dinner.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:47:He said no more, and the fire talked for a while by itself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:81:They went out by the east road, over the saddle. Beyond the top the country changed, as Seln had said it would: the road shrank to two ruts in a grey scrub that stood as high as a man's belt, and the mule cart, which had looked a toy in the meadow, fitted those ruts as a foot fits a boot. The three boxes rode on the sacks under their own canvas. Karis walked beside the off wheel, where she could have put her hand on them, and never did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:89:The mule took to Brom at the first halt. It walked the length of its tether to reach him and laid the whole weight of its long head on his shoulder, and sighed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:95:Cael watched the carter at that halt, and at the next, and learned more from his hands than he had from his mouth. The man carried a lump of mutton fat in a rag, and he worked it into both axles every time the cart stood, though neither had begun to complain. His trace chains were bound in strips of old sacking from end to end, so that they could not ring. He had cut the iron heel-plates off his own boots, a long time ago by the look of the leather, and when the mule threw up its head at a bird he laid two fingers on its nose before it could think of making a remark. He had been asked what was on his road, and had declined to say; but everything he owned had been taught not to make a sound, and Cael supposed that was an answer too.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:115:The carter said nothing at all. He took the mule's head, and they went on faster than before, and the wired wheel ticked at every turn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:123:The carter had stopped the mule. He stood with his fist in its cheek-strap and looked up the valley, and he spoke for the second time that day.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:139:"I shall say where I want each of you, and I should be obliged if nobody argued until we are at the far end." His voice was hardly louder than the wind. "I go ahead, thirty paces, on the left verge. The boy and Lira walk ten paces in front of the mule, one to a rut, and you do not look at each other; you look outward. The carter keeps his beast's head. Karis is at its off shoulder, where the cart is between her and whatever is nearest. Brom is behind the tailboard."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:153:The cart had its own noises, and he learned them, since they were the only ones he could do nothing about. The wired wheel ticked. The grain shifted in its sacks. And on the mule's harness, somewhere behind the saddle-pad, a loose buckle clicked against its own tongue at every step.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:157:A little way past that, on the left, something stood up out of the grass that was not grass. It was a cart-shaft. There was only the one, grey and split, pointing at the sky at the angle of a man's raised arm, and whatever it had once been fastened to lay down in the stems where nobody could see it. The carter did not turn his head as they went by. Karis did, and then looked very steadily at the mule's ears.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:161:She did not stop walking. She only raised her right hand to the height of her shoulder, with the fingers shut, and held it there. She had spent her life listening to moving air and knew its sounds better than a carter knows his mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:167:The hole was moving. It went along beside them through the bottom, up the valley, at exactly the pace of a mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:179:It showed itself at the half mile.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:201:Karis was at the mule's off shoulder with her face as white as her fingers. Her right hand had gone to the breast of her coat, where the notebook was. It stopped there. It came down. He saw it go up again within ten strides, and stop on the same button, and come down; she was a person watching the one thing in a month that she most wanted to write, at the one moment when taking out a pencil might, for all she knew, be a declaration too. He counted, since counting was what he had. She decided against it five times in that mile.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:203:Behind her the carter walked with his palm spread over the mule's nostrils, and the mule's ears lay back along its neck.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:207:Cael had seen him fill a doorway and had seen him fill a district's frame. He had seen him make a harness room feel small by standing up in it. He had never seen this. Brom was walking with the bill held low across his thighs, and his arms were brown to the wrist, and somehow he had drawn in everything about himself that was more than bone and muscle and a coat. He was exactly as large as his body and no larger. It was still a good deal of body, and it was all there was, and it walked at the pace of a mule and looked at nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:211:Wind had his feet first. They had leaned for a month, a dozen times a day, and they leaned now toward the rise as if the ground sloped that way. Pressure began to gather under his breastbone, slowly, a breath at a time. The Iron read wanted to open and tell him the mass of everything within a pace and a half, which was nothing but a mule; it did not feel like doing anything, and he shut it. Ember offered a point in the dry grass at the foot of the swell, and behind it another and another, so that he could see how the whole slope would take fire along a line. Anchor had found the wired rim and asked about it. Blade wanted Brom's bill. Storm turned over, once, like a great fish under a boat, and showed him the air between himself and the grey thing as a lane already laid.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:217:He had done this for two years so that a house on a bluff might keep its charter, and he had hated every Fifth-day of it. He was doing it now so that a mule, and a man with twenty-two years on a road, and four people he would not have traded for a tier, should come to a fence. It was the same act, and it felt entirely different.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:239:A gully came down out of the right-hand hill, and at its foot the floods of other years had spread a fan of pale stones clean across the valley, a hundred feet wide, with a skin of water shining on it. The ruts ran straight over. The mule's feet rattled in the wet, and the cart came after with a noise like a sack of nails. Out on the right the grey shape came to the edge of the fan and stopped as if it had met a wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:247:It had done nothing. It had answered nothing. For a mile it had gone beside six people and a mule like a man kept waiting outside a door. Such a man has been told that a word will be said, and that he is to do nothing until he hears it. They had not said the word. He did not know what the word was. But he had put down nine hands at every stride for a mile, and Lira had held her feet, and Karis her pencil, and Brom had been only a large man with a hedging tool. Not one Path had been declared on that road, and not one fragment had answered; and so the thing had been given nothing, and had reacted to nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:21:The second time she pulled up on the line itself, stumbling, with her weight all wrong and her face dark with temper, and said that it was like running hard at a door and then leaving it shut. The third time she ran as she must have run on a painted floor with a coach watching. She came down the bar like something thrown; and three strides short of the line the air cracked, and the gravel went up in a fan, and she was standing twelve feet beyond his mark with her hands out in front of her and the heron lifting off the far bank.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:29:"I mean that I didn't decide it. I was running, and then I'd gone." She looked at her own feet as if they belonged to somebody she had trusted. "It went off by itself."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:79:He had told three people what it cost them, and he thought he owed the book the same account of himself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:105:*Deployment since the Line: none.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:123:"No. It gets quicker. On the hill, at the end, I didn't hear myself doing it; it was only a kind of leaning back, all day." He shut the book on the pencil. "I thought that was the worst of what they'd done to me, that I'd got so I could do it in my sleep. I'd have given a good deal not to be good at it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:191:"So far," said Karis, "one fact about a fence and one about myself. I'm not at all sure which I'd sooner have had."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:213:"Then it's a complaint. Against himself."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:243:*Twenty-fourth day. Second line: forty posts, cut and set. Four marks and flour. He said "Cheap," and Brom has kept the word. Earned, six. At dusk the sealed one took my left foot for a stride, on an empty road. Rent. Deployment since the Line: none.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:253:Nobody had sent him, and he was clear with himself about that, as he tried to be about most things. For fifteen years somebody had always sent him, and there had been a line on a sheet to say where, and a second line for the hour at which he was to be back. When he left the last of those houses he had expected that particular habit to be among the first to go.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:259:He did not use paper, for he had not put pencil to it for that purpose since a crossroads, where he had lit a fire with the last one. But the old form ran in him like a tune, and as he climbed it set itself out in the voice he had kept for the audit. *Cold road: leaves the north road at the dead tree, eleven hundred paces above the last roof. A mule's width from the outset. Water at the second turn, forty paces off the track, to the left. Three places of concealment below the crest, none showing use.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:263:It was long, and it stood straight, and he had found the same at every one of them, on five roads, for twenty days. A man who stands in a place, morning after morning, to watch a road leaves the mark of his feet there, and of his waiting, and Seln knew that mark as a baker knows his own thumb in a loaf. He had left it himself in every town he was ever posted to. There was none of it here. The grass had never been stood in, and he did not think that there was, in all this country, one person whose work it was to watch another and write down what he did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:265:He had not known that he would find that restful. He stood in the notch himself for a moment, from habit, and then he stepped out of it again, feeling a little foolish.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:273:He did not offer it. That was not sullenness; he had examined himself for it, with some care. He told them whatever bore on the day in hand: he had given them the gauge of the east road when a horse depended on it, and he had put five people where they ought to stand on a boat. The rest he kept, as he had always kept the rest, and he went about with it and waited for the level voice. *What did you see?* It would come at a fire, he supposed. The boy would say it, since the boy wrote everything down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:285:A boy brought the slip in at first light on the thirty-third day, on a mule that had been made to trot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:299:The woman with the hides was on the western lip with her two mules. She had got a third of her load up the ramp by herself, a bale at a time, and she was grey to the hair with dust. Cael knew her, for she had once laid a barley cake on the shaft of their cart, and she had been standing ten feet away when an old man asked him what he was. She pointed at the bed of the wash with her whole hand, and that was her greeting.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:305:A pole went under the broken corner, lashed fore and aft, so that the wagon would ride on three wheels and a skid. The long rope went from the tongue up the western ramp to the mules, and to Brom. That was the haul. But the bed fell away downstream, and a thing dragged across a slope will always swing its tail to the low side and go over. So a second line must run from the tail-ring back to the eastern bank, and somebody must stand there and pay it out, a foot at a time, and never let it go slack.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:315:The mules leaned, and the wagon groaned and came a yard, grinding, and the skid bit and held, and Cael kicked the wedge in under the hind wheel. It came another yard, and on the eastern lip Lira let a foot of line go round the stump, and then another, with her whole body bent back against it, and the tail came round sweetly behind the nose.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:323:The wagon settled onto its skid again. Then, having been denied that way, it went the other: the rope up the ramp slackened and snatched, a mule lost its feet, and the whole dead weight of the thing came back down the haul rope into the one man who had it across his shoulders.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:339:The check line had fouled. Cael saw it as the mule found its feet again: a loop of it had dropped off the lip and caught behind a rock in the bed, twenty feet out from the eastern bank, and while it lay there Lira could neither pay out nor take in. She had seen it too, from above. It was twenty feet of open air to that rock, and she could have been standing on it in the time it takes to blink.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:343:"Now," said Brom, and the mules leaned.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:355:She thought about that, and she neither took her own words back nor argued with his; and then she clicked her tongue twice at her mules.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:21:He chose the splints himself, and they were the mountain's own; he found two plates in the bank above the wall, each as long as her shin and no thicker than a slate, and he laid one down either side of the leg. They had no canvas to tear, for the canvas had gone with the cart. Cael's coat had lost its left sleeve at a river. He got the knife into the seam of the right one, and Seln cut that as well, and Brom bound the two plates on with it in three places, one-handed, with Seln's fingers for the knots.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:63:Above the wall, in the dark, a plate let go by itself and slid, and stopped; and a little later another.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:75:He knew what she was telling him. He had given her the order of it at the wall's end, the ledge and then herself, and she had been turning it over ever since to see whether it would bear.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:109:He went for water first. The seep was at the bay's eastern corner, a hundred and thirty yards from the rock, and every yard of the way lay under the stripped face, where the plates were still coming down singly, with no warning but a small dry tick. Cael watched him cross it without once hurrying. He stopped whenever a slide began, and judged its line, and stood still until it had gone by, sometimes within a few feet of him; and he came back in the rain with the skin full and grey grit to his knees.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:145:It was the middle of the eightieth day. The rain had gone through, and Cael had got himself propped at the western end of the rock, where he could watch the ledge; he had been watching it since Seln went out along it in the first grey light and passed from sight where the shelf was broken. In six hours he had seen nothing move on it but a bird.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:155:"There is a healer behind me, with a mule. I did not fetch her. I met her below the shoulder at sunrise, already on her way up, and she has not altered her pace since for anything that I could tell her about a leg." He looked at Lira's. "She said that she would see it for herself, and that it was a quarter of an hour either way."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:157:The clinking stopped beyond the wall's end. Somebody said one word to an animal; and then a woman was standing at the corner of the rock, a stride and a half from where Lira lay, with a cheek-strap in her fist and a brown mule's long head at her shoulder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:159:Her eyes went over the five of them a single time, and it did not take her as long as it takes to count five. He saw them go to the slates on a leg, and to a dark cloth at a wrist, and to the way that he himself was sitting, with one arm shut against his side; and they rested longest on Karis, who had nothing wrong with her that could be seen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:171:"You're not. I can see your leg from here." The woman put the cheek-strap into Seln's hand without troubling to see whether he would take it. "Water, somebody. I've had these on a mule since before it was light, and I'll not put them on that as they are."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:175:He began with the hands, since she was holding them out. They were broad and brown, and the nails had been cut as short as nails can be. She was not tall, but she was square in the shoulder, and she wore a man's coat of some thick brown stuff that had been made for a bigger person and taken in. Her hair was cropped as short as a boy's. He thought that she was perhaps ten years older than himself, and he could not have said why he was sure it was not more. On top of the mule's pack, lashed along it in an oiled cover, there was something with the shape of a crossbow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:241:It took the greater part of an hour, and he counted it by the shadow of the wall, which crept the width of two hands across the floor. After the first of it the hollow was silent. Brom held Lira as he would have held a full pail, and Seln stood at the mule's head with the rein in his fingers; and Cael watched a stranger's face, and saw it grow a little older while he looked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:247:Oryn did not get up. She turned herself about on the floor, slowly, and let herself down with her back against the rock, a little at a time, as a carter sets down a load whose weight he has known for years.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:253:"One mending. A Tide has a reserve, like a purse, and it's spent at the rate of what it mends. Mine holds three in a day, if I've slept under a roof and somebody's fed me, and on a road it holds two." She wiped her face with her sleeve. "After that the reserve's gone, and I'm a woman with a mule."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:269:It was her own bread, out of the mule's off pannier, a dark close loaf from some hold's oven, and she broke it in six and gave five pieces away before she bit into hers. There was a cheese as well, and four onions. Brom looked at the three rows on the lid of the box, and then at the pannier, and swept the rows back into his bag without a word.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:309:"They were right. I put the mule's feet on four of them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:385:*Deployment since the Line: one.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:387:On the far side of the hollow the healer was asleep already. She slept sitting up, against the mule's pack, with one boot off and her hands turned palm upward on her knees.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:11:"No. You'd not know to." She had looked across at the carter, who was backing his mule between the shafts of an empty cart. "He'll be at the last bend above the beach, with her nose in a bag. Either way there'll be something to carry."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:113:One went to the right, toward Lira, and the other came for him. He set himself to meet it, with the knife low, and it was not there. Its teeth clicked on the skirt of his coat, and it was back out of reach, a clear yard, with its head level, before his arm had finished moving.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:263:She had said so herself, yesterday, about the tail. She had not thought to say it of the sides.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:15:Brom leaned on his rope through all of it, and Cael thought that he had never seen him so content. He had stood idle in a yard for five days and in a meadow for eleven. Here he had found a way of putting his whole weight to use that a woman with a needle could not have forbidden, and he had found it by himself. Iron Skin had not once risen to meet a load on that shelf. Cael had his own things to keep down there, for Compression rose whenever the rope snatched, and Wind leaned at every yard that Lira ran, and he paid the rent on both fragments and spent neither.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:27:*Deployment since the Line: one.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:49:"I want to measure it again," he said. "Myself."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:89:"She told me not to pull, and that the wrist would say if I had. I carried a box up a fan on my back, and you looked at the wrist yourself, and it was dry." He got up. "A cart's a box with wheels on. It sits on me. There's nothing hanging from a hand."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:93:He had it ready, and it was not the one that she expected. On any floor he had ever stood on he would have decided this himself, and in a breath, since the plan was always his. But the thing in front of him was a weight and a gap, and whether a back could take the one over the other; and there was a person on that shelf who had known such sums since he was twelve years old, and it was not Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:123:"I've stopped arguing," said Karis. "I'm only counting now." She had his left arm turned to the light already. The cloth was dry at the wrist, and she let it go. "Eight. Nine feet. Broken across, clean, two feet wide, no bottom found. Cart: taken over on one man's back, with four to steady her." She stopped. "And then I shall write that a carter by himself does not cross it, and that two planks of oak might serve, eight feet long, and that I haven't tried them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:177:*The nine: held, the whole length of it. Anchor asked at the seventh as it has never asked. Deployment since the Line: one.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:15:They had gone back up into the loft that morning. Karis would not sleep a second night with a floor between herself and three boxes, and nobody had cared to argue with a person who could not so much as shut her hands on the argument. So there was the ladder to be dealt with: twelve rungs, one of them whiter than the rest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:17:He had always taken an inventory at the start of anything. For two years it had been the same one, a count of the fragments he carried and how each of them lay idle; and on the fifty-fourth morning he caught himself at the head of a ladder, taking another.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:27:*Fifty-fourth day. The ladder. Brom, one hand. Karis, elbows. Lira, seated, counting aloud. Mine, by the arms; the calf gives eleven strides on the flat and no rungs at all. Seln, as usual. The nine: idle, and asking. Deployment since the Line: one.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:51:The hold's mule had come the length of her tether. She laid the whole of her long head on his good shoulder, and leaned, and sighed; and Brom stood under the weight of it with something like gratitude.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:193:He had a keg of salt behind him, and a coil of new rope, and the boy, who rode the leaning mule at a walk and looked a year older than he had at a plank. The steading's woman went out through her gate to meet him, which Cael had not seen her do for anybody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:217:"*Cleared*, and a colon after it," she said. "He's never used one before, and everything after the colon is what *cleared* cost. And *five confirmed*: he won't write seven, and he won't write that two went down the river, because nobody counted those with a bow; he writes what somebody stood over." She looked up. "The last noun has changed again. *Nothing lost* was casks, and *no losses* was a cart and a mule. *None lost* is us."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:227:"He's forty marks in his box against your coming," said the carter. "He told me to say that, and to say that he'd not paid a forty since the thaw." He scratched his neck. "I'm to tell you nothing about the top of his sheet, for there's nothing. The man wasn't down when I left."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:229:He led his mule away to the barn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:243:*Deployment since the Line: one.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:245:Down in the yard Lira was going along the fence on her stick, by herself, slowly, in the last of the light. She stopped under the platform, and the bowman looked down, and she looked up, and neither of them lifted a finger.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:267:The grain was moving: three carts had come in by the east gate at first light, with mules bigger and darker than the Thornwater beast. The people with them were strangers to her, and they gave five bandaged persons in a yard no longer a glance than they gave the geese.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:293:She returned to the hide. She had been dictating its features to Cael since breakfast, one at a time, since she could not copy the thing herself and would not have a line of it lost.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:323:And Karis had made the rule herself. *I should like to find out what one learns the other way.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:331:That was what the other way cost, and she made herself price it honestly, for it would have been easy to call it a triumph. One got exactly what one was given: she had three sentences, and no means in the world of cross-examining them, and their only witness was on her way east at a mule's pace.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:3:*Second day past the Line. One fire. Deployment since the Line: none.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:29:He had no Path of his own, and so no tier and no rank, and he said so to himself plainly, because the dark was a good place for plain things. Everybody else he knew had come away from a Kindling at fourteen with one: a single gift, which an Arbiter named and a registry ranked ever after. His own Arbiter had woken too, on a brass plate in a windowless room, and it had taken eleven seconds over him and gone dark, and it had been dark ever since. What answered him now were nine fragments, which was the log's word for them: abilities taken out of other people's Paths, each one inside a fight that somebody else had begun in earnest, and each one written into Hesk's book with a name beside it and a price.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:97:"Mules," he said. "Small feet, and narrow. A good many, going both ways, and none of them lately." He nodded at the gelding's quarters. "And one horse, which is him. There's not a shoe-print on this road that isn't his."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:141:Lira looked, and she had her mouth open to answer when a mule came down through the red trees on the far side, with a rider on it, and walked into the river at the cairn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:143:The rider did not hail them. She put the mule's nose at the upstream cairn on their own bank and came across on the slant, a long quartering line, and the water was never over the animal's knee. Halfway she passed within ten yards of the place where Lira had meant to cross, and Cael watched the surface there as she went by: it was smooth, and darker than the rest, and it turned slowly in its own length, like something asleep.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:151:The mule came up out of the shallows beside their cairn and stood dripping. The woman on it was fifty or so, brown and spare, with a lip that had been split a long time ago and had healed a little crooked. Her hat had the brim cut away on the right side. A hatchet hung in a loop at her saddle-bow, edge outward, where anybody could see it, and behind her lay two flat leather bags with long flaps.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:153:She looked them over. She took in the horse's feet, and the iron on the wheels, and the shape of the canvas, and gave each about as long as a clerk gives a line he has seen before. Then she lifted her chin at the cairn beside her and at the one across the water, once each, and turned the mule, and rode it forty yards down the bank before she let it drink.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:191:She gathered the mule's head and rode up through the red trees by the way they had come down, and she did not look round. Nobody out here seemed to, and he had begun to think that it was a kind of manners.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:197:*Fourth day. The letters to Hesk and to Vell went west by a courier on a mule, a copper each, as far as the turf fire. She showed us the ford by riding it. She did not ask what the letters were. She did not ask what we were either. Entered, because I can't remember the last person who didn't.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:201:*Deployment since the Line: none.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:227:"So has a mule, and a mule goes unshod on this ground. I have been looking at the prints for two days, as the large one has, and I do not think that anybody on this road has ever wished to be heard from a ridge." He considered the gelding without ill will. "I do not say he is wrong. I say he is an announcement."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:269:*Fifth day. Deployment since the Line: none. At dusk, on an empty road, Shadow got as far as my boots, seal and all. Four strides, both feet, before I had it shut. I didn't ask for it and I didn't use it for anything, so it isn't a deployment. It's what the thing costs to carry. Call it rent. Entered as rent, and Seln heard it, which is right. He would.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:275:There were three, going the same way, at a mule's walk. They were narrow things, high in the wheel, hardly more than half the width of the road, and Cael saw why when the first of them went by a place where the bank had slipped: it passed with a foot to spare, and their own cart, coming after, had to be lifted round.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:287:They did not talk. He noticed that first, and it was a good hour before he believed it. An old man led, with two mules, and behind him came a boy of fifteen or so with one, and then the woman; and when the old man wanted the boy he did not call back to him, but lifted a hand, and the boy looked. Once the woman clicked her tongue, twice, and the old man slowed. From the middle of the morning until the sun stood in the west Cael heard no word spoken above the creak of the wheels.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:327:He went back to his mules. He had said nothing further, and even so it came to more than the woman on the mule had given them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:365:He wrote the day's lines in what light there was. *Sixth day. Deployment since the Line: none. Ruling: hold.* Then he put the book away and ate cold flour-cake with the rest of them, in the dark, with the river going by.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:367:Forty yards up the road, somebody else's mule shifted its feet. Nobody anywhere lit a thing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:37:He was looking straight into the animal's mouth, and something in him had already found it. Ember would set a point on anything that he looked at for long, and it had the place, and it offered. *There.* It would have been his deployment and not hers, and from any bank it would have looked exactly like hers.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:41:It was over before he reached her. The hound had done the work itself; it had driven forward, as they drove at everything they could feel or remember, and the knife was only the length of a hand, and a hand had been enough. It lay across her with its head on her shoulder, and she lay under it with her elbows locked and her eyes wide open.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:69:It was all the report he made. Lira had dragged herself up out of the water onto the dry stones without anybody's help. She sat with her right leg stretched in front of her and her boot still on, and her face was the colour of the gravel.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:83:He made himself think it through to the end, since nobody else was placed to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:85:They could go. The near channel was open behind them, twenty yards of green rock under fast water, with a hole beside it. One of them could not stand, and must be carried over that; the man to carry her had one arm; and a third could not close her hands on a sleeve. They would do it in the dark, with their backs to three animals that had just shown how well they could learn, and somewhere on that rock somebody would slip. He knew what Iron Skin did when a load shifted and there was no time to choose, and he had watched a Wind burst go off by itself on a river bar.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:255:He came slowly, flat-footed on the green rock, with a bow held up out of the wet. He had been told not to come down, and had told them so himself. He stopped at Brom's stone and stood looking into the deep water under the lip. Then he came up onto the gravel and went about it with the tip of his bow, touching each one as a woman touches loaves on a board: the first, at the far landing; Lira's, half in the river; the one that lay beside the boulder. He walked the thirty yards down to the tail by himself, and walked back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:275:The cart was on the near beach. The carter had brought it down from the bend when he heard no more, and he stood at the mule's head with his hat off, looking out at the bar.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:277:He put no question to them. He let down the tail-board, and then he walked out along the rock by himself, in the last of the light, as far as a white stone that lay a little out of its row; and he picked it up in both arms and carried it to the lip, and set it down at the end of the line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:299:She unlaced it herself. Lira's face went grey and then greenish, and she held to the edge of a manger with both hands and did not make a sound, while the woman felt along the bones of the foot with her thumbs, and up the ankle on both sides, and took a long time over it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:305:"I've seen it in a mule and I've seen it in a man."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:413:He could have stood behind that white muzzle himself. He owned the means, and at dusk Shadow had been all but lifting his feet for him; nobody on that gravel would have had to go down thirty yards in the open and land locked, and there would be no holes in his leg tonight. He had chosen the loud thing, in front of a witness, and taken the bite, and it was the first time since he left the hill that the seal had sent him a bill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:419:*Deployment since the Line: one.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:421:He had written the other word some fifty times, and he had expected the new one to look like a stain on the page. It did not. All those weeks he had supposed that the *none* was the thing he was guarding; but it had been more like a purse kept shut, so that there should be something in it on the one evening when it was wanted. He had opened it once, for one deployment, and counted out what the thing cost, and shut it again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:15:It was the last morning of the job, and he let himself know it. Three lines remained of a table that had sixty-two. After the ledge there was a shoulder, and a hillside going down into a brown valley, and a man at a ford who would count seventy marks into Karis's purse. He had walked a mountain for seven days with nine things shut in him, writing down what a cord and a clock and a girl's feet could tell him, and not one line of it had wanted anything that he was. He thought that he would be sorry to come down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:27:She was. She went out along the ledge under its roof of rock, close in, and from that distance there was nothing to show that one foot came down later than the other; she looked like a thing that the mountain had been keeping that shelf for. He made himself look at the fin. It stood ahead of them and a little to the right, pale in the half light, with the frozen shale banked along its upper side, and he judged that they were thirty yards short of its nearer end.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:87:He was looking back the way they had come, at a cart standing by itself on a white floor with its shafts down and three boxes under one canvas. The frost about its wheels had begun to crack. Cael could see it from where he stood: the whole apron shivering, as the skin on a pail of milk shivers when the pail is kicked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:179:He got himself onto his right elbow, with a care that he had never before had to take over breathing, and looked out past the low end of the wall to the east.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:215:That made four people, and one of them was himself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:25:She went over them slowly, and Cael knew what she was reading, for he had read it himself in a jug's worth of lamplight three nights ago: four lines in a book, and nothing gone wrong in any of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:35:"You held your tongues on my road," she said. "All six of you, and the mule. I stood at this gate and watched you do it, and I've not seen that done by strangers before or since. He'll have had that in his head when he struck it." She wiped her hands down her apron. "The boy?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:37:"Fed, and asleep when we left. Pike's keeping him till the mule's fit to climb."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:123:She let it pass. The three boxes stood under the slope of the roof where a carter had set them down at noon, each with its paper tag, and she had put her hand on every one before she sat. The loft smelled of barley; below them a mule shifted in its stall, and the ladder had one rung that was whiter than the rest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:293:"You own that way, and so do I. You will not use it. There is a bowman with excellent eyes, and a book in a meadow two days west of here, and a ferry; I taught you that arithmetic myself, more or less, by being the other side of it." He looked at Karis. "And so I come to your second clause."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:317:He wrote the day while Brom trimmed the wick. *Fifty-second day. Thornwater. Seven at the ford, by Seln's count, and one of them lies on the bar. Karis has a hypothesis, in two clauses, and tomorrow at dusk we test both. If I spend anything it will be Wind, once, where it can be seen. Deployment since the Line: none.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:27:"There he had two tales, and I asked for both." Seln looked at the ground while he told them, as he did when he was reading from memory. "The first is his own time. A salt string was going under a ledge herd on the cold road, and a block had come down across the track; and a Force at the tail of the string shifted it with his Path, since that was quicker than unloading. The hill came down, with the herd upon it. They found two mules."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:85:Brom took the middle box by himself. He knelt, and had Lira set it on his back, and passed his rope over it and across his chest. Then he stood up under it and walked onto the fan with his hands clasped before him, as a porter walks under a chest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:101:He had found his weir. Islands of solid rock stood up out of the fan here and there, the bones of the hill showing through its skin, and a rope laid over bare rock would have sawed itself through in an hour. So he had Seln cut a length of red trunk, as thick as a thigh, and peel it. He bedded that across a notch in the first island, and laid the ferryman's rope over it, so that the log should turn and the rope not rub.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:103:One end went down to a bridle on the cart's axle-tree, where a pull from above would lift her nose. At the other end he put himself. He stood in a loop that went round his chest and under both arms, facing downhill, with his hands behind his back; and when Karis, up at the log, raised her arm, he walked down the mountain. Seln and Cael went down with him, hauling hand over hand, and for every yard that three people descended the cart rose one, nose first, half buried, with the plates parting round her and closing behind as water closes behind a punt.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:183:"If the salt crews are right about three days for a mule," said Karis, "then sixty. Perhaps seventy."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:211:Seln himself was forty yards farther on, down on one knee at a place where water came out of the rock, and he was not filling the skin. He was looking at the wet ground beside the lower of two small pools.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:235:*The nine: held, on a hill where three of them would have been of use. Deployment since the Line: one.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:31:"It's the same thing with a wheel on it." Pike laid his one hand on the paper. "Oxhollow's six days north-west of this plank, and Thornwater's two days east of it. A cart from the one that wants grain from the other comes round by my ferry, and it's eight days, and eight home. There's high ground between the two holds. The salt crews say there's a shelf along the top of it, a bench, with a gorge down one side. A mule string went over it once, before I'd this book, and came down in three days." He looked at the horned head, twice. "Somebody's told the ford so. The ford wants to know whether it's true before it sends a wheel."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:55:There were fourteen slips. He gave each the same attention, the bark and the hide and the two shingles, and he sorted them as he read, as he had once sorted a board in Ardenmere by what each card would cost him. There was a fence to be set for a steading on the north water, at five marks, and a wheel to be fetched for a carter on the south road, and hay to be got in at the upper inn for a mark and the loft to sleep in. A string of mules wanted walking westward to the turf fire, and that slip he passed over without stopping.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:57:When he had done, the paper with the horned head was at the top of the heap in his mind, and it had got there so quickly that he distrusted it. He made himself say why, in items, as he would have made a stranger say it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:111:He had told that man at that same plank, ten days ago, that nobody was coming to take his report off him. It had been easy to say, and he had learned since that the saying was the smallest part of it. The promise had to be kept at exactly such moments as this one, when the report was sixty yards off and plainly worth having, and when nobody but himself would ever know that he had turned his back on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:161:The cart went up to the saddle between two people. It had no beast, for there was not a mule in Lowmarch to be hired for a mountain by a crew that might not bring it back. Seln took the near shaft and Cael the off, and they leaned, and the narrow wheels went round. On the flat of the meadow it was nothing. On the hill Cael counted nine strides and felt the calf begin to close, and at the tenth Lira's shoulder came in beside his without a word, and took the shaft, and he fell back to the tail and walked with his hand on a box until the leg forgave him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:179:"I've lifted nothing. I'm leaning." His whole plain face had lightened, for the first time since a river. "I leaned on a mule for five days at Thornwater and nobody stopped me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:203:*The nine: held. No Path has been lifted by any of us since the ford. Deployment since the Line: one.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:13:She came and took it herself, and Cael knew her broad dusty back, and the long knife on her thigh. As she turned away she saw four people in linen under the eave, and she stopped, and looked at Cael for a breath with her head a little on one side, as she had looked at him across a wash. Then she tucked her letter into her bodice and went.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:17:The sack was nearly empty when Pike brought out a folded sheet, sealed with plain wax that had no mark in it; and he read the outside to himself and frowned, and read it again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:77:*Deployment since the Line: one.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:85:He read it twice to himself, and then he turned his shoulder and gave it to the grass.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:133:"He came down the cold road on the fifty-seventh, with his three and his mules, and he walked to the plank before he'd had so much as a wash, and stood in front of the place where that slip had been." He turned the cup. "Then he asked me for it. I'd kept it by, struck, as I'd struck it, and I gave it him, and he read it twice; I counted, since it's what I'd have done. Then he had me open the book at your leaf, and he read your line."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:157:"That's been on my oak since the thaw," he said. "It's the carters' well, at the fork, where the south road splits. It went sour. A lad was sent down after a bucket, and they wound him up asleep; he woke, and he's not been right about a ladder since." He drew five strokes on the bark. "Nobody's been down it; they water at a stream a mile on, and curse me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:255:The frost had heaved the top course of the lining at the thaw, and six stones had gone in, and he felt for them with his feet. He stooped for each one, to the shoulder in that water, and got it into the loop, and jerked the rope twice, and it went up past him, dripping, toward the coin of light. He brought up the bucket, and a length of chain; and he brought up something else, which had gone in after water a long while ago and was past being anything, and he sent that up with his face turned to the wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:331:A carter came up the south fork with two mules while the last bucket stood on the curb. He stopped them at the edge of the halting-ground and stood watching Brom, plainly waiting to learn whether a large young man would drop where he stood. When a full minute had gone by he came and dipped his own cup, and then he scratched a wheel on the slip with the point of his knife.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:333:"The lad that went down for the bucket was mine," he said. "He's not been easy on a ladder since." He looked at the faggot, and at the candle on its knotted string, and let them both go unexplained; and then he watered his mules at the trough.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:451:*Deployment since the Line: one.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:13:It was. Along the upper shelf, east of the last animal, the stone was scoured pale in a band as wide as the shelf itself, and ahead of the first it was dark and furred. They were mowing it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:125:He had the west end, and he had it faithfully. His eyes were on the last animal of the line, a big one with a broken plate on its near shoulder, which stood a little beyond a rib of the hill, so that from where he sat he could see it and it could not have seen its own fellows at the eastern end. He had been counting the slabs along its spine to keep himself awake, and he had got to seven.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:135:A piece of the upper shelf had gone. He could see the scar, pale and sharp-edged, where a slab as big as a barn door had been lying along the lip, and the slab itself was sliding still, far down the face, breaking as it went. The easternmost animal stood above the place with its hindquarters low and its legs spread wide, for it had been standing on the thing when it went.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:195:"Yes." Karis's eyes rested on her, and then on Brom. "I have watched Iron Skin start at your wrists when a rope snatched, and go down again, and you told me in a loft that something leaves you when it comes. A burst went off by itself on a gravel bar, in our first week, because a foot slipped."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:215:Karis lifted her eyes to the slope. Thirty animals, each the weight of a loaded cart, stood on loose plates above a shelf that had dropped a piece of itself an hour ago under one of them; and below them there was a roof-pitch of the same stone, and at the bottom of that a bench, and five people.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:231:"The other one I gave you because I couldn't hold a pencil. This I can write myself, and I have. But it's derived, and a derived thing in one hand is an opinion." She was looking at the leaf, and not at Hesk's book, which she had never read. "You had the far end of the line. If you'll put down what you saw there, in your own words, then it's two people's."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:5:It took Brom three strides' worth of standing still. He set his boot in the stirrup and bent, with the box riding up his shoulders, and drew. When the string dropped home he made a small sound through his nose. It had opened his fingers. Cael could not see how deeply, in that light; he saw only that Brom held one hand away from himself for a breath, as a man holds it after picking up something hot. Then he laid a bolt in the groove out of the sleeve on the stock. That left four in the sleeve, and one in a pale thing on the floor behind them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:81:He could see the stone. It was a paler thing at the lane's end, the size of a loaf. The mule stood beyond it with her long ears forward, hobbled, and in no fear at all; nothing in that grass wanted anything that she had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:227:He had counted his strides from the place where he dropped into the rut, and he was at a hundred and thirty when he heard it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:287:There was grass under him that was only grass, and a mule's shape, and a big man down on one knee a yard in front of him with a bow-stock at his cheek. Brom's eye was not on him. It was on something past his hip.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:301:It went up out of the grass along sixty yards, thirty to the north of the stone and thirty to the south, a wall of yellow-white fire as high as a man's knee and as straight as her wrist could draw a curve that size. He felt her discharge go past him like the door of a furnace. It was the first thing that anybody but himself had declared on that hillside since noon, and after what he had carried down the slope it seemed to him a clean and ordinary thing, like bread.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:5:Cael could tell that from the wood. It was a slab of ash on two trestles, long enough for a man to lie on, and it had been scrubbed so often that the grain stood up pale and clean, and round its feet the earth was swept. A bench ran along the wall beside it. Eight people were sitting on the bench when the five came up from the ford with seventy marks, and it was plain that some of them had been there since the mule was first seen on the valley road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:29:After the third she did not get up at once. She let herself down onto the end of the bench, a little at a time, with one hand on the wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:35:"A tooth, which the smith can draw. A cough that's had him every cold term for twenty years. The child's fat, and it's cross, and there's nothing the matter with it." The healer's voice had gone hoarser. "I've looked at every one. That's what the bench is for. It isn't the ones I mend who need me to come on the day. It's the ones I have to tell from the others."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:115:She did the rest of them in the time it took to load a mule. She looked at Lira's foot, and at how the long strip crossed in front, and said that it would do. She had Brom's wrist out of its cuff and into it again. She told Karis to eat, and to ask nothing of herself for three days more, not so much as a spark to see by. To Seln she gave a nod, as one gives it to a man of one's own trade, and Seln returned it exactly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:125:He did not say it, and it was not like the things he had left unsaid at a fire; he was not sparing himself. He had no right to the sentence, and that was a different sort of silence.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:133:She nodded, once, as to a thing entered. Then she clicked her tongue to the mule, and went out under the lintel at a carter's pace, on the low side.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:153:She tried her hands on the mule, at the first turn of the hill, as a person tries a lamp that has gone out once. She laid her palm on the warm brown neck, under the mane, and counted. It was all there. A beast's bed is broad and shallow and runs slow, and she had felt this one's every day for thirty rounds; it told her of a sound heart, and a near hind hoof that would want the smith inside the month. Her hands were as they had always been.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:157:The road went up the hill in a long slant, and she went up it below the mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:161:She knew the first of them, as anybody on those roads did. It was Teague, off the north water, with a string of two mules behind him and his three; and she gave them what she gave everything on legs, which was a look from a stride and a half as they drew level. A spear, carried upright and grounded at each step, by somebody who never hurried. A boy who was all wrists. And a bow, on the back of one who walked a hair short on the left, at the hip.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:163:Her fingers woke for that. She had not touched it, and she did not need to. It was old, ten years or more; the joint had been out and gone back in by itself, a little wrong, and grown a seat there. She knew what it would cost to unmake that. She knew, too, that the captain had stopped his walk by half a pace to let her see it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:173:He nodded. He had asked, and been told the price, and she saw him put it by. She gave him a nod of her own, as one gives it to a man who has not wasted one's time, and went on up the hill below the mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:205:She left that hold at first light on the ninety-first, with two days of road in front of her and the mule going well. And somewhere on the first of those days, between a ford and a stand of the red trees, she found that she had been doing a sum for the better part of a morning without having set herself to it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:227:The fifth was not. On the hundredth she would mend nobody. She would spend a mending's worth of herself on a boy with nothing wrong with him, to learn a thing that would help no one on her route; and no hold owed her that day, and she owed it to none of them. If it was to be had, it must be added.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:231:Then she clicked her tongue to the mule, and went on at the same pace as before, on the low side, toward a gate that was still a day's walk off.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:235:It was still the eighty-fourth day, and the light was going off the hill above Oxhollow, when Teague counted his three. He had done it before the healer's mule was out of hearing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:237:They were behind him on the hill. The spear had not changed pace for the stop. The boy had both mules by one rein and was looking back up the road after the woman, with his mouth a little open, as he looked after everything. The bow had stood square on both legs for as long as the healer's eyes were on that hip, and now the weight had gone over to the right again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:249:But it was a day he had spent and a thing he had hoped for, and he could not set it down against five names. Pike had struck a ford off his hold and given it to them; that had cost nothing, because he had chosen the pass himself. The healer's lateness was not theirs on any ground he had read.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:257:The mild one was by the gate-post. Teague found him at once, this time, and was pleased with himself for a breath, until he saw that the man had been looking at him since the shoulder of the hill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:265:The old keeper was on his bench by the stone house, with a tally-stick, and he gave Teague the rest in the time it took two mules to pass.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:285:The fourth was the only one that he might have kept. It gave something away. It told five people at the bottom of a sheet that the name at the top expected to stand on a road beside them before the cold term was out; and it set a term on that, which was his to set, since he had three behind him who would be standing there too.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:297:Cael heard the mules on the gravel and shut the book on his pencil.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:321:They crossed without a sound. Cael watched it as he would have watched a thing well made. The one with the spear went second and grounded it at every step. The one with the bow walked a little short on one side and kept the pace regardless. A boy of sixteen or so brought the two mules through on a slack rein, and not one of the four looked down at the water. They had kept the hold on roads since before he knew there was one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:325:*Eighty-fourth day. Oxhollow, at the ford. The map is delivered: sixty-three lines, and the last one says no. Seventy marks, counted. Earned, a hundred and thirty-two. In hand, a hundred and sixteen. Six owed to the smith for a cart that is in a gorge.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:329:*The nine: held, on a mountain and off it. Deployment since the Line: one.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:335:On the far bank four people and two mules were going up out of the river into the dusk, quiet, a crew's length apart. He turned his head the other way. The north road climbed the brown hill behind the hold in one long slant, and a mile up it, small and steady, there was a mule, and on the low side of the mule a woman, walking at a pace that she could keep until dark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:3:Six of them went over the stone a little after noon, and the mule stayed where she was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:9:Seln had the locked case as well, shut, on its strap across his chest. He had looked once at the mule's pack, and once at the stone, and had kept it on him, as he had kept it on every road. Everything else that they owned was either in a loft two days west or under the feet of a mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:13:So they went in as they had gone round. Brom held one end at the stone, and Karis walked the other out until it came tight, and stopped; then he came up to her, and she went on. The other four walked behind, in file, in the track that the cord left. The grass stood to Cael's hip. It closed over each of them as they passed and did not quite rise again, and when he looked back from the third length there was a lane trodden through it, as straight as a ruled margin, with a grey stone and a small brown mule at the far end.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:47:"Whose hand's that?" said Lira. "You've got every sort of alphabet there is, in those boxes. You copied half of them out yourself."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:67:Cael came and looked. All along the brink, as far as he could follow it to either hand, the floor turned up and rolled over on itself, smooth, with no corner anywhere, so that its crest stood a finger above the grey within and a hand above the turf outside. He had stepped over that a quarter of an hour ago and never marked it. A person running would have had to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:159:She had said it lightly, with her hands on the tenth sheet. Then she seemed to hear it herself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:5:She did not go alone, since Seln had made a rule and she had lost no time in seeing the sense of it. Lira had the watch with her. Lira sat down in the grass ten yards short of the place, with her back turned and her knife across her knees, to look at everything that was not a ring of stones; and Karis went on the last ten yards by herself, with a notebook, and a pencil, and no lamp.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:23:She counted them, and at each crossing she made herself attend to one thing only, as she would have made a student attend. The third time she watched for the instant, and it had no width that she could find. The fifth time she went over slowly, a hand's breadth at a time, to learn whether it dimmed; it did not, and then it was gone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:37:It was the only measurement that she could take tonight, and she took it with the stubbornness of a person who has been given one tool. From the turn of the ruts to the stone it was eighty of Cael's strides, by his count, and his were a yard. She paced it herself, there and back, and her own count was a good deal larger, as it always was. She wrote both. She paced it a second time and was one stride out, and wrote that too, and the mean of the pair.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:41:A hand held out across the stone did nothing at all; the sigil hung where it was. She had watched the healer stand so at dusk, for a long while, and had not asked her what she felt. An arm did nothing. But when she stood astride the stone itself, with one boot on either side, she had the sigil or had it not according to how she leaned, and Karis would not write down a lean. She put: *Narrower than one stride of mine. No finer figure tonight. Wants daylight, and a second person to watch my feet.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:45:It was very tidy, and she made herself look at that, because it was what she was afraid of.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:51:Her pencil had come down on the page. She looked at the point of it, resting on white paper in the starlight, and she knew exactly what kind of sentence it was about to begin. It would have had *as though* in it. It would have said what the thing was like; and a likeness, set down in a notebook at midnight, is a hypothesis that has not yet had the honesty to call itself one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:73:"Because yours sits nearest the skin of any of us. You had your hand up before the rest of us had finished the stride." Karis shook out the long cord, with its scraps of red wool. "I walk on the line itself, with this end. Brom has the other, at the last mark. When the cord comes tight between us I stop, and find the line under me, and that is a station. Brom cuts a turf there with the bill and turns it over, so that we leave a black mark in the grass at every cord. Then he comes up to me, and we do it again."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:81:"You keep the stone. Somebody must be standing at the place where we began, so that we know it when we come round to it from the other side. And I should like one of us to be outside the whole time, with the mule, where an Arbiter is an Arbiter."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:157:They went round the north side with the ruts beside them. He could see where the carts had gone, forty yards off, keeping the same distance from the line the whole way, as exactly as if the carters had had a cord of their own; and he supposed that in a sense they had. A man who walked in front of a mule here for twenty-two years, with a Path in him, would have learned to the yard where he ceased to be comfortable.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:181:They took the south side at a better pace, since by now each of them had the work by heart. Lira drifted out twice more and said so. Brom's turfs lay behind them in a long curve, black on the pale grass; and when Cael looked back along them from the fiftieth, he could see the curve closing on itself like a hoop.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:183:From the south he had the whole of it in one view, for the first and only time. The grey stone was away on his left hand, with a small brown mule beside it. Beyond it the ruts swung out round the north in their wide curve. The floor lay in the middle of everything, with its eleven broken stones, and on the far side, where no turf had been cut, there was the low place in the grass where Seln had knelt.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:191:They had come up from the south, with the grey stone in plain sight ahead and a square brown figure standing beside it with a mule. Karis reached the end of the sixty-second cord, and stopped, and found the line, and Brom cut his turf; and the stone was still some way off.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:193:She coiled the cord over her shoulder, and walked the rest on the line, in her own short steps, counting under her breath, until she set her last foot down against the stone itself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:221:"Five years in the weather. A cold term either side."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:3:The mule went over the top of the brown hill, and after that there was nothing on the north road for him to watch.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:7:It was the start of something, and so he took the inventory, as he took it at the start of anything. Three ribs were broken on the left side, low; they had been taped on the eighty-first day, and the tape was to come off on the ninety-ninth and not before. The calf was bound in a healer's cloth and was closing by itself, as his hurts did. Lira had a shin that was four days old, and an ankle that no healer on this road was going to mend, and one boot. Brom's left wrist wore a linen cuff, and he was to hang nothing from it until the middle of the eighty-seventh day. Karis had been told to ask nothing of herself for three days more, and she was asleep against a box.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:13:It was the keeper. He let himself down onto a flat stone by stages, with the notched hazel across his knees, and he looked at the river for some time before he spoke, like a man who has all his life counted what crossed it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:23:"He's a lad and two mules. It's sixteen days of road, and I'd as soon he had somebody walking in front of him for the first six." The keeper tipped his stick at the gate-house wall. "Your boxes'd ride. There's room on the bed going out; it's coming home that she's full."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:27:"There's none. I've no paper for it and I'm not sending to a plank for some." He scratched his jaw with the stick's end. "You walked my hill for seventy and brought me a no. I'd have paid the seventy three times over in carts, by next thaw, if you'd brought me a yes to please me. I can find a bed for three boxes."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:51:The carter was a long young man with a narrow sleepy face and the largest feet that Cael had ever seen on anybody. He was the keeper's sister's son. He had two grey mules, and a cart a foot wider in the bed than the one that lay in the gorge. In the six days that followed he said perhaps forty words, and most of them were to the mules. The red oxen drew his wheels over for nothing, since the wheels were the hold's own. He walked through behind them with his breeches rolled to the thigh and his boots hung round his neck by their laces.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:57:They were a day old. The dew had softened their edges, but the gravel gave way there to a strip of grey silt, and the silt had kept everything. Four people had come up out of the river in single file, each a crew's length behind the one in front, and two mules after them on one rein. He crouched, as far as five bands of linen would let him, and read it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:61:"That's the boy," said Lira, over his shoulder. She was standing on one boot and one bare foot, with her arms wrapped round herself. "He's carrying more than the others. Look how his heels go in."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:71:The carter had halted his mules on the bank and was waiting, with his sleepy face turned toward them. He had seen Lira's foot. He looked at it for some time, and then he went round to the cart's tail and took down a pair of boots.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:89:The carter thought about it with real care, and the mules stood, and the river went past.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:119:He tried it now beside the near mule, since there was nothing else to do and six days in which to do it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:121:The mule did not hurry and did not stop. Its near fore came down, and he put his left foot down with it; four of those, and a breath, to the count of three; four more. Whenever he ran ahead of the beat the tape caught him, and whenever he fell behind it the cart's wheel came up at his heel. Between those two there was a place the width of a plank where neither of them touched him, and by the afternoon of the eighty-sixth day he could stay on the plank for a mile together.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:123:It was a small thing to be proud of. He entered it all the same. In two years of other people's fights and eighty-six days of road, nobody had ever taught him anything that he had so little wished to learn, and it had been taught him by a strip of pitch and a mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:127:He knew, since he could hear her. She had been told a month, and it was plain that she had taken the month as she had taken the mule, as a thing agreed to and not to be discussed. She set the great boot down on its whole sole at every step and looked at the country. There was a good deal of it to see. The road went south down a broad valley of alder and rough pasture, with the grey mountain shrinking behind their left shoulders, and twice a day it forded some small brown water where the mules drank below the crossing. She named nothing and timed nothing. Once, in the middle of the second afternoon, she said across the cart that she had never before walked anywhere slowly enough to see what was growing on a wall, and that it was mostly moss.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:129:Karis slept. She woke to eat, and to ask which day it was and write the number down, and then she folded herself up again between two boxes with Seln's coat over her and was gone. Cael watched her do it and was easier each time. On the mountain she had looked at her own hands before she looked at anything else, and by the third morning on this road she had stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:135:He walked behind the cart with his hands hanging, as he had walked behind another cart up a saddle, and every little while he turned his left wrist over and looked at the cuff. He ate his bread with the right hand. When the carter's off mule cast a shoe-nail in the middle of the morning and stood on three legs, it was Seln who held the hoof, and Brom stood by with both arms folded so tightly that the knuckles showed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:163:Cael had half risen to say something, and he sat down again. He had been on the point of telling Brom that the week was out, and that he was free of it. He saw now that it would have been like telling a man the price of something he had made himself. Nobody had kept that week but Brom. He could finish it in whatever way he pleased.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:167:The moss came on the fourth day, and the carter stopped his mules at the edge of it without being asked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:201:Then they emptied the cart. The mules were led over one at a time, picking their way between the chalk marks with their ears laid back. After them the cart itself went across with her bed bare, the carter at her head, and Seln and Cael each at a wheel to turn it by the spokes whenever the rim came up to a cross. He could do that much. It was done at the height of his hip and it asked nothing of his ribs that the tape would not allow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:249:"Five halting-grounds, and not a new ash on one of them. The chock-stones are where they found them. They eat cold, and they sleep in a row with the mules at the windward end, and in the morning there's nothing to show for it but where the grass lay down." He turned a page back to look at what he had put. "We've had a fire every night since the ford, on the strength of a finding. He's had none for longer than I've known what a hold was, on the strength of never having needed one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:261:*Mine: I breathe to the tape and walk to a mule. Brom: the week kept, to the hour, and three boxes carried over a moss. Lira walks. Karis is awake.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:265:*The nine: held. Deployment since the Line: one.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:57:Oryn had loaded the mule in the hollow. Two of the boxes hung from the pack-saddle, one on either flank, and her own panniers were lashed on top with the crossbow in its cover. She took the animal by the cheek-strap and walked it to the edge of the break, and there she changed her hand and put herself on the outside.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:61:"If she slips with me on the inside, she goes that way with nothing to stop her. This way she's got something to lean on." Oryn clicked her tongue. "She's a mule, and she'd sooner lean than fall. Most things would."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:63:They went out onto it together, the brown beast with her near flank brushing the wall and the square woman at her shoulder on the open side, a boot's length from the rim. The mule set each small hoof down and tried it. The woman's eyes were on the scratches; and where a marked stone lay under the animal's line she moved its head a little with her hand, and the foot came down there. At the far end Seln received the cheek-strap, and the boxes came off, and she brought the mule back as steadily as she had taken it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:87:Then there was nobody left on the near side but himself. He had known that it would be so, and had not let himself think of it. He set his left hand on the wall and his right foot on the first scratch.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:99:He stood still, with the gorge breathing cold up his back, and he knew what he was choosing between. On the one hand was what he could see for himself. On the other was a line the length of a finger, cut with the point of a knife by a mild man who had come this way in the rain and stood on forty-four stones, one at a time, to learn which would have him. Cael had not asked for that report. He had been given it, since it bore.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:117:The track had been cut for mules. It left the shoulder by a notch and dropped in long slants across a hillside of brown grass and broken rock, turning back upon itself at every hundred yards, and the valley came up to meet them so slowly that the pale thread of the road seemed no nearer at dusk than it had at noon. Going down was worse for him than the level had been. Each step jarred, and the tape told him so, faithfully, some four thousand times; and he catalogued her, since it took his mind from his side.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:119:She never hurried and she never stopped, and that was the first item. She had the carter's pace so deep in her legs that he thought she could not have walked at any other. On the steepest of the slants she shortened her stride and kept the beat, and where the track ran level she lengthened it again, and the time between one footfall and the next did not change by a hair. He tried to walk at it himself and could not, for he went in fits, quick and then slow, as he had always gone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:121:The second item was the side she kept. On every slant she was below the mule. When the track turned, and the fall of the hill went over to the other hand, she passed behind the animal's tail without breaking step and came up on the low side again; and she did it at every turn of that hillside without once seeming to consider which side was the low one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:133:Lira rode. She had been put up on the pack-saddle at the shoulder, on top of the panniers, between two boxes, and for the first hour she sat as stiff as a person on a throne that she expects to be taken away. She had not been carried anywhere since she could walk. She looked straight in front of her, over the long brown ears, and when the mule stopped to consider a turn she looked at the sky.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:139:"The mule."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:141:"I've not noticed a mule."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:165:Lira did not answer for the length of one turn of the track. Then she asked, from the back of a mule, over the heads of four people, and Cael could hear what it cost her to do it so.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:171:"So I would have, that week," she said. "It's knit now. It's knit wrong, short, and across itself; but it's knit, and it's strong."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:183:Lira looked straight in front of her, over the long brown ears, and Cael watched her take it. He had seen her take a result before, on a painted floor, with a sheet of figures to tell her how far she had fallen short. There was no sheet here. There was a square woman with tired eyes, walking below a mule, who had spent what she had on the leg above that ankle, and had said to her face what the rest was worth and to whom it was owed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:187:"Then show me the tape," said Lira, a quarter of a mile farther on. "Properly. What's on it I did myself."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:193:"Always that way, up the outside. That's the side it went over on, so that's the side that's slack, and you're lending it a cord." She laid a second beside the first, and then a long one that went over the top of the foot and under the arch and crossed itself in front, like a figure of eight. "That one stops it rolling." Her thumb went on up the shin, of its own accord, and pressed along the new bone. "And this is ahead of where I'd have looked for it. You've good legs. Somebody's taken trouble with them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:235:"They feed me, and they feed her." She nodded at the mule. "One of them shoes her. One gave me this coat, the year I came, off a peg that nobody was using any more. I get a bed for two nights and a table to work at, and nobody's put a coin in my hand since I crossed the Line."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:245:Cael turned that over. He had counted a great many things in his life, strides and fighters and watchers at a gate, and he had always taken the count for the thing itself. It had never once occurred to him that a number might only be the name of the people one had lost sight of.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:301:The hold stood on the far bank, in a hollow of the hill, behind a palisade of split logs with a gate in it, and between the gate and the water there was a low stone house with a turf roof. The ford itself was a hundred yards of pale gravel under a foot of quick water, wider than any he had crossed past the Line. On the near side a yoke of red oxen stood in the shallows with a chain between them, chewing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:305:She had not slackened her pace for the river. She walked the mule into it on the low side, with Lira up, and the water went round her knees.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:307:A man had come out of the stone house. He was old, and bent, and brown as a nut, and he had a hazel stick in his hand with notches cut all down one edge. He watched them cross as he would have watched a cart cross, counting. He nodded to the healer as to somebody who was owed that and more; and then his eyes went along five people in torn clothes, and a mule, and three boxes, and came back to Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:315:The old man took that in without any change of face. Then he jerked his head at the door of the stone house, and they followed him in, all but the healer, who had gone on up the bank toward the palisade with her hand on the mule's shoulder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:331:"Two feet, clean through," said Karis. "He took our cart over it on his back." She touched Brom's sleeve. "No carter crosses that by himself."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:337:"It isn't there any longer," said Cael. "It went on the morning we came to it. There's a lip of rock thirty-one paces long that a mule can be led over, by somebody who knows how, and there's no road." He put his own finger on the margin, where Karis had written small. "And that's the herd. They're shale-backs, on the face over the bay. If anybody uses a Path beneath them, they run, and the hill comes with them, and that's how your cart went into the gorge. She's written that it's derived."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:357:"Earned, a hundred and thirty-two," she said. "In hand, a hundred and sixteen, and six owed to a smith."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:13:"Lira's had that since dusk. The rest of you have it now. It's fourteen days late, and I'm sorry for every one of them." He looked at the square woman by the ladder. "And there's one thing more that I have, from the night of the birthday, which none of you is getting. I read myself, as she set it. What I found is hers before it's anybody's. I'm telling you that it exists, so that nobody learns later that I sat on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:21:"Somebody had to give one. I was the only person there who knew that it was a different ruling for me." He made himself go on. "I spent a quarter of an hour that I'd said I wouldn't, and I let six people stand at a den believing that all six were alike. If I had told you at the stone, at noon, you'd have planned for it. We'd have been in that lane by two hands, and Lira would have two arms."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:55:"I've tried that line by myself, more than once. Every time, I was a woman in a coat." Her hands came down. "I'm not going to guess past it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:91:It was not said as a proposal. Cael heard under it a cup of barley on a trestle and a number with her thumb over it, and a small bald man telling her that she wanted to see them both at the thaw.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:119:It lay there for as long as it takes to count two. It was the first time that Cael had ever seen her touch that man, who had not been hurt since he crossed the Line and had given her no occasion; and Seln stood quite still under it. Then she took the hand away, without a word, and went down the rungs into the dark. Cael could not have said whether a tired woman had steadied herself at a ladder's head, or whether it had been something else. He did not guess. He had heard her say, not half an hour before, what she did with a thing that she could not read.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:123:Brom had not drawn it up again. He sat at the head of it with his legs hanging into the barn, as he had sat on the first night of the listening, and when Cael came to the edge he went on looking down into the dark where the mule was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:147:He had not slept. He had come down the ladder with Hesk's book inside his shirt, and she was there already, a square shape against the white of the yard, filling a flask from the dipper. The mule stood at the trough with a blanket on her. There was a pannier on the coping with its strap buckled, and a coil of rope beside it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:169:"You said that you'd say when, and you go in an hour. I read myself on the night of the hundred and second, as you set it, alone and on my back. I found—"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:177:"I do. I never said that I wanted the first thing you saw, the first time you looked." She put the flask into the pannier. "How many times have you read yourself?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:211:*Hers. Three readings of myself, on three different days. Then all of it to her, at once. Nobody on the road, to find out whether I can.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:215:She slung the pannier on her back and went to the mule, and laid her hand once on the brown neck. Then she walked away across the yard toward the northern fence, on the low side of nothing, and she went through the wicket without a glance behind her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:217:He stayed at the well until the grey had come into the east. Then he turned back sixty leaves, to a line that he had set down under an inn's eave on the forty-ninth night, when he had counted himself free. He read that through, and came forward again to the clean leaf, and wrote the one that had to stand across from it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:241:The track went up through the hold's grazing, past a stone hut, toward a low place in the hill. The goats lifted their heads to see her go by with nothing on a rope. She had a pannier on her own back, with the strap across her chest, and it rode where no pack had ridden since her first year: tape, flask, shears, linen, a cup, a needle, and a loaf. She had the spare rope over her shoulder. It was thirty feet of good three-strand, and she had laid it up herself; and she had carried it for eleven rounds without once needing it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:257:She went over the top without turning to see Thornwater below her. There was a mule in that yard that would be standing under three boxes by now. She had read the beast before she left her, as she read anything of hers: a sound heart, and a broad slow bed, and no notion whatever that she had been given away.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:267:It was the mule again. She had lain against that warm brown flank on a good many frozen nights and had never once thought of it as a thing that she owned. She lit no fire. It was a road; and besides, she had her back against a chock-stone and a great deal to go over, and she went over it better in the dark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:275:She put no question to herself about what he was. She had heard a small pale person say, by a lantern, that there was a thing she would not write down; she had seen a calfskin with rings on it, and heard a quiet man say *yet*. Those were other people's trades. She had gone into that loft with one thing that she knew, and she had said it, and come down a ladder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:293:He would read himself twice more. He was frightened of what he had found, and he would do it regardless, since he had been told to. Then he would sit on some bench of hers and wait his turn, and say it; and she would be the first living person to hear what the thing that she read with had made of the one body that it could not read.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:303:It was a slow heavy knocking, a long way down the valley, with water falling under it. She knew that sound. There would be a wheel at the end of it, and a fence, and a keeper who counted people off a ridge. Beyond the fence there would be a bench, with whoever had sat down on it sixteen mornings ago when her mule should have come in sight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:5:She said it from the tail of the carter's cart, where she sat with the ash stick across her knees and her bound foot propped on the left-hand box. The cart was empty but for folded sacks and three boxes under their canvas, and it stood in the yard at Thornwater with the mule in the shafts and noon coming on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:17:Karis had allowed the foot on her box, and nobody remarked on that either. Cael stood with one hand on the cart's rail. He had discovered, in the last half hour, that a mule's walk was the first pace in five days that suited his leg; he could go eleven strides beside the wheel and ride for a hundred, and the carter had only looked at him once and moved a sack.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:53:They took the stretch with the sun straight over, and it was only a mile of grass with frogs in it. He rode the last half on the tail-board, beside Lira, with his back to the mule, and watched a white stone get smaller behind them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:57:He saw before the mule was in the water that the hold's men had been down. There was nothing at all on the gravel; and along the lip of the rock the white stones ran out from the near bank in their row, a yard apart, and did not stop until they reached the bar.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:77:It had been a slow day. The carter would not take his mule faster than the worst leg in the party, and that was sometimes Cael's and sometimes the cart's own, for a hind wheel had begun to talk. In the middle of the afternoon the tail had slid in a wet rut, toward Brom, and he had caught the rail before it could go over.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:101:The carter had heard all of it. He stood by his mule with a rag of mutton fat in his hand, and his jaw was working; and when Seln knelt in the lee of the stump with his flint, he took the beast by her cheek-strap and led her forty yards up the road. He stood there beside her, upwind, with his back to them. He had kept that road cold for twenty-two years.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:129:"I'll not do it by myself," said the carter. "I'm telling you that now. I'll do it with five that's been down to that ford, and I'll not do it else." He turned his hands over to warm the backs. "But I'll do it tonight."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:147:He sighed, and brought it out into the light, and turned it over himself. The linen was clean from the elbow to within a hand of the wrist, and there it was stained through, in a patch the size of a coin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:155:Seln unwound it for him, and looked, and wound it again more tightly, and kept what he had seen to himself. Brom sat through it with the patient face of a mule being shod.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:173:The carter had got up quietly and gone to see to his mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:207:*Deployment since the Line: one.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:237:Cael had never seen him do it. On their first day at Lowmarch he had simply been discovered at the plank's far end, looking at the last slip as though he had been there for some time. Now he started at the left-hand post, in the open, with his hands behind him, and went along the oak a slip at a time, as Cael himself went along it, giving each of the fourteen the same attention; and at the tenth he stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:263:He knew what the voice would say, for he could hear it. *What did you see?* He had been on the other side of it himself for two years, with that same mild face writing down his answers.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:293:Brom had gone to the plank's far end. He stood there by himself, with the sewn arm in its sling, and Cael, who knew him, could see that he had been getting ready for this since the saddle and perhaps for a good deal longer. He waited until Pike had come along the oak to the ink-pot. Then he said it, low, and he did not make a question of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:21:Oryn was waiting at the east gate with the mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:23:She had the two panniers on, and the hold's woman had roped a roll of six grey blankets across the pack, behind the long shape in its oiled cover, with instructions that they were to come back. The healer stood on the mule's far side from the hill. She had slept, by the look of her, and she gave the five of them one glance from her stride and a half, each in turn, and seemed to find nothing in it that wanted her hands.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:29:Lira had a boot in each hand. One was the carter's left, which she had found standing outside the barn door at first light. She hung it on the mule's pack by its laces, with great care, against the other one going.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:39:"That's the Fallow," said Oryn, and clicked her tongue to the mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:65:"No. I don't suppose she does." She took the leaf from him and read it through again herself, with her lips moving, and Cael watched her come to the end of it and go back two sentences. "She's got a book like the old man's, hasn't she? She writes down what happened. And she's put you in it as a thing that she's never had to write before."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:119:He watched the healer walk, since it had become a habit. She kept the carter's pace. She was below the mule on every slope, though the slopes were so gentle here that he could not always have said which side was the low one, and she changed over without seeming to think. He set himself to her beat, and breathed to the tape, and for long stretches of that afternoon the tape had nothing to say to him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:153:"The day after tomorrow," she said, half to herself, "there will be six of us who saw it. I mean to write down who."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:169:"That's it," said Oryn. She had halted the mule. "I've never been nearer than these ruts."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:181:They left the mule at the turn, with her rein under a rock. Brom knelt out from under the box and set it beside her. Then the six of them spread across the old line, in a row, and Cael took the right-hand end. Lira was next to him, and then Brom, and Karis, and the healer; Seln had the far end, on the north.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:191:She had stopped dead, on both feet, as she had taught herself to stop on a gravel bar. Her head was a little on one side.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:211:"Back," said Lira; and Brom said it with her, and Karis, and the healer, and Seln himself, a breath behind the rest. It was five voices on the one word. Not one of them had taken a second step.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:215:Karis was looking down the row, from face to face. Then she went forward again by herself, a foot at a time, with her hand held out in front of her as though she expected to touch something; and at a certain place she stopped, and drew back her toe by the width of a hand, and put it out again. She bent, and felt about in the roots of the grass, and came up with a grey stone the size of a loaf.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:229:Neither one was a good enough reason by itself. He knew that. He kept silent on the pair of them together, and his own shadow lay out in front of him across a grey stone and on into the grass.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:239:*The nine: held. Deployment since the Line: one.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:243:They made their camp at the turn of the ruts, eighty yards back from the stone, and there was no fire. Seln had chosen the place. It lay a little below the crest on the western side, so that a person lying down could not see the ring and could not be seen from it. They ate cold bread from Thornwater's oven, and the hold's blankets went round, and the mule stood with her tail to the wind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:249:The mule had settled the matter of the line for herself an hour before. She had been left with her rein under a rock, and she had drawn it out and grazed her way down the old road; and when Oryn went to fetch her she was standing with her forefeet on one side of the grey stone and her hind feet on the other, pulling at the grass, as easy as a beast in its own paddock.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:15:The sun stood as high as it would go at that time of year, pale and small, and their shadows lay short on the frosted grass. They had gathered on the western side of the grey stone, with the turn of the ruts at their backs and the ring two hundred yards and more in front. Oryn had the mule by the cheek-strap a few yards behind the rest, and her free hand tucked into her armpit for warmth; she had watched the whole of the morning from that stone without a word.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:17:Cael looked at the leaf that he had been given. There were five rows on it, each headed with a letter, and his own was not among them. That was as it should be, since he had been handed the pencil for the very reason that there was nothing of his to enter. He had kept the count at a great many trials of other people, from the east tier of a yard, and had thought himself used to the seat.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:33:"No," said Karis. She tried it a second time, and a third, quickly. "No. It has not set. A lecturer would say that the declaration did not render. And that is not the whole of it. When I am empty and try to set one, I feel myself reach into the purse and close my fingers on nothing. Here I am not even reaching. I am three parts full, and I can't find the hand to reach with."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:93:He stepped across, and for a breath he only stood, with his eyes half shut. Cael knew what he was reaching for, since he carried a piece of the same thing himself, sealed, and had felt it lift in him at every dusk for two years.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:99:"I have been only a man in a coat for a great deal of my working life. Will you all look at the mule for a count of three, and then look back?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:101:It was an odd request, and they did it. The mule was regarding a tussock; Cael counted three, and turned his head, and the place where Seln had stood was empty.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:107:He was crouched low against a tussock of the pale grass, side on to them, with his face turned down into the crook of his arm and his hands drawn up into his sleeves. He had put himself to the south of them, where the low sun came from behind him into their eyes. There was no Path in it, and no trick that a child could not have understood once it was shown; and it had still taken five people, all of them looking for him, the better part of a breath to find a man in a dark coat on an open hillside at noon.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:127:"I know what it'll be. I heard it come back off a mountain." Lira's face was pale. "It went off by itself, that morning. I never told it to. I've got to walk into a place with dogs in it on this foot, and I'd like to know, before I do, whether it can."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:137:He turned his head. She was a yard and a half along the line from the place where she had planted herself, with the left foot forward and the right drawn up beside it, looking down at the pair of them on the frost.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:187:She had given the mule's rein to Seln, and she came up to the stone at her own pace, rubbing her hands together, and looked at Brom.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:215:Brom stood on the western side of the stone, where he was an Iron Skin with his Path in the next room. He stretched out his left arm. Oryn kept her two hands on it, and she stepped over the stone by herself, so that she stood inside and he outside, with the mended wrist held between them across the line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:245:Cael watched it happen. Five people had each lost a thing, a stride from where they stood, and had it handed back, and every one of them had turned inward to look at it. Oryn was hobbling the mule, with her lips moving in a count that she did not seem to know she was keeping. Brom knelt by the middle box. Karis sat with her eyes shut, and Lira walked up and down on the great boot, a little apart, and Seln stood looking east.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:265:He had been certain, in the part of himself that kept accounts, of what would happen: a thing taken out of a Path would stop where Paths stopped. He had supposed it since a plank, as the others supposed it, and for the same reason, which was that no other sum came out even; and he had braced for the read to go from him as a count had gone from a healer's lips.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:39:They spent the rest of that day getting ready for a road. The Oxhollow carter had his off mule at the smith's for a shoe. Brom went over the three box-ropes a hand's breadth at a time, since they were all the rope that the five now owned. Seln was gone from the meadow between the mail and dusk, and came back with pale dust on his boots, and said nothing; and Cael, who had made a promise at that plank, looked at the dust and then at the fire.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:45:It lay as it had lain on four other crossings, dry and red, thirty yards from lip to lip, with a ramp cut down either side and the thorn stump standing on its eastern edge. The carter took his mules through at a walk and halted them on the far flat to blow, and the five sat down among the chock-stones where they had twice made a camp.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:93:Cael had read the last lines but two again, to himself. *I still lose to you every time I run it.* He could see the bout as it had gone, on sixty feet of sprung oak, with a blade's edge declared against him in earnest and nothing held back on either side; and he could see a tall, exact person walking it through afterward in an empty yard, alone, looking for the place where it turned. Something in Cael stirred at the thought, quick and bright, and asked *Edge?* of the nearest arris, which was the lip of the wash. He told it no. It was Ephram's, that question. He carried it for him, and paid the rent on it, and had never yet spent it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:105:He did not join them. It would have been very easy, and he had been joined to things himself on no better evidence, by people with seals. Two points could be ruled together by anybody. He would wait for a third.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:111:They had camped short of the lower crossing, on a halting-ground that they knew, and Seln had a fire going. She took the roll from the middle box herself, and carried it to a patch of bare beaten earth where the last of the light fell, and unrolled it there on the ground with a chock-stone on each of its corners; and beside it she laid the calfskin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:163:It was only a plank on two kegs. A small brown mule stood beside it with her pack still on and her head down, and on the pack, lashed along its top, lay a long shape in an oiled cover. Astride one end of the plank sat a carter whom Cael had not seen before, a heavy man with a grey stubble, who held his right hand out in front of him, palm upward, as a person holds a hand out for coin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:167:Her eyes were shut and her lips were still, and the sweat had come out along her cropped hair in the cold; and Cael understood that she had come through that gate herself not long before them, and had sat down to this without so much as slipping a pannier. She had owed it for fifteen days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:195:"The ring," she said. "I know it. I've been round it thirty times, with the mule, by the ruts." She set the bowl on the sill. "You broke on the seventy-eighth. It's twenty-one days by the rule, and I don't lift a tape on a guess, so that's the ninety-ninth. The long one wants a morning with nothing else in it. That's the hundredth. I'd meant to sit at this gate's table from tonight until then; the grain's moving, and that bench fills as fast as I empty it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:221:It was the place where he had written on his first stay at Thornwater, and on his second. Two of the boxes stood behind him among the barley sacks, and the third, with the corner that was worn white, waited by the ladder's head for the morning. Down in the yard the Oxhollow carter was backing his mules to an empty bed. He would load grain at first light and go home by the ferry, sixteen days in all, and he had said goodbye to nobody; but Cael had seen him stand for some time looking at a boot that hung from his cart's tail, and then untie it, and carry it over, and stand it carefully outside the barn door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:9:They stood on the east, at the mouth of the den, and everything that they wanted was on the west. The floor lay between, twenty-six strides across. From its western lip the lane that the cord had trodden ran down through the grass, as straight as a rule, for two hundred and seven yards; and at the end of the lane there was a grey stone and a mule, and beyond the stone a Path was a Path again and a healer's hands were hands. That made two hundred and thirty-three yards from where his boots were.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:11:He had one other mark on that slope. North of the lane a rut came down from under the floor's north-western lip, knee-deep and a stride across, where the rain off the rim had cut itself a road. He had marked the head of it from the ninth cord, and Seln had crossed it on both his rounds in the grass.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:41:Cael had the ruling, and he made himself look at both ways before he gave it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:43:Behind him the slope fell away to the east, and the line on that side was nearer by twenty-six yards. It was also the hounds' own door. There were three runs across it and not a stride of it that any of the six had walked; the grass stood hip-high the whole way down, the ground was steep, and in a quarter of an hour it would be dark. If they came out alive on that side, they would be half a circle of open country from a mule. The west was a lane trodden flat by their own feet, with a stone at the end of it that five of them could find with their eyes shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:45:"There's a bow on that mule," said Oryn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:105:It got up. It shook itself from the head to the tail, like a dog out of a pond, and looked at Brom with no ill will whatever. Then it walked off along the foot of the lip, the way that they had come, and jumped up onto the white again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:173:She ran along the rim itself. It was a thing no wider than a roof-ridge, round on top, with the white a finger below it on one side and the turf a hand below on the other, and she went along it as he had watched her go along a ledge under a mountain, close and quick, with her arms a little out. The great boot came down every second stride with a slap. There was no burst in it and no Path, since there could be none; it was a girl of thirteen on the lanes at Fenmark, who had only legs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:191:It did not snap. It closed on the arm between the wrist and the elbow, as a door closes on a hand, and its jaws stayed shut. She was on her back, and he saw her do then what he had seen her do at a stone at noon: she gathered the whole of herself, heels and hips and shoulders, and asked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:217:It was the size of a calf. He bore the long head down until the jaw lay on the floor, with Lira's forearm lying on the floor between the teeth, and he put the whole of his weight on it and kept it there. He had the thing inside the read, every pound of it; and each time that it gathered itself to wrench he had the gathering a breath before the wrench came, in the neck and the shoulders, and leaned his own weight to the place where it meant to go.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:299:Her voice was quite level. She was a different person already, and one that he had not met. She was a woman on her knees in the grass with nothing in her hands, and her linen was two hundred yards off on a mule, with everything else that she had left there at noon.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:303:Brom had it out before Cael had so much as thought. It was a strip of plain linen folded in four, which had lived inside his shirt since the middle of the eighty-seventh day; she had tied it round his wrist herself, on a mountain. She tore it across at the fold and put the halves into Cael's hands.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:309:The healer had dragged the tail of her own shirt out from under the brown coat, and she was tearing it upward, with one hand and her teeth, into strips three fingers wide. She did not wind them herself. She had lifted Lira's forearm across her own knee, to keep it higher than the heart, and she would not let go of it for anything.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:327:The lane went down in front of him into the dusk, a thin pale stroke with the grass standing dark on either hand, and he could not see the stone at the end of it, or the mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:23:"It went at one stride," said Karis, "and it came back at one stride, going the other way. I have that from all five of us on the ninety-fifth evening, and from myself nine times that night, and from each of the trials at noon on the day after. And when it came back, it came back as it had been. You have the measure of that, Lira, and I have never bettered it: a clock that stopped and started again without losing the time."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:61:Cael stood where he was. He had been a row on that leaf since noon on the ninety-sixth, ruled in Karis's hand at the foot of five others, with a numeral against it; and he had filled it for her himself, with a pencil going, in two halves that were each of them true.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:79:The healer had her mule loaded by noon.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:109:Oryn stood looking at the mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:121:"I know who I've got on that road a good deal better than you do." She said it without heat. "And I'll not have spent a night of myself on an arm and then walked off from it in the week that it wanted watching. Those are my five days. You didn't ask for them, and you're not to carry them. Go and shoot your bow."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:123:She lifted the second pannier down herself, and the steading's woman took the loaf out of it again, as if she had expected nothing else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:177:She had stood in a yard a quarter of an hour before and watched a healer unload a mule. That made five days; and there was a child at some gate with a chest that wanted listening to, who would wait those five days longer, since a girl had run along a rim on a leg that she had been told not to run on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:209:"Oldest to the right," she said. "This first is a district hall's, and I had it off the step myself; it was dressed in my grandmother's time. The pale one is from a library that I used to be allowed into, and the honey-coloured is Ostrand's wall. And that is the oldest dressed stone between Denvash and the Line that anybody can put a year to. A mason was re-laying a drain at the foot of the old gate, the one that the city counts its years from. I call it the founding stone, and I have never owned anything older."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:255:"It is the same band, cut to the same depth; a fingernail, he said, and I measured it." On the southern strip the lines stood sharp and white. On the northern they were grey and narrow, and in two places the pencil had not found them at all. "That is weather. That is a north wind on the Fallow, taking a little off a stone in every cold term. Now the founding stone." She put its rubbing between the two strips. "It has stood in a north wall for as long as that city has had a gate. Look at how much of the tool is left on it, and then look at how much of that band is left on the north."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:285:Lira came up the ladder at noon and found the bow lying on the planks in its goatskin, and the lean man standing in the worn place himself, with his own left hand in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:21:He knew why. He was the cut; he had told a girl so in a loft, in so many words. The cut is the man placed where the subject will run, and he is never where the blow falls. At a ford he had lain in a pool for an hour and a quarter while four people were bled on a bar of gravel thirty yards from his face. And tonight, while a hound took a girl's arm on a white floor, he had been two hundred yards away with his fingers in the lashings of a mule's pack.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:25:There was the other thing, and he came to it when he had finished with himself, as he came to the difficult page of any file.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:37:Seln had done the sum himself, in the half-breath before. It was the first thing that a person of his trade did on entering any room, and he had done it without choosing to: who is here, and what can each of them see, and where will each of them be tomorrow. There were four who had known for years and had been tried. There was a healer, who was owed everything and knew nothing, and who walked seven roads. She had her back turned and a hundredweight of copied stone between.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:47:The seal was a stranger matter. It guarded the place where a boy had learned that craft, and the place was a man. Seln had spent fifteen years keeping other people's secrets in drawers; he had not looked to end by being one himself, kept shut on a hillside by a person of eighteen who had weighed a friend's throat against it and had still stopped to count who was looking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:51:The stars went over, and the frost came down, and toward the end of the watch he let himself think about the iron.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:95:He had taken those rounds for what a junior takes everything for, which is a thing that he has not been told. He had supposed a post, or a store, with a guard on it. It had never once entered his head that a place might be left off a map because of what the ground itself did there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:117:The healer had named the hour herself. She had woken with the sun and eaten the sixth of a loaf that lay by her head, and slept again, on purpose, for two hours more; then she had taped Lira's foot without a word, and sat down against a pannier with her eyes on the top of the sky. She would do her two when it was straight up, she said. They had all told her that it was the hour at which those animals had never been seen about, and if she was to shout twice more she would do it then.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:125:"No. Don't write it. I can see what you want to know. I've taken the swelling down and started the rest, and that's all there was to do. The voice comes back by itself, rough first, and you'll leave it be until it does. You don't whisper. A whisper's harder on it than talking." She had flexed her fingers. "That's one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:183:He had been at the stake since first light, by himself, with a calfskin and a stick of charcoal and a sheet of Karis's clean paper. Cael had watched him from the camp: a narrow dark figure three cords up the line, sitting on its heels, that drew a little and then sat for a long while with its hands hanging.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:189:The healer had come too. She had not been asked. She looked at the square brown bar in the grass, and at the stamp on its neck, for about as long as she had looked at five people under a rock; and then she let herself down beside it, by stages, and sat with her knees up and the iron against the small of her back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:197:"The arm. That was last night, and it was one, and it was the size of two. The throat. The hand. Three mendings in two days." She turned her hands over on her knees and looked at them, and let them lie. "That's a route. Near enough, that's what I carry between a hold and the next one, for the people who feed me and shoe the mule. Three serious hurts and a road's worth of small ones. And I've spent it on a road two days from the nearest roof, on a crew that's not on my circuit, sitting against a piece of iron somebody drove where they'd measured to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:201:"The top gate of my round's eleven days north of here," said Oryn. "That's by the road, at her pace." She tipped her head toward the camp, and the mule. "I'm not telling you for thanks. I've watched you. You count everything that's put in front of you, and that's a figure nobody would have put there."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:251:It lay on the afternoon like the iron itself. Cael looked at the stamp, a thumb's breadth below the beaten head.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:265:She looked at him for a long breath. Cael knew that look as well as he knew his own hand: she was weighing a source. Then she turned the leaf back toward herself and wrote under her question, and held it up again for all of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:283:"We looked very thoroughly," said Seln. "I have been asking myself since last night what a paper is for that sends a crew to fetch a thing which is not there."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:293:Cael saw it then, as he saw a seam in a bout when it was already past mending. A paper had asked whether anybody who went into that ring would come out of it. He had carried the answer down a hill himself, with a light.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:11:"Sixty yards of four-strand. I laid that rope up myself, in the cold term, with him turning the hook." He jerked his chin at his boy. Then he looked at Karis, who had the purse out on her knee, and his long face soured further. "Put that away. What'd I do with it? There's not a rope-walk nearer than the turf fire, and I can't draw a wagon out of a river with marks."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:65:It had come down a mountain inside her shirt and six days along a valley, and it was soft at the folds. Pike laid it on the oak and put two fingers on it, and read it to himself from the careful writing at the head to the foot, where five strokes stood side by side over a horned head and three wavy lines; and then he read it again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:83:It went down the meadow in that level carrying voice, past five fires. A woman looked at Lira's boot. A man by the nearest tent said something to his neighbour, low, and the neighbour looked at the strange cart, and then at the empty place beside the smith's shed where a hired one had stood. The Oxhollow carter had halted his mules at the edge of the grass to listen. At *carts go round* he had nodded slowly, like a person who hears his own errand read out to strangers; and at *cart lost* he looked at his boots.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:113:"I want one thing straight, for my own page," she said. "The seventy is in here. I counted it at a ford, and it hasn't altered on the road. Earned stands at a hundred and thirty-two. What you have written under that line is another figure, and I don't believe that it is money."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:117:"Seven," said Lira, under her breath. She was counting on her fingers. "A boat, a fence, a wagon, a mile behind a mule. A ford. A well. A mountain."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:157:"I have that noun," she said. Her voice was very careful. "I have had it since the fifty-eighth day, from a carter's wife, in three sentences, and I have had nobody to put a question to. You have just said it to me yourself, at your own plank. I think that I may ask now. What is it?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:167:Under the eave there was no sound, and out in the meadow a mule shook its harness.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:189:Cael knew the place. East of a square with a dot in it, two days of ticks out, a double line left its straight and went out to the left in a curve and came back to itself, as a path goes round a pond; and inside the curve a carter had drawn nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:191:She read the paper's third sentence over to herself, with her lips, and her finger had not moved.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:217:A hundred marks was more than everything that the board had paid them in ninety days, the mountain included. It was to walk two days east of a hold where they were known, and pick up something that somebody had left lying, and carry it home. He could find no opponent in it. There was a ring on a road, and a rule with no reason given, and instruments, which were only things; and he heard himself thinking that, and distrusted it, since he had thought the same about a shelf.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:227:Seln had not spoken at all. He stood at the plank's end with his hands behind him, and he had not yet troubled with the words; it was the sheet itself that he studied, where it lay, as a joiner studies a board.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:271:It was true, and he had not known it, and she had. He had refused nine things a stride at a time for ninety days, and thought himself a careful person; but a refusal that is made again at every stride is the opposite of a rule, for it is a door that somebody is holding shut with his hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:49:He saw the six days then, all at once, as he saw a bout when it was over. A girl with one arm tied had gone off behind a barn each evening with another man's flint-bag full of pebbles, to teach five fingers the work of ten, so that when the day came she could pay for it herself and ask nobody to hold a purse open for her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:67:"Earned, a hundred and thirty-two. In hand, a hundred and eight, less six." The hinge creaked. "A hundred and two. It is the first that I have paid out. And been glad to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:105:The small man stopped talking, so suddenly that even the geese seemed to notice. He looked up at Karis, who was not much taller than himself, with his head a little on one side.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:115:"I've had my hands on those two trees for twenty-six years," said the small man, quite mildly, "and I never knew that. I shall have to go and tell them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:131:The hold had eaten. Its fourteen had drawn off to the far end of that low room, to their mending and their dice and an old man asleep in the chimney corner, and they did not stare; but Cael felt himself counted from under a good many eyelids. The board had been scrubbed. Six bowls stood on it, and six horn spoons, and a round of bread that was black on both faces.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:149:Seln carved. He had asked for the knife himself, to the surprise of everybody but Brom; and he stood at the board's end with his cuffs turned back and looked at the kid for some time before he touched it, as he would have looked over a room before he went into it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:163:It was Brom who began the song. He had a great deal of voice and no notion where to put it, and he got through the first line by himself. Lira came in on the second with the words that they used at Fenmark, which were not Brom's words, and the two of them stopped and looked at each other across a bowl.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:189:"Nineteen."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:193:"Nineteen," said Brom, and lifted his cup. Karis creaked it after him. Seln inclined his head.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:201:"Here, at the board, with us looking. You never write the good ones while they're happening. You write them afterward in a loft, by yourself, as if somebody might catch you at it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:209:*Hundred and second day past the Line. Nineteen. Written at Thornwater, in the steading's common room, the fire lit for us. Witnesses: four of my own, and a healer who's a stop.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:211:For a breath only the spit made any sound, ticking as it turned. He had put four of them down as his own, without asking leave, and the fifth by the one name that she had ever given herself in their hearing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:249:"Then I'll tell him so. I've been three days on that road, light, and I've a mule to see to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:257:Seln passed it, and she read it for herself, once, and gave it on to Karis. Cael had written a healer down as a stop, in pencil, a minute before a one-armed man's line came in at the door to say that the country had already entered her otherwise. A clerk's fair copy of that same count was in a boat by now, going west to whoever had put the question.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:261:The small man brought the basket to the board himself, with a knife, and set it down in front of Karis.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:267:She had the hold's whole attention. The dice had stopped at the far end of the room, and two children had crept up as far as the bench's end. The steading's woman had come and stood behind her husband with her arms folded, and it was plain that she had heard a good deal about apples in twenty-six years and had never before heard them disputed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:273:Karis looked; and Cael, beside her, could see it for himself: the green was white within, hard and wet, with a star of small black pips; the brown was the colour of cream, and its pips were fat and tawny.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:291:"It is. It'll be sour when I'm dead." He laid the knife down. "I put the pair of them on two different stocks with my own hand, the year that I married her, and they've never agreed about anything since. You'll not tell a keeper from an eater in the one basket, on the one day. You want to see them both at the thaw."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:293:He said it without any triumph, as a thing that he had found out for himself in some year of his own; and Cael, who had watched Karis rule five rows at a stone and refuse to rule a sixth, saw that sentence go into her and stay.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:303:"Three," said Brom, with enormous content, and took the rest of the brown ones for himself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:309:The steading's woman cleared the board herself. She took six bowls on one arm, and left what was not hers to clear: a basket with three brown apples in it, and a book, and a scrap of bark. Cael drew Hesk's satchel up from under the bench to put the bark away with Pike's other lines.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:317:"I have had the day in my head since the shelf. I had it the last time, also. I stood in a corridor on that occasion and told you about a board." He had his hands behind him. "I thought that I should like to learn whether I could say the thing itself. Many happy returns."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:341:He had been going down the middle of himself, under the breastbone, where the ten lay closest. They drew together there, as he had told a healer in a yard; it was like the place in a hold's yard where every path crosses. He went into that crowded place at his own uneven pace.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:361:He took his hands away from himself, and the going-along came home and lay down. He did not go back for another look. It was a little while before he could have said why, and then he could: he had spent years asking that thing to show itself, and it had; and he had liked it a good deal better as a line on a leaf.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:367:*Hundred and second day, night. The loft. Read myself, as she set it: once, alone, on my back.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:371:He ruled a line under that, and wrote the inventory beneath, since a birthday was as good a day as any to count what he carried. There were ten confirmed. He set down the nine in their order, each with the Path that it stood beside and the person it had come from, and against the second of them he wrote *the Bronze from Ardenmere*, as he always did. He set down the tenth: Tide-adjacent, from Oryn; partial; Iron; himself, and whatever his hand was on. It was the first that did nothing in a fight, and the first that he had reached for knowing what he did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:373:Then he came to the foot of the list, where a line had stood by itself for as long as he had kept this book and the one before it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:387:*Daeva's rematch, Reydan's answer — both ride east. Nineteen. Ten fragments. Hesk's package is on a ferry somewhere. The last one said mind the shape. I'm starting to.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:389:*Deployment since the Line: thirteen.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:391:Below him the yard was white and empty. On the platform by the western gate a lean man stood where he always stood. Cael shut the book on nineteen, and put it under his hand, and lay down beside a wall of other people's barley.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:5:It lay a yard west of the stone, where it had come down, with the frost of one night on it. Seln had passed a turn of the long cord round its hind legs. The healer brought the mule up, and the mule looked at what she was to draw, and laid her ears along her neck, and was led round twice before she would stand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:25:The mule drew the thing away to the south, outside the line, a hundred yards and more downwind, and left a flattened track through the grass like the lane on the far side. Cael watched it go. He had held one of those by the head for the length of thirty counts, and thrown another, and been struck by a third in the dark; and in daylight it was a long grey animal with frost on its hide, being pulled over a field by its feet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:41:It was a strange afternoon. They spoke of a good many things. Lira asked how far a mule could smell a dead dog, and the healer told her. Brom wished to know whether a bow was meant to pull to the left or whether that was him. Karis wrote that she would open the box under a roof and not before, since there was wind, and held it up; and they agreed that there was wind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:59:*The nine: held. Deployment since the Line: thirteen.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:69:She had said the whole of it over to herself that morning, to have it exact, as she would have got a card by heart. It was the last part that she kept coming back to. She had spent her life catching things. A lath, a wrist, a rail at the top of a climb; a small pale person who never looked where she put her feet. Her left hand went out for things before she had been told that they were falling.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:77:She let herself have that, for the length of a few breaths, since nobody else was going to give it to her. Twenty yards on a thing as round as a rail, in a boot like a bucket, on a leg that she had been forbidden to run on, with two dogs under her heels and one beside her knee; and she had been pulling away from all three. There had been nothing in it but legs. She had felt thirteen. She had felt the lanes at Fenmark come up under her as if they had only been waiting.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:81:She left the ankle alone. She had asked a healer once, from the back of a mule, and had been told what it was worth and to whom it was owed; and last night that same healer had watched her not ask and had answered regardless. That made twice told. She would not make it twice asked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:93:It was hers. That was the whole of it, and she made herself look straight at it. He had had it off her before either of them knew that he could, and for two years it had been the one that he was shyest of, since it was hers and she was standing there. She had stood at that stone at noon, yesterday, and gone up on her toes, and asked for all of it with a panel's worth of wanting; and she had been given a step.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:121:The healer got up in the grey. She did not go to the mule. She walked the ten yards to the stone by herself, past the place where a dead thing had lain, and stood on the near side of it with her boots together, as she had stood on the first evening with her hands held out over nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:133:For the seventh she fetched the mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:137:She was a long time at that one. The mule turned its head and nosed her elbow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:139:The eighth was the longest, and she did nothing in it at all. She let go of the mule and walked three strides into quiet ground and stood there, square, with her arms hanging and her face toward the pale thing on the hill; and he counted a hundred before she moved. He thought that he understood it. She had told four holds' worth of carters, for thirty rounds, that she had no business in that place. She was finding out what she was when she stood in it: a woman in a brown coat, with a mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:149:It was also the only thing that he had ever watched that woman do for herself. She had not done it for a table. She had got up before the light to do it where nobody would be looking, and she had found that somebody was, and had said nothing, and gone to see to a mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:161:"You could. And if she puts a foot in a rut, you come off, and you come off onto that." Oryn had looked at the sling. "A fall off a mule's a fall. You walk."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:177:"You've got his other boot up there on the pack. If you say one word about my putting it on to match, I'll have it off that mule and you'll wear it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:197:Karis looked up at her. She looked at the sling, and at the hand that had gone back into it, and at Lira's face. Then she got up by herself, brushing her knee, and found her pencil, and wrote something quite short and held it out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:201:The healer had not broken step. She was ten yards ahead, on the low side of the mule, and she had her head turned on her shoulder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:211:The boy was on the mule's other side, at the off shoulder. He was breathing to her beat, as he had learned to on a road while she was not looking, and every so often he stood still for the space of a breath with his weight on the right leg. She had counted his strides between. It was eleven when they left the stone. By the spring, where they halted for the night, it was nearer forty.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:213:He had looked across the mule at her when Karis fell. He had seen the hand come out, and seen it go back. He had not said anything, and he had not looked away.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:215:*Later.* It was still on her side. She could wait. She had waited four days on a mountain to admit that a mule had a kind eye.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:239:"I tell myself that every night."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:253:He sat among them with the cake warm in his hand. He had walked a day at a mule's shoulder with nine things shut, and they had gone back under his refusal like dogs that had been run; the heaviest of them lay easier tonight, and the cold had left his fingers at about noon. He had a sore leg and an arm that was coming back. He was, he supposed, mending.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:267:Cael noticed it as a person notices that a noise has ceased. He had been walking at the mule's shoulder since first light, across the last of the pale grass, and he reached for the number of his strides and found that he had none; he had not stood still since the spring. He tried the calf on purpose, a long step and then a quick one. It was sore, and it was a leg.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:271:He looked across the mule's withers at the healer. She was on the low side, as she always was, with her hand on the cheek-strap and her eyes on the road, going at the one pace that she had. She had said the ninety-ninth for his ribs, at a gate, and the hundredth for the other thing. The hundredth was tomorrow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:275:It was a mile to the fork. He walked it at a mule's shoulder with a whole leg, and for the first time in fifteen days he let himself look.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:277:She would lay both hands on him in the morning, and all that she was, for as long as it took. She would do it in earnest, since she did nothing in any other way. He had felt that ninth thing come to him at the end of a bout with a friend who had asked only to be shown straight; he knew, as well as he knew anything about himself, what her morning was likely to be made of.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:279:He wanted it. That was the first thing that he found when he looked, and he was shocked at the size of it. He had wanted very little for himself since he crossed the Line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:281:The second thing took him the rest of the mile. A woman was walking on the low side of a mule, six feet from him, who had added a stop to the only thing she owned in order to read a boy whom she could not read; and she had not the least idea that the boy might come away from her table with a piece of what she read with.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:365:There were five people on it, under the window of the back room. They had seen a mule come up the valley and had come out and sat down, as eight had sat down at Oxhollow, and one of them was a man with his shin wrapped. She did not eat first. She went along them at her stride and a half, with two fingers for each wrist and her lips moving to ten, and at the end of the bench she turned round.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:5:The spark took on the third stroke. Karis's small brass lantern had come out of the middle box, and when its flame stood up Cael saw the camp that six people had made without meaning to. They were ten yards west of the grey stone, on the old line of the road, with the mule standing over them and her ears laid back. A long grey thing lay between them and the stone, where it had come down out of the air, and nobody looked at it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:39:He had come to himself. He looked at the healer when he said it, since she was the one of the six who had no reason to know what she had been looking at.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:43:Oryn looked at him for as long as it took her to draw one breath. She had seen a boy burst sideways out of a file with a crack like a whip, in a place where she herself could not so much as count; she had seen fire in his fist. He waited for what every person with a seal had asked him since a district hall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:73:He listened for it the whole hour, and he learned what that hillside sounded like at night. The pale grass never stopped. The wind went over it from the north in long breaths, and it hissed and lay down and stood again, so that he was always hearing something come and finding that it was only the country. Twice Brom shifted his feet. Once the mule blew, and once, far off toward the north, a bird got up and cried and settled. There was a small dry ticking, close by, which he could not place until he saw that it was the frost, taking hold on the wet cloth in the heap.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:79:It was closed. That was all that he let himself see: skin, where there had been none, and on it two short rows of small pink marks, one row on the upper side and one beneath, each mark no bigger than a barleycorn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:93:"A mend that deep holds nothing for a season. Nothing. That arm is not to hold. It's not to strike. It's not to catch. Not a rope, not a knife, not a fall, not a friend. Not until I say. And I'll say at first snow, near enough, if it's let alone."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:109:She said it as Brom had once said the hour of a week, to have it exact. Then her eyes went down the length of herself to the great boot, which lay on its side in the grass with the foot still in it, and came up again; and she shut her mouth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:119:She got one hand to the ground, to push herself up, and the arm folded. She did not so much lie down as stop being upright. She went over onto her back in the trodden grass, on the old line of the road, with her broad hands open at her sides and her mouth a little open; and she was asleep before Seln had come in out of the dark to put a blanket on her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:123:There were six of Thornwater's blankets on that mule, and there were six people.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:155:Seln was standing at the lantern's edge. There was grass-seed in his hair and the strap of a locked case across his chest, and his breath had long since come back. He had gone the better part of five hundred yards by himself through that grass with eight hounds on a floor behind him, and come up a lane with a bow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:175:He washed it himself, from the skin, with his teeth set, and wound a strip of clean linen out of the healer's pannier round it; and he took up the bow again with that bundle and went back to the north.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:193:*Lira: left forearm bitten through and held; mended at the line, an hour, by lantern. One arm till first snow, by order. Right ankle gone a third time, and not mended. Karis: throat; no voice. Brom: four fingers, on a string. Seln: nothing. Oryn: spent to the bottom, and asleep on the road.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:223:*Deployment since the Line: thirteen.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:227:It was not the whole. He looked at that squarely, with the pencil lifted. There were things that he had done at noon, by himself, a cord north of this stone and on a floor, which nothing had seen and which he had written of only to say that he had told nobody. They were not in the twelve. He would not put them into a count that he could not yet explain, and he would not pretend to the page that they had not happened.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:253:The mule shifted. Somewhere to the north Brom cleared his throat, and was quiet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:19:"*About five years*," said Seln. "I said five, with a cold term to either side of it, and at the stake I said that it might be six. I should put *five or six*. It reads a little surer than I am."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:53:Karis laid the roll in the middle box, by itself, at one end, a little apart from the name and the five rows and the founding stone, as a person sets down on a bench the one tool that belongs to somebody else's trade.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:85:The others went up; and Brom took the lantern's fellow from its nail and lit Lira up the ladder ahead of him, a rung at a time, with his big hand held ready under her heel and never touching it. He had been told what that arm might not do, and had decided for himself what the rest of her was likely to want. Seln went last and made no sound.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:157:She settled the matter of the mule before the middle of the morning, and she settled it as she settled the order of a bench.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:159:Cael had not seen that it was a matter. He had known for eleven days that three boxes stood in that loft at a hundredweight each, that the carter who had brought them east was gone home with grain, and that the ferry was two days off. He had put all of it where he put the things that would be dealt with when there was leisure, behind a table and a birthday and a week of slips.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:175:"You'll have the mule," said Oryn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:181:"I've had her thirty rounds, and she's a mule. She'd as soon carry boxes as panniers. Boxes don't want mending at the end of the day." The healer spoke more quickly than she was used to, and looked at none of them. "There's a beast at every gate on my round. They feed mine, and one of them shoes her. The next has a jenny that's eaten her head off since the barley was in and done nothing for it. I'll have that one off them, and they'll thank me. You've nobody who'd lend you so much as a goat."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:185:"You can't. Nobody's put a coin in my hand since I crossed the Line, and you'll not be the first to try." Her eyes came round to him, tired and level. "And you least. I've a bill against you that I've not finished adding. I'll not have a mule put in on the other side of it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:189:She went to the animal herself, where the small brown mule stood at the trough with her ears forward, and laid a hand flat on her neck, under the mane, and stood so for as long as it takes to count ten. Cael could not tell whether she was reading the beast or taking leave of her, and he thought it likely that for that woman the two had never been different acts. Then she took the hand away and spoke to Brom over the brown back, without turning her head.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:191:"She goes on the low side of nothing. You go on the low side of her. She'll not cross moving water with anybody on her near hand, and it's no use arguing; she was like that when I got her. Her near hind's been shod since the Oxhollow road, and it'll want looking at by the thaw. She answers to a click." She showed him, with her tongue. "She's never had a name, and she's not to be given one. It's a beast. You name a thing and then you're sorry for it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:203:Lira stood looking at a mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:249:Nobody cheered, since it was not that sort of hold. The steading's woman said "Well," to nobody, and went back to her door, and the small man was heard to remark that he had said so. At the back room the healer pushed herself off the door-post. She had not been watching the sack at all; her eyes had been on the place where a left arm was, behind the planks of the walk, and whatever she had seen there, she went in without a word.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:325:"There's one thing more," he said. "I read myself, on the night of the birthday. I found something. I can't give it to you. It's the healer's to hear before anybody; that was her price, and I'm paying it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:5:The room was warmer than Cael remembered it, and emptier. The cold term had sent half the crews home to their own fences, and the benches down both sides had room on them; but there was a fire in the great hearth that a man could have stood in, and it threw its light across the plaster where the old sheet had hung since the sixtieth day. A boy held the new cloth taut with both palms. The one hand moved over it, steadily, and went to the horn pot on the bench and came back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:7:*Teague.* It stood by itself on the first line, as it always had.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:29:She went and read it, with her nose a hand's breadth from the wet ink, and came back looking thoughtful. One of the two was a crew of four who had brought a string of mules through the cold road's pass and lost none of them. She had seen them in the meadow, she said, and they were none of them young, and one had a wooden foot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:31:"So that's what tenth is," she said. "It's just in front of a man with a wooden foot who never lost a mule."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:55:He did it on the eastern landing, with the scow's rail still under his hand. The spear was ashore already, and grounded. The boy had the two mules by one rein and was looking up the bank at the lower inn's chimney, where there would be something in a pot. The bow came off last, and stepped down onto the planks with the right foot, and stood on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:59:He sent the boy to the smith's with the mules and went up to the plank by himself, as he always did, before he had so much as washed. Pike was taking in his book for the night. Teague read the oak from the left-hand post, a slip at a time, and at the second slip he stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:89:But it was not what had stopped him at the second reading. Something in the paper itself was wrong, as a load is wrong on a mule a mile before the girth shows it. He went round it twice more, looking for the place. The witness was not named, and there was nobody at the far end to say that the things had ever lain there. The words were a clerk's. None of that was a reason either. He had taken worse paper than this from better men, and brought it home.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:93:He laid it back under the stone himself. He set the corner on its own line of dust, as nearly as he could, and took his hand away.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:119:"I shall be brief, since I am not disinterested, and I want that known before I begin." Her voice was small and exact. "I have three boxes on a cart outside. Every leaf in them is about the country we left, except the handful that I have written myself since a river. In ninety days I have met one thing on this side that somebody else thought worth a sheet of paper, and it is lying there."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:129:Brom had turned the paper toward himself with one finger, and he had been doing a sum. Cael knew the look of it. He had worn that look over a wash and a fan and a cut axle.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:167:"There is a margin. It has been pricked, here and here, at even distances, with a wheel; that is done so that every sheet from a desk is ruled alike. No hold has such a wheel. The broker's clerk writes on whatever comes to hand." His finger moved to the head of the sheet. "*Recovery.* One word, set by itself, with a stop after it. That is how a class of business is named at the top of a paper that will afterward be put away with others of its class. A carter would have begun, *I have lost.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:199:Nobody at the table needed the sum done twice. A square woman had stood in a gateway on the eighty-fourth day, with a mule's cheek-strap in her hand, and had said nine days. The road to the Fallow Ring went through Thornwater, and by no arrangement of theirs it went through on the day that she had named.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:219:*The nine: held. Deployment since the Line: one.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:229:Teague was there. He stood a little way out from the eave, on the trodden grass, with his thumbs in his belt, and his three behind him at their distances: the spear, grounded, and the bow on the good leg, and the boy with a heel of bread. That made four. The Oxhollow carter had come up from his mules and stood chewing, which was five.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:5:She had waited seven days to do it. Cael had watched her carry that restraint across the Fallow and up a ladder and through a birthday, with eleven sheets lying dry in the dark not six feet from her blanket; and he had understood that it was a kind of manners. She would not look at a thing until she could look at all of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:25:"That is the ring laid flat," said Karis. She turned herself slowly on the keg, all the way round, as she would have turned in the middle of that floor. "I could not see them all at once when I stood there. Each one hid the next. Now I can."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:35:She had Lira lift the first strip by its head and hold it to the light of the door. The rubbing stood out white on grey: fine lines, straight and hooked and crossing, a band of them that had gone right round the foot of a stone and met itself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:55:"I was wrong about apples." Karis rested her throat, and went on. "I looked into one basket, on one day, and I said *two*. A man who had kept those trees for twenty-six years showed me three. This is one basket." Her hand went round the eleven strips. "I have one floor. If a hook is cut a little wide on the ninth stone, I cannot tell whether that is a different kind, or the same kind a little wider. Nobody could, from here. So I shall count. And I shall not number the kinds from one site."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:75:She did not know, and she made herself say so over, there on the keg.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:141:"The same, if nothing breaks. A round never gives a day back." She pushed herself off the door-post. "Eleven's a good deal. I've been worse. I've been late twelve times, and nobody died of it that I know of."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:217:The first was the table of the trials, with five rows on it in Cael's writing, one to each of them who had stepped over a stone, and a sixth that she had ruled herself and filled in from his mouth. The second was the floor: *Round. Level. No seam. Matches nothing.* The third was the perimeter, sixty-two stations, and against every one of them the same offset in the same hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:219:She had made each of those leaves by itself, on a different day, for a different purpose, and she had kept them apart in the box. She had never before set the three in a row.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:225:She had been afraid of it, and she had looked at the fear as she had once looked at her own tidy rows at a stone. She had been frightened that night by how well a thing agreed, and had feared that she was fooling herself. This time she feared that she was not.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:3:He drew the ladder up himself, hand over hand, and laid it along the edge of the loft.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:67:Seln's head had turned. He had put himself at the loft door, where he could hear the yard, and until then he had not looked at the lantern at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:83:"The first is that if I tell her, it's a different morning. She would be reading a patient that she'd been warned about. She might keep some of herself back, to see; or she might go in looking for the very thing I'd named. I don't believe that either of those is the whole of her, and the page says that it has to be real. I'd have turned it into a trial."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:115:"If I tell her, she may say no. She's a person who says no; I heard her say it to a girl on a mule. And then there is no morning. She goes north in a day or two, and I never lie on that table, and the one time that it could have been tried is gone." He made himself finish. "I want it, and that's under all three. I have been a thing that instruments couldn't read since a brass plate went dark over me, and she's the first that ever tried it with her hands and told me so to my face. On that hill I was the only thing that worked, and I can't tell any of you why. If I had a piece of what she reads with, I might be able to look."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:167:"That it is the nearest, and that it is not the thing itself. A person who writes down only the first has begun to forgive himself." The mild eyes rested on him. "You wrote such a leaf for the captain, the night before, and you did not tell him, and you have carried that. But he had asked you for a fight, and a person who asks for a fight knows that it will cost him something. She believes that she is the only one in that room who will be doing anything."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:173:"I'm not to make one come. That's the first rule, and it's kept; I never put this in her mouth. She said it herself, at a ford, before I had so much as thought of it. *When you're not broken, I run the long one.* She put a day on her route for it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:225:It was a thin difference, and it did not make the thing allowed. He was exact with himself about that, since he was trying to be exact about all the rest. To cross the yard now and knock would be right. To say in the morning, before she had so much as washed her hands, what those hands might lose, would be right. Four people had shown him both doors. A leaf written first opened neither of them; it meant only that when he walked past them he would be doing it with his eyes open, and could never afterward tell anybody, himself least of all, that he had not known.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:249:Nine things lay in him, and he went along them as he went along anything at the start. Wind was in his feet, and still. Pressure sat under the breastbone. The read was shut, as it had been since a rut. Compression lay in his frame, and Ember in his right hand, with nothing to look at. The bright quick one had asked about the table's edge when he lay down, and Anchor about the sill, and he had told each of them no. The heaviest lay low, like weather that has blown itself out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:251:The ninth had drawn itself in very small. This was the first light, and not the last; that one came up at dusk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:69:The grey of the yard came in, and the cold with it. Brom was sitting on the bench under the window, alone, with his hands on his knees. He had come down the ladder before it was light, Cael understood, and had put himself where a person could be sent from; he was on his feet before she had opened her mouth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:111:"You let me walk in here with my purse open. I'd put a day on my round for this. I've wanted very few things for myself since I crossed the Line, and this was one of them, and you knew that it was; you watched me want it at a ford." Her voice had not risen. It had gone lower, and each word came out separately. "You used it. You lay there like a man asleep and you let me spend."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:115:"And your four told you. I've sat with that a minute, and I find it's the part I mind most. It wasn't a thing you did in the dark, by yourself, without anybody to say what it was. You'd been told its name by four people, and you'd agreed with them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:131:"Now what it did. You'll want that, for your book, and I want it for my hands. I'll tell you what I'd tell a second healer, and no more." She spoke slowly, sorting it. "I felt nothing go. I'd swear to that. Near the end there was something going along beside me, at my own pace. I took it for myself. You hear your own feet, in a long one, when you're tired. Then it wasn't beside me any longer. It was in you, going by itself, and you were different."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:135:"I've spent a mending. I knew that I would; a long one costs that, and I'm no emptier than a long one leaves me." She unfolded her arms and looked at her palms. "Past that I can't tell you, since I can't read myself. No Tide can. So I'll learn it as I learn everything. There are five on that bench after noon, and the first of them will tell me whether these are what they were. If they come back short, you'll hear it from me before you hear anything else today."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:143:"There's a line in there about what it's to cost you. Whatever I like, you've written, down to my walking north without you on the road. I've been paid in a good many ways out here. I've had a coat, and a cold fowl, and a shoe on the mule. I never yet had a patient hand me his own fine, ready counted, before I'd said what was owing."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:169:"That's the first sensible thing you've done since you took your boots off." She considered him, for as long as she would have considered a bench. "Then use it on yourself first. Before anyone."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:171:"On myself."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:183:He lay down. She put the horn cup into his hand, with water in it, and waited until he had drunk. Then she took Hesk's book back and sat down on the three-legged stool herself, for the first time since he had come into that room, with the book on her knee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:209:She had not said that part aloud. She had taken it for tiredness. In a long reading the healer goes along the patient and comes home to her own left hand, over and over, and toward the end she begins to hear herself as a person hears her own boots on a road at night. This had been like hearing a second pair. They had fallen exactly with hers, with the same short step on the right, where her heel was raw.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:223:She gave herself away every day. That was all that her trade was. She found where a stranger's current was standing behind a break, and opened the way with her own, and held it open while six weeks of knitting went through in an hour; and she paid for the six weeks. An ox-driver had a foot today because she had been a little older by evening. She had never grudged one of them. It did not come from nowhere, and she had always known where it came from, and had chosen each time to spend it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:229:She had known it by the fourth line of his leaf, as she had told him, and she did not say it over now, even to herself, since a thing said over in an empty room is half given away. She turned the keeping of it over once instead, to be certain that she had her reasons straight and was not merely being hard. If the answer had been yes, then to tell him so was a pardon. If it had been no, it was a sentence, and a person can serve a sentence and be done. Either way he would have had from her, a day late, the very thing that he had gone round her to avoid, which was an end to not knowing. He had made her answer into a thing that never happened. It could stay so, and he could do his own carrying.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:247:"That's one," said Oryn. "And you'll sit down tonight, or I'll have it open again myself."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:289:It was himself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:303:He went up the ladder with the sound of himself going beside him at every rung.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:17:"She said that I'd turned her table round. Nobody lies down on it without being told what she means to do to them, and this morning the one who wasn't told was the healer." He made himself give them the rest of it as she had given it to him. "She said the part that she minded most was the four of you. I hadn't done it without knowing its name. I'd been told the name by four people, and agreed."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:29:"She's set two things on it. I'm to use it on myself before I use it on anybody. And whatever I find, she is the one I tell, on a day that she names." He looked round the four of them. "So if there's anything to find, it's hers before it's yours. I'm saying so now, in order that nobody should think later that I kept a thing back without leave. It's part of what's owed."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:37:"Nothing that you'd see. It's contact, and self; it stops at the length of my arm. It won't turn a blow or light a wick. It's of no use in a fight, and of no use to anybody but me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:49:He understood her, and he saw that the others did. She had spent two years ruling a leaf about what he was, from the outside, with a curve on it and a column, and she had wanted the result of that morning more than she had ever wanted a result; she had written so against herself in the dark. Now there was a thing in him that read how people were built, and she would never ask him to turn it on a wall, or on a stranger, or on herself. She had put that down, in the only voice that she had, before she could so much as begin to wish it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:99:Brom took his time over that. Below them a mule shifted its feet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:123:It was the last of the year's. The carters had come up through the stretch at noon the day before and slept in the cart-yard, three heavy men in sheepskins, with frost on their mules' eyelashes; and they wished to be unloaded and gone before the roads grew any harder. Holds farther up the valley would fetch that barley through the whole of the cold term, a cart at a time. Until then it lay in Thornwater's loft, under Thornwater's thatch, which was all that a grain hold was for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:173:He had not asked that thing for anything since a rut on a hill, and not once in a hundred days for practice. But this was a yard, at noon, inside a fence, where a healer mended with her door open and a researcher might light a lamp; and if he was to be half a beat behind himself on some road next week, he would sooner learn what that was like here, among geese.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:177:Something went out of him into the cold, as small as a breath. His weight went over, and the yard was not under it. He came down a foot to the left of where he had been, with his heel striking before he knew of it, and went three staggering steps toward the geese before he had himself again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:249:*Deployment since the Line: thirteen.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:275:He had not been asked that by Brom, or by himself. He stood up from the coping, since it seemed a thing to be answered standing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:281:"That's you, then. You'll get used to yourself. Most people have to, and they haven't your excuse." She rubbed her palms together slowly. "You'll use it on yourself when it's quiet enough to use, and not before. I said so on the table."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:19:He had thought about what it would be. He had lain awake over it in a loft, with the book under his hand. He had been read by a great many things, and the only one of them that he could do himself went down: it opened under his boots like an eye and took the weight of whatever was beneath, a pace and a half, straight into the ground. He had supposed that a healer's reading would do the same, and that he would feel it come down into him from her palm as a plumb-line comes down a wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:29:It was only after that that he found the beat. It was slower than his heart and slower than his breath, and it did not vary. It was the pace that she kept on a road, below a mule, and she was going through him at it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:47:He had supposed that it would be like the talk at a bench, short and to the purpose. It was nothing of the kind. It was the low running murmur of a person who is feeling her way down a passage in the dark and saying what her hands meet, less to be heard than to keep herself from being lost; and he lay under it and counted, and tried to do as he had been told, which was nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:61:"Everyone I've ever had my hands in, this is the part where I know." She said it as a finding about herself. "Iron Skin, you feel the banks first — dense, walled, everything held. Ember, the banks are a fire's, hot at the edge. Wind you barely feel the banks at all, the water's too fast. But there's always a bank. It's how you know which river." A pause, long, her current going along Ember and stopping at the ignition point and lying there as if it had found a stone in a bed. "I can't find one here. I keep putting my hand where the bank ought to be and there's more water."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:81:It was at about the hundred and thirtieth that she came to where the ninth lay, and that one did a thing that none of the others had done. It drew in. He felt it go from her as a snail's horn goes from a finger, small, and smaller, into some place behind the rest where even he could hardly have put a hand on it. He had not asked it to. He had not asked anything of it since a lane. It was a thing made for not being found, and somebody was looking, and it had hidden by itself, as its first owner had hidden on a back stair before he was old enough to choose.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:91:*A hundred and forty-four*, he counted. Twelve minutes. He lay with his neck loose and all nine doors standing as they stood, and for the first time since a morning in a windowless room there was somebody in the house besides himself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:157:It would be right. He did not hide that from himself, lying there. Four people had said so in a loft, and nothing that he had written afterward by a bead of oil had altered it by a hair. There was even an honest way to do it: he could shut the place, and when she lifted her hands he could tell her all the same what had stood open in him while she worked, and what he had come within a breath of taking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:173:The lines came after. They stood where they had always stood, plain, in the place that belonged to nothing else, and nobody in that room could see them but himself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:179:Note: current-perception component; architecture-reading component. Self and contact.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:188:It said *unnamed*, as it always said; and he knew the name, and how she laced a boot. *Iron*: it was as light as the one before it, which a friend had given him on sprung oak. *Current-perception* was the going-along. *Architecture-reading* was the lamp in the rooms. *Self and contact*, and under it, *Contact range*: it did nothing whatever at the length of an arm. He had nine things that could each be used on an opponent, and here was a tenth that was of no more use in a fight than a spoon.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:216:He had told her not one true thing about himself since a hollow of shale, beyond what she had asked; and he gave her that word as he would have handed her the first coin of a debt.

