rg -n -F -e 'the reserve' -e 'five thumb' -e 'thumbprint' -e 'hundred marks' -e 'rope' -e 'map' -e 'case' editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-{01..44}.md | tail -n 100" in /Users/drive/fractured-path-monroe13
 succeeded in 0ms:
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:13:"Lira's had that since dusk. The rest of you have it now. It's fourteen days late, and I'm sorry for every one of them." He looked at the square woman by the ladder. "And there's one thing more that I have, from the night of the birthday, which none of you is getting. I read myself, as she set it. What I found is hers before it's anybody's. I'm telling you that it exists, so that nobody learns later that I sat on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:137:Cael stood at the loft door for some time afterward, with the frost coming in round his feet and the yard white below him. A boat had gone west on the hundred and second day with a clerk's fair copy in it, and that was eight days ago. He supposed that by now it lay on somebody's desk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:169:"You said that you'd say when, and you go in an hour. I read myself on the night of the hundred and second, as you set it, alone and on my back. I found—"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:159:Cael had not seen that it was a matter. He had known for eleven days that three boxes stood in that loft at a hundredweight each, that the carter who had brought them east was gone home with grain, and that the ferry was two days off. He had put all of it where he put the things that would be dealt with when there was leisure, behind a table and a birthday and a week of slips.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:325:"There's one thing more," he said. "I read myself, on the night of the birthday. I found something. I can't give it to you. It's the healer's to hear before anybody; that was her price, and I'm paying it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:5:She had waited seven days to do it. Cael had watched her carry that restraint across the Fallow and up a ladder and through a birthday, with eleven sheets lying dry in the dark not six feet from her blanket; and he had understood that it was a kind of manners. She would not look at a thing until she could look at all of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:3:He slept through the third night, and woke on the hundred and second morning to find that a forge was only a forge.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:55:"It's the hundred and second day."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:189:"Nineteen."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:193:"Nineteen," said Brom, and lifted his cup. Karis creaked it after him. Seln inclined his head.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:209:*Hundred and second day past the Line. Nineteen. Written at Thornwater, in the steading's common room, the fire lit for us. Witnesses: four of my own, and a healer who's a stop.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:367:*Hundred and second day, night. The loft. Read myself, as she set it: once, alone, on my back.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:371:He ruled a line under that, and wrote the inventory beneath, since a birthday was as good a day as any to count what he carried. There were ten confirmed. He set down the nine in their order, each with the Path that it stood beside and the person it had come from, and against the second of them he wrote *the Bronze from Ardenmere*, as he always did. He set down the tenth: Tide-adjacent, from Oryn; partial; Iron; himself, and whatever his hand was on. It was the first that did nothing in a fight, and the first that he had reached for knowing what he did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:387:*Daeva's rematch, Reydan's answer — both ride east. Nineteen. Ten fragments. Hesk's package is on a ferry somewhere. The last one said mind the shape. I'm starting to.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:391:Below him the yard was white and empty. On the platform by the western gate a lean man stood where he always stood. Cael shut the book on nineteen, and put it under his hand, and lay down beside a wall of other people's barley.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:189:"And you've not gone back to it in eight days. I'm not asking you why, since I can see why from here. So you'll go back. You'll read it three times, on three different days, with a night between each. Read it frightened, and read it when you're not, and then you'll know what's there and what was only you." She buckled the strap. "After that you tell me, all of it at once and in order. You don't give me a piece of it now because I'm leaving and you'd like to have paid."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:211:*Hers. Three readings of myself, on three different days. Then all of it to her, at once. Nobody on the road, to find out whether I can.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:113:"I'll read it at the fortnight," she said, over her shoulder. "That's the hundred and tenth. If it's taken by then, it's taken."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:267:She rested her throat. Cael saw that she was choosing how to say the rest, and he saw also that she had made up her mind to say all of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:277:"Everything I can date, I've dated. The floor's under all of it. I don't know how far."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:115:The healer read the arm at first light on the hundred and tenth day, in the yard, with the frost still white on the well.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:159:Cael had not seen that it was a matter. He had known for eleven days that three boxes stood in that loft at a hundredweight each, that the carter who had brought them east was gone home with grain, and that the ferry was two days off. He had put all of it where he put the things that would be dealt with when there was leisure, behind a table and a birthday and a week of slips.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:281:"Of yours. Not half an hour after you'd stood at that stone and asked for all of it, and been given a step." He kept his eyes on the valley. "And I walked back and gave Karis a row for her table that had two true halves in it and wasn't the truth. I had three reasons. One was the healer, who knew nothing. One was sealed. The real one was that I'd found a thing that I couldn't finish the sentence for, and I've never been able to hand anybody half a sentence."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:5:She had waited seven days to do it. Cael had watched her carry that restraint across the Fallow and up a ladder and through a birthday, with eleven sheets lying dry in the dark not six feet from her blanket; and he had understood that it was a kind of manners. She would not look at a thing until she could look at all of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:133:She had the tenth strip across her knee. She had put it as she would have asked the hour.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:159:It was not kindly said, and it was kindly meant. Lira laid the tenth strip down, and set her four pebbles on it one by one, and got up off the clay on her flat foot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:271:"A name that is read out to a meadow is on a boat inside the month," said Seln, from the end of the trestle, where he had stood through all of it with his hands behind him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:171:The place behind his breastbone took on weight. It filled slowly at first and then all the way to the brim, like a cup under a careful hand; and he lay under a healer's palm and kept his eyes open through all of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:188:It said *unnamed*, as it always said; and he knew the name, and how she laced a boot. *Iron*: it was as light as the one before it, which a friend had given him on sprung oak. *Current-perception* was the going-along. *Architecture-reading* was the lamp in the rooms. *Self and contact*, and under it, *Contact range*: it did nothing whatever at the length of an arm. He had nine things that could each be used on an opponent, and here was a tenth that was of no more use in a fight than a spoon.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:35:"This morning there's a tenth." He kept his eyes on her face, which hung over him upside down and grey. "It wants a real thing done to me for a long time, at a cost, by somebody who means it, and you had told me at Oxhollow what the long reading was: both hands, and all of you, for minutes. I knew when I lay down what it was likely to be. At the end there was a place in me standing open, and the thing that you read with was at the door. I could have kept that door shut. I'm better at shutting them than at anything else I do. I opened it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:55:"Is that all of it?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:161:"Then you may have my bread," said Seln, "all of it, with my compliments," and sat down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:335:He could count, though, by sound and by where each one lay. He went along them, and there were nine that he knew; and the thing that went out from his palm and came back was a tenth, lighter than any of them. He had not been able to hear that one in three days of listening. He could tell it now only as a walker tells his own stride, by the going of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:351:It was none of the nine, since he had every one of the nine in his ear as it went by. It was not the tenth, since the tenth was the thing that he was going along with, and the tenth went round it as well. He listened into it, and there was no sound in it of any kind. Nothing in it moved, and nothing in it answered him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:371:He ruled a line under that, and wrote the inventory beneath, since a birthday was as good a day as any to count what he carried. There were ten confirmed. He set down the nine in their order, each with the Path that it stood beside and the person it had come from, and against the second of them he wrote *the Bronze from Ardenmere*, as he always did. He set down the tenth: Tide-adjacent, from Oryn; partial; Iron; himself, and whatever his hand was on. It was the first that did nothing in a fight, and the first that he had reached for knowing what he did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:377:*Still open. Still real. Now I can see it. That's worse. The tenth fragment lets me feel my own currents, and there's one in the place session nine logged, and it isn't the tenth, and it isn't any of the nine. It was there before Oryn. It was there before Brom's read caught it. I don't know what it is. I know where it is now. Patience is over; observation begins.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:7:"It came. There are ten. I told her all of it on her table before I was off it: the plate, and what I am, and where each of the nine came from, by its Path. She had Brom fetch the leaf, and she read it." He looked at Seln. "I gave the ninth its Path and no more. I told her that I'd used it once on that hill, and that I had looked to see where her eyes were before I did."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:93:"Nine. Ten. I can't hear the tenth. It's the thing I'm hearing with."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:129:Cael carried one at a time. It was the first load that he had had on his back since a mountain, and the ribs took it without a word. He went up past his own blanket at each turn, and past Hesk's satchel on its nail, and set the sack down on a heap that had begun level with his knee and now stood as high as his chest; and by the tenth it had come to him that he was being walled into his own bedroom with other people's breakfast.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:259:*Eighty-ninth day. The west bank, a morning short of the ferry. The boxes rode from Oxhollow on the keeper's grain cart, for nothing, since the map said no. In hand, a hundred and fourteen; a boot. Six owed to the smith and a rope to the ferryman.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:17:Seln had come down the ladder last, and the strap across his chest was gone. The case lay in the loft between the second box and the third. He had looked back up at it twice from the yard, like a man who has left a child with strangers, and had said nothing about it to anybody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:213:It cost more than it had at the wash. She felt it go through her from the heels up, like a rope taking a load.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:23:"You'd have reckoned the cart," said Karis. "He reckoned the map as well. It's the same sum; you stopped halfway."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:47:He had read that man's feet in the silt of a ford, and his halts at five halting-grounds after it. He had watched him count three people on a sill before he looked at a room, and had been told by him on a river bank, in a level voice, what a mountain was worth. Now he had the man's answer to a hundred marks as well, at second hand, from a book-keeper who gave out nothing that he had not seen with his own eyes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:81:The price came first, and it was a hundred, which was too much. A surveyor's kit was a thing that one man carried on a strap; he had seen such a kit once, years ago and a long way west. It was two days out to that rise and two home, on a road he knew, and nobody paid a hundred marks for four days and a case. But *too much* was only a reason if a man could say what it was too much for, and he could not.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:147:He had a good many reasons. He could have said what he had said to Lira under the eave, that he wished to see it, and it would have been true. He could have said that a hold had asked for them by name, and that a crew which was asked for ought to find out what it was made of. There was a rope with no figure against it. There was a cart in a gorge, and a line in a book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:163:"I have listened to four cases that turn upon what this says," he said. "I should like to add what it is."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:215:*Ninetieth day. Lowmarch. The seventh line is said: seventy in the purse, sixty-four on the wall. Tenth. The smith is paid, and we have a hundred and eight. A rope is owed to the ferryman, with no figure. Two holds have asked for us by name, and will wait a fortnight.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:231:The hide-woman had her arms folded. Under the ash the old carter had lifted his chin at the first word and was holding it there. Two men in tarred aprons had stopped with a bundle of shingles between them; and the boy who took the standing-money stood on one leg beside a tent-rope, with his bag forgotten in his hand. That was ten.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:5:Oryn had hobbled her on the western side, with a full net and the two panniers still roped on, and the long shape in its oiled cover lashed along the top of the pack as it had been since a mountain. The healer looked at that cover for a breath before she turned away. Then she walked across the line with her hands open at her sides, and Cael saw her fingers close once, hard, as the count went out of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:9:Seln had the locked case as well, shut, on its strap across his chest. He had looked once at the mule's pack, and once at the stone, and had kept it on him, as he had kept it on every road. Everything else that they owned was either in a loft two days west or under the feet of a mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:31:"Ten cords," she said at last. Her voice was not altogether steady. "I worked it at the stone from the round, with a pencil. And I have walked it now with a rope, from the edge, in a straight line, and there is not a full stride between the two. I have never in my life had a figure come home like that."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:119:"I have been looking for what we were sent to fetch. A case, I supposed, or a tripod, or a chain. A peg. The mark where a peg had been." He turned one hand over. "There is nothing on this floor but ourselves, and nothing at the foot of any stone. I have walked a round in the grass at twenty yards from the lip, and another at forty. I find our own track, coming in from the west. I find nothing that a person has set down, or dropped, or driven."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:125:Cael had expected a good many things of this place, and that had not been among them. He stood on a grey floor with the paper's second sentence going through his head: instruments, and abandoned, and a site. Somebody had sat at a ruled desk and written that, with the sum in words and again in a bracket, and had sent it over a river with a hundred marks in a bag. It had lain a month under a stone. A captain had read it twice and put it back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:21:*The high traverse, Oxhollow to Thornwater. To be walked with a cart, and mapped. Paid at Oxhollow, on the map: seventy marks.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:39:"It's six days of road, and he'd not trust seventy marks to the inside of a carter's hat. I'd not, either. That's what *on the map* means. You put the map in his hand, and he puts seventy in yours, and his mark goes on that paper under my strokes. Then you bring the paper here, and I say it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:43:"What does he call a map?" she said. "I've a registry sheet in the middle box with the whole continent on it, printed, and a calfskin that a carter drew for his own wheels. They aren't the same kind of thing. Which does he want?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:123:Cael knew every board of it. They had loaded it in a yard on a bluff with a coach standing by, and it had gone under a gate and down a road and over a ridge, and its bed had carried three boxes and a locked case under one canvas for the whole width of a province. The smith had done what he said he would. The axle had been cut and the hubs set in, so that the wheels now stood a hand's breadth inside the line of the bed on either side. The cart looked narrower in the hips, and a little surprised.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:153:So three numbered boxes went onto the cart they had come east on, left and middle and right, with the corner that was worn white turned outward where Karis could see it; and at the bottom of the third, under the masonry chips, the reserve lay in its canvas with five thumbs in the wax. Seln came down the meadow as the canvas was being roped. He looked at the cart and seemed to recognise it, and put his hand for an instant on the place under the seat where a locked case had ridden from a bluff to a river. The case was on its strap across his chest. He said nothing whatever about a plank, and Cael kept his eyes off his boots.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:167:He bore it as far as the dead tree. Then he stopped the cart with a word, and took the long rope from under the canvas, the ferryman's, which had drawn a wagon out of a wash and a boy's worth of bad air out of a well. He cut nothing. He doubled a bight of it, and knotted it, and passed the loop over his head and one shoulder so that it lay across his chest like a bandolier, and he made the two ends fast to the cart's frame on the near side, low, behind the wheel.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:175:"I'm not pulling. Look." He walked forward until the rope came taut, and then he simply went on walking, with both hands clasped behind his back like a schoolmaster, and the cart came after him. "That's my chest. She said nothing about my chest. There's not a finger on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:181:Karis walked round him once and examined the knot, and the lie of the rope over his breastbone, and both wrists, which were behind him and idle.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:197:*Seventy-first day. The east road, above the wash. Taken at the plank yesterday: a hold's slip, Oxhollow's, for the high traverse between that hold and Thornwater. Walked with a cart and mapped. Seventy marks, paid at Oxhollow on the map. We walk it from this end and come down on his.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:207:"It's a rope."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:251:He had slipped the rope from his shoulder, and he stood with his head back and his mouth a little open, looking at two hundred yards and more of stone that would slide if a goat sneezed on it. Cael knew that face. He had seen it turned on a wagon in a wash with three wheels and a river coming.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:37:"That's not holding anything either. It's closed, and it's sound, and for a week it's the weakest thing on you, so this is to remind the hand." She tapped the cloth. "It'll take what sits on it. It won't take what hangs from it. No rope, no rail, and no hauling anybody up anything; and if you find it shut on something that's pulling away from you, let go."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:77:He said it across the gap, to her, before she had opened her mouth. Oryn considered the box, and the rope that went round it and over his chest, and his two hands, which were open.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:93:There was something else that wanted to help him, and it was harder to turn away. The Iron read would have given him every plate on that lip within a pace and a half: which was seated on rock and which on its fellow, and which meant to go. He would have known the ground as he knew his own weight. He had refused it on a fan, when all that hung on it was a map.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:187:"Then show me the tape," said Lira, a quarter of a mile farther on. "Properly. What's on it I did myself."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:323:"A wheel floats on it," said Brom. "It wants a man on a rope, up the hill side, leaning."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:127:"That's the registry's stamp," said Lira. "That's theirs. What's it doing in a field two days past a fence that isn't on their map?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:135:He had laid two things side by side at a wash four days ago, and had refused to rule a line between them for want of a third. A query had come down to a registry from somewhere above it, asking which way certain people had gone. A paper had come over a river with a hundred marks, in the hand of somebody who had learned to write at a ruled desk. And here, on the edge of a place where a carter would not put a wheel, was a bar of iron with a registry's stamp on its neck.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:213:It lay in the air a breath. Lira was looking at the stones, and Brom had the bill over his shoulder and was counting his turfs. Oryn, who had stood at that stone since first light, was watching five people come toward her along a line that she could not see. Nobody took the words up, and Karis did not seem to look for anyone to do so. She had entered a property of the figure, and she bent to tie the cord.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:3:The ferryman would not take silver for his rope.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:11:"Sixty yards of four-strand. I laid that rope up myself, in the cold term, with him turning the hook." He jerked his chin at his boy. Then he looked at Karis, who had the purse out on her knee, and his long face soured further. "Put that away. What'd I do with it? There's not a rope-walk nearer than the turf fire, and I can't draw a wagon out of a river with marks."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:15:"A rope. When you've one. I'll not put a figure on it, for a figure's a thing you can pay and walk off from."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:19:Karis wrote it down as he had said it. *Owed: one rope, sixty yards, four-strand, to the ferryman. No price.* She put the purse away uncounted, and Cael thought that it was the first debt she had ever entered without a number beside it, and that she did not like the look of the line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:71:"And the map?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:135:*Recovery. Surveyor's instruments abandoned at a site on the Fallow road. East of Thornwater; the second day; where the road leaves its line at the rise and comes back to it. One hundred marks (one hundred).*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:203:None of them had been meant for the others' company. The woman with the barley had never looked at her husband's map with a pencil in her hand, and the carter had certainly never read a broker's paper; and yet all three were speaking of one patch of grass, two days beyond a fence, as though they had agreed on it beforehand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:207:"I said it. I'm bound to say it; twice, the day it came, to the whole grass, with the price." He turned the cream sheet a little with one finger. "The broker's clerk brought it up from the landing the morning after the last sack, sealed as you see it, with a hundred marks in a bag, counted. I've had that seal on bales for thirty years. I never had it before on a slip that sends a crew two days past Thornwater."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:217:A hundred marks was more than everything that the board had paid them in ninety days, the mountain included. It was to walk two days east of a hold where they were known, and pick up something that somebody had left lying, and carry it home. He could find no opponent in it. There was a ring on a road, and a rule with no reason given, and instruments, which were only things; and he heard himself thinking that, and distrusted it, since he had thought the same about a shelf.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:251:The account was square, as far as coin could square it. There remained a rope with no figure against it, and a line in a book that said *cart lost*, and neither of those could be paid off; they could only be added to, by whatever the five of them did next.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:257:This was a different matter from a map. Six people were about to walk into a place where every one of them believed that all six were alike, and he was the only one who could find out whether that was so.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:81:Cael saw it from the barn door. The small brown animal stood at the well with her pack-saddle on and both panniers roped, and a net of the hold's hay tied behind. The steading's woman had come out with a loaf and a cold fowl in a cloth, as six other gates did for that round whenever it came by, and was stowing them on the off side without a word. The bench under the back room's window was empty for the first time in six days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:111:He had seen her do her sums aloud a good many times, on her fingers, with the answer coming out at the end like a weight on a scale. She did not do this one aloud. She stood for as long as it takes to count twenty, a square brown woman in a dead man's coat with a loaded beast in front of her and a road beyond it; and then she walked to the well and began to unrope the near pannier.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:67:*Not a rope, not a knife, not a fall, not a friend.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:339:She said it as she would have said a weight. He felt it go into him like a key. She had told him that at a ford: when he was not broken she would run the long one, and she wished to feel it not read him properly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:53:Nobody asked him why. He saw Seln take up the strap of the locked case by two holes, as he had taken it up at a ford.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:253:Brom had never held one, as Cael saw from how he turned it over; but he had drawn carts and hauled rope all his life, and he found the iron stirrup at its nose, and put his boot in that, and bent and took the string in the fingers of both hands.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:109:He had been a case to a great many people. He had never before been a patient.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:39:They spent the rest of that day getting ready for a road. The Oxhollow carter had his off mule at the smith's for a shoe. Brom went over the three box-ropes a hand's breadth at a time, since they were all the rope that the five now owned. Seln was gone from the meadow between the mail and dusk, and came back with pale dust on his boots, and said nothing; and Cael, who had made a promise at that plank, looked at the dust and then at the fire.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:67:*A query came down to the registry from somewhere above it. Bracken would not say where and I did not ask twice. It wanted former enrollees' last known direction of travel. Bracken answered it. He gave them a map reference to the Line and nothing past it — which is true, and which Bracken enjoyed being true. He told me so at the plaque wall with his hands behind his back, and then told me he had not told me.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:83:"Bracken. A map reference to the Line and nothing past it. Somebody up a stair wanted to know which way we'd gone, and he gave them a place that everybody already knew we'd been, and wrote it out properly, I expect, with a number." She was grinning. "I never liked him. I'd like to shake his hand."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:103:Cael folded the sheet along its creases. He had two things now, and he laid them side by side in his head, as Karis had once laid a noun beside six numbers. Somebody above a registry had asked which way certain people had gone; and a paper had come over a river with a hundred marks, written by a hand that had learned its trade at a ruled desk, sending whoever took it two days east of a fence.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:147:"On this? No. It is a map of days. He has drawn a day's haul the same length uphill and down, and bent his roads to fit the skin. I could lay an edge across it and learn something about the calf." She settled back on her heels, and her eyes went from the one to the other, the black print and the brown hide, lying side by side on a carters' halting-ground.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:149:"Their map has no roads. His has no quiet ground. Between them you get the country."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:251:It was no bigger than a grain of barley; it caught, and the flame came up round it, ordinary and smoking, and the seed was gone into it, and Karis let out her breath in something that was not quite a laugh. He had last seen her do that across sixty yards of mountain, with her hands shaking like a rope under load.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:87:"I don't run it on a road. I've not the day to spare for it, and he's three ribs short of whole." She turned back to Cael, and for the first time since a rock he saw something in her face that was neither tiredness nor arithmetic. It was appetite. "When you're not broken, I run the long one. It won't read you either. I want to feel it not read you properly."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:259:The big one sat on the well's coping with a box-rope coiled over his knee. His left wrist had a cuff of clean linen on it, tied with a knot that Teague knew, since he had seen it on his own people. Mended, then, and under orders. The girl was on a trough. She had one boot, and the other foot was bare and strapped, crosswise, by somebody who had been shown how. It was on its whole sole. He had read those feet in an inn as the quickest thing in the room, and he would not have laid a copper on them now.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:325:*Eighty-fourth day. Oxhollow, at the ford. The map is delivered: sixty-three lines, and the last one says no. Seventy marks, counted. Earned, a hundred and thirty-two. In hand, a hundred and sixteen. Six owed to the smith for a cart that is in a gorge.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:315:There were no instruments, and there would be no hundred marks.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:5:Two of the three boxes were to stay in the loft, and so two of the three box-ropes were free. Brom married them end to end with a splice that he worked by feel, and hung the whole length from a fence-post with a stone on its end to take the stretch out, while she stood by with her short cord, the one that was knotted at every foot. When the rope had done creaking she laid the short cord along it again and again, and at each length she tied in a scrap of red wool from the hold's mending basket. Cael watched her count. She told nobody the figure, and wrote it in her notebook with her shoulder turned, as a person writes down the weight of his own purse.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:7:"It's a cord now," she said, coiling it. "It was two ropes, and they are not to be thought of as ropes again until I have done with them. Nobody is to tie a box with this."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:13:The middle box stood open on the barn floor, and she had been at the others since before he woke. She had the lid off the third. He came up the ladder in time to see her lifting out the masonry chips, each in its twist of paper with a number inked on the twist, and laying them in a row on a sack; and when the last was out there was a flat canvas bag at the bottom of the box, sewn shut and sealed, with five thumb-marks in the wax.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:23:She had the two panniers on, and the hold's woman had roped a roll of six grey blankets across the pack, behind the long shape in its oiled cover, with instructions that they were to come back. The healer stood on the mule's far side from the hill. She had slept, by the look of her, and she gave the five of them one glance from her stride and a half, each in turn, and seemed to find nothing in it that wanted her hands.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:33:"It ends here," she said. "At about this fence, or an hour beyond; the plate isn't fine enough to say. I have carried this sheet for ninety-four days in case it should be of use, and from this gate on it is a clean piece of paper."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:5:The nine were where he had left them. He could have named each by its sound, lying on his back among the barley, and he had no wish to; they went on beneath his thinking as a mill goes on beneath a miller, and the only one that he noticed was the ground, which had come back to its proper time. He lay and enjoyed that for the length of a few breaths. Then he saw that Lira's blankets were empty, and Karis's, and that the purse was gone from the nail where Karis hung it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:133:"I made that," said Brom. "Seln offered. I said that a day like this wanted the proper bread."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:191:It was only a number. He had been given a great many, by a great many people, and most of them had been wrong; this one was merely true. A line in some register moved down a row. The last time that he had felt it move, he had been in a room with a brass lock on its door and a house's seal on every paper in it. There was no seal here. It had been said to him across a goat, in a grain hold on no map, by a girl with one arm, who had counted toward it over a mountain so that he should not let it go by.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:265:"*That place.*" He rubbed the lines on his fingers. "I'd no wish to give it more. But if she says it wants a proper name, I'll use the name."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:13:Cael brought it down with his right hand. The left would not close properly on the ring.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:93:"A mend that deep holds nothing for a season. Nothing. That arm is not to hold. It's not to strike. It's not to catch. Not a rope, not a knife, not a fall, not a friend. Not until I say. And I'll say at first snow, near enough, if it's let alone."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:155:Seln was standing at the lantern's edge. There was grass-seed in his hair and the strap of a locked case across his chest, and his breath had long since come back. He had gone the better part of five hundred yards by himself through that grass with eight hounds on a floor behind him, and come up a lane with a bow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:77:Compression made no sound of its own. He found it as he would have found a rope in a dark barn, by leaning on it: the whole of his frame creaked, very faintly, like hemp under a load.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:23:"And the maps?" said Lira. "He counted eight sheets with holes in them. That's not in your four."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:45:Seln had not moved from the trestle's end. His hand had gone, as it went a dozen times a day, to the strap across his chest, where a locked case hung that he had carried shut on every road past the Line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:171:"So that's two boxes on two backs, and one left over, and a case that the quiet one won't put down." She counted it on her fingers. "And a girl who's to fall on her face sooner than put a hand out, on a road that's ice in the ruts. Who steadies her, when the pair of you have a hundredweight apiece?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:197:She unbuckled the near pannier, which was empty, and left it on the saddle for them. The off one she slung on her own back by its strap, with her tape and her flask and her shears in it. The long rope that she kept coiled on the pack for a spare she took off and hung over her shoulder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:199:"For the next one," she said. "They never have a rope fit to lead with."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:107:"I've one rule at this table that I never taught anybody, since I'd not have thought it wanted teaching. Nobody lies down here without being told what I'm going to do to them, and why, and what it's like to cost. I tell a child. I tell a man who's out of his head with a fever, and I say it twice, in case one of the times gets in." She nodded at the plank. "You lay down there this morning knowing what you were going to do to me. The one person in this room who wasn't told was the healer. You turned my table round. That's what you did, and I'll thank you not to dress it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:95:"That's how you find a handful. One at a time, for money." She nodded toward Hesk's satchel on its nail, where a broker's cream paper lay folded in a book. "Somebody paid a hundred marks to learn whether a crew would come out of the first, and I do not think that he has stopped wanting to know."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:141:Vastin received the broker's completion report as a courtesy copy, forwarded through a registry office that had no interest in any board east of the Line, in the second folder of the morning, marked *Fallow Ring: crew returned, instruments not recovered*. He read it twice — a broker's report from a town on no registry map, on his desk with no routing note — and did not know why it had reached him, and filed that, in the register of a man who had never once in forty years pretended to knowledge he lacked, as the whole of what he knew about it. He returned the copy to its folder unannotated.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:147:He had not slept. He had come down the ladder with Hesk's book inside his shirt, and she was there already, a square shape against the white of the yard, filling a flask from the dipper. The mule stood at the trough with a blanket on her. There was a pannier on the coping with its strap buckled, and a coil of rope beside it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:199:"You've written it, and I've one thing to put under it." She took up the coil of rope and hung it on her shoulder. "You've four people up that ladder who'd say yes to you before you had finished asking. They're yours, and they'd let you, and that's no reason at all. Don't read anyone on the road to find out whether you can."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:241:The track went up through the hold's grazing, past a stone hut, toward a low place in the hill. The goats lifted their heads to see her go by with nothing on a rope. She had a pannier on her own back, with the strap across her chest, and it rode where no pack had ridden since her first year: tape, flask, shears, linen, a cup, a needle, and a loaf. She had the spare rope over her shoulder. It was thirty feet of good three-strand, and she had laid it up herself; and she had carried it for eleven rounds without once needing it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:305:She shortened nothing and lengthened nothing. She shifted the rope on her shoulder, and went down toward the sound at the one pace that she had, on the low side of nothing at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:43:He had wondered, since a night when the boy told him the whole of it in order, what it would be to see his own stair in another person's feet. He had supposed that he would feel robbed. He considered the matter now with some care, standing in frost with a locked case against his ribs, and what he found was nearer to what a mason feels who passes a wall and sees that somebody has laid a course as he would have laid it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:77:He did not read the stamp; he knew what it said. He let his eye rest on it as one lets a hand rest on a latch. A man answers for what he takes: he had said that at noon. Iron of that pattern is counted out across a board by a storekeeper who does not look up, and signed for in a book with ruled columns, and carried away in a case with a strap.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:79:A case with a strap.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:95:He had taken those rounds for what a junior takes everything for, which is a thing that he has not been told. He had supposed a post, or a store, with a guard on it. It had never once entered his head that a place might be left off a map because of what the ground itself did there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:109:"I should like the carter's map," said Seln. "And the charcoal, if it came east in that box."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:215:"I shall say first what this is worth," he said. "It is one man's memory of a thing that he was told not to read. I carried a case once, between two rooms. There were eight sheets in it, of the country east of the Line. I counted them. I saw what a person sees who is counting."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:219:"I have traced three things from his map, and no more. Here is the first water; those are his three strokes. Here is his square with a dot, which is the hold we slept at. And here is his day's measure, a tick to a day, so that what I draw shall be on his scale and not on mine."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:237:"Five years ago by the stake, maybe six. They paced the circles and stamped the stakes and drew the maps with holes where the circles are. That's not ignorance. Ignorance doesn't stamp iron."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:263:"I carried the maps. I never saw the file. I'm not going to draw you one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:273:Cael read them twice. They were the whole of it, and there was not a verb in them that reached past what six people had seen with their eyes. A map had a blank where this place was. A bar of iron had a stamp.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:279:It had ridden there since the ninety-first morning, folded once, between a letter in a narrow upright hand and a leaf out of a ledger. He opened it on his knee, and they heard it again as Pike had given it to a meadow: the recovery, and the instruments, and the rise where a road left its line. A hundred marks, in words, and again in a bracket.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:291:"It is a question. It is put to this hillside, and the hillside cannot write. So it is put to whoever will walk here for a hundred marks; and there is a clerk at a landing, with ink on his cuffs, who has been told to see what is brought down and send word of it west by the next boat." He lowered the paper. "The word that he sends does not need to have a brass level in it. It needs only to say what became of the crew."

