editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:225:It was not a log. It was a whole tree, a pale one, with its root-plate still on it like a great clenched fist, and it came round the upstream bend riding low and slow, and turning as it came. The ferryman's boy saw it first and shouted, for on a river, it seemed, a person might shout as he pleased.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-03.md:241:He grunted, and his boots slid a hand's breadth on the planks, and he bent his knees and put his back into the pole until the cords stood out in his neck. He was a large young man pushing a tree with a stick, and he had never looked less like anything else. The root-plate swung, slowly, and went wide of the cable by the length of an arm, and the whole pale mass turned under the stern and went on down the river.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:95:Cael watched the carter at that halt, and at the next, and learned more from his hands than he had from his mouth. The man carried a lump of mutton fat in a rag, and he worked it into both axles every time the cart stood, though neither had begun to complain. His trace chains were bound in strips of old sacking from end to end, so that they could not ring. He had cut the iron heel-plates off his own boots, a long time ago by the look of the leather, and when the mule threw up its head at a bird he laid two fingers on its nose before it could think of making a remark. He had been asked what was on his road, and had declined to say; but everything he owned had been taught not to make a sound, and Cael supposed that was an answer too.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-06.md:121:He had waited for the hour, and he went without his Path, as he had gone everywhere since a crossroads fire; Shadow was a declaration like any other, and he had told four people to make none. Nobody had given him the reason for the hour, and he had not sought it; but a carter of twenty-two years kept that rule, and so did a hold that had stood behind this fence for longer, and he had not lived to his age by supposing himself wiser than the people of a place. The man on the platform watched him lift the bar. He did not call down. Seln had the feeling of being entered in a column, correctly, by somebody who would not trouble him about it afterward.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:289:*No instrument out here reads me. No Arbiter station, no assessment panel, no registry seat with a seal on it. The board reads what we did. Since my Kindling I've been the case the instruments couldn't parse, and it turns out the cure was a country with no instruments. Write it plainly, because it's true: I'm free. First time.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:117:It was only as the feather went back behind the man's ear that he saw whose hand was on that paper besides theirs. The whole meadow had watched Pike strike a hold and send the bottom of his sheet to do the work of its top. Whatever came up from the ford, or failed to, he would have to stand at this plank and say it, twice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:205:He took his cart over without another word, and it was the first time Cael had watched him do it as a thing that could go wrong. He led the mule a step at a time. Brom went at the tail-board with both hands on it, and halfway across the near channel the off wheel slid a hand's breadth toward the white stones, quite gently, as a plate slides on a wet table. Brom leaned, and the carter clicked his tongue, and it came back into its groove.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:241:There were a good many. The rock had cracked in old floods, and slabs of it stood up here and there a hand's breadth clear of the water, some no bigger than a plate and some as broad as a table. He went from one to the next like a man buying a horse. He stood on each, and shifted his weight, and came down off it shaking his head.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-04.md:57:"I never have," he said. "Not once. Not a plate."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:99:He counted it onto the oak in fours, and he did that ten times, until there was a heap of thick worn silver that would have covered a plate. Karis held the purse open between her two wrists, since she could not grip it, and Brom swept the coins into it with the side of his right hand, and they rang.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:165:"No. I'll put it properly. That's everything I know about the wrong country." Her chin went along the shelves, left to right. "Every leaf I've copied since I could hold a pen, and every one of them is about the place we walked out of: tiers, and ranks, and what an Arbiter station does with a person. I've read forty accounts of how a Path is assessed, and there isn't a page in three boxes that has the word *stillhound* on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:179:A man came out of the black water to his waist, and got one knee on the gravel's edge, streaming, with nothing whatever in his face. He had let his Path go while he was still under the surface, and it did not matter now, for every head on that river was turned to the place where the air had cracked. He moved once. It was a short and exact movement, under the jaw, and Cael had sat across a counter from that man for two years and had never guessed that he owned it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:249:"An hour and a quarter," said Seln, out of the collar. "It lay eleven feet from my face for the whole of that time. I had my Path lifted for the whole of it. It scratched the good ear twice, and it never once turned its head toward the pool." He drew a long uneven breath. "I submit that for your second clause."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:197:"It's a lecturer's word: when you bring up Iron Skin, something leaves you besides the grey. An assessor feels it from across a floor and calls it presence, and gives you a mark for it; a station reads it off a brass plate. You felt your own on the gravel bar, the morning it came without asking." She tapped the page. "Nobody west of the Line has ever cared how far it carries, or what it might be like to something with a nose for it; there was never a reason to wish it small, and every school we went to taught us to make it larger."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-09.md:297:"Quite. If water masks it, then there is one of us who may use what he has for the whole of the evening, provided that he does so up to his chin." Seln's mild face showed nothing at all. "I had meant to propose the pool beside the tail. I have not lifted my Path since a fire at a crossroads, and I find that I should rather like to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:29:He had no Path of his own, and so no tier and no rank, and he said so to himself plainly, because the dark was a good place for plain things. Everybody else he knew had come away from a Kindling at fourteen with one: a single gift, which an Arbiter named and a registry ranked ever after. His own Arbiter had woken too, on a brass plate in a windowless room, and it had taken eleven seconds over him and gone dark, and it had been dark ever since. What answered him now were nine fragments, which was the log's word for them: abilities taken out of other people's Paths, each one inside a fight that somebody else had begun in earnest, and each one written into Hesk's book with a name beside it and a price.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:251:It went all at once, like a cart lifted off a foot. The big fool had put his Path down. In front of her the dog checked. It had come to the edge of the gravel at the bar's head, where the channel began, and the declaration it was running to had gone out like a lamp; it stood there on three legs with one forefoot raised over the water, and its head began to swing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:115:They stood round it, and it was a handsome thing. The registry had printed it from a plate, in black, on a sheet as long as Brom's arm, and the whole of a continent was there: every district with its bound ruled round it, and every chartered road a firm double line, and the towns in three sizes of letter by their standing. Cael found Ardenmere, and a river he had crossed on a bridge, and a bluff above Ostrand with a small square on it. Somebody had written *ridge* beside a pass in the east, in pencil, and a little farther on, *Shelf; a pine; water at 36*, in the same small exact hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:117:A hand's breadth past that pencilled word, the plate ran out of things to say.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:69:It was work, besides, that four half-mended people could do. A man with a bitten calf could pace a level shelf and write. A girl with a stiff ankle could tell him whether a hundred yards of it would bear her, and a researcher who could hold a pencil for a quarter of an hour had a cord, and a clock, and a head that kept numbers as a jar keeps water. Nobody would have to be fast, or hard, or to set anything alight; the job asked for no Path from any of them, and for no fragment of his.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:5:Twenty-six were full grown. Four were half the size, with plates of a paler grey that had not yet gathered any green, and those four kept to the inside of the shelf, against the hill. Each of the grown animals was as long as the cart and a good deal broader. It stood low on four legs like the stumps of trees, and its back and both flanks were roofed over with horn, in slabs that overlapped as slates overlap on a house, the largest along the spine and each with its keel. Lichen grew on the old ones in rings. When one of them stood still, which was nearly always, it was a heap of the mountain's own stone, and his eye slid off it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:7:The head was small for such a body, and carried at the height of a man's knee. It ended in something like the beak of a tortoise, and with that the animal scraped the rock. He could hear it, now that he knew to listen: a dry patient rasping, thirty times over, like a yard full of masons dressing stone a long way off. Under that there was another sound, whenever one of them shifted its weight, for the plates moved on each other with a low clatter, as a slate roof settles when the sun goes off it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:27:Cael weighed it. Thirty animals stood three hundred and fifty yards up a slope of loose plates, and the five of them had a cart, and a rule in a one-armed man's words. To go back was to go down the fan, and to go on was to do for one afternoon what carters did every year.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:125:He had the west end, and he had it faithfully. His eyes were on the last animal of the line, a big one with a broken plate on its near shoulder, which stood a little beyond a rib of the hill, so that from where he sat he could see it and it could not have seen its own fellows at the eastern end. He had been counting the slabs along its spine to keep himself awake, and he had got to seven.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:137:He turned that over while the last of the plates ran out below. He had been watching one animal, on purpose, at the far end of a line three hundred yards long; and he had not seen the cause of what it did, and it had not seen the cause either, and it had moved all the same.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:141:"The west end went up, three steps. I was counting plates on the last animal, and it moved before I heard anything at all."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:151:"The plate tipped, and I saw the edge of it lift. The one on it went down behind, as if somebody had kicked its legs away, and it scrabbled." She swallowed. "And they all went up. Karis, they all went up at once. Not one of them turned to see what had happened; they were already going."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:163:"I saw the plate tip, and I began to count when it tipped. I saw them step in the same instant, both ends, as near as I can judge; and I had said *one*, and begun to say *two*, before the noise of it got to me here." She laid one finger on the glass. "It had three hundred and fifty yards to come to us. It had the best part of that again to go along the shelf to his animal, round a rib. His animal ought to have heard it a count after this one did, and it moved with the rest."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:187:"Then what did the one on the plate do, that a rock didn't?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:197:"They've no Paths," said Brom. "They're cattle with roofs on."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:215:Karis lifted her eyes to the slope. Thirty animals, each the weight of a loaded cart, stood on loose plates above a shelf that had dropped a piece of itself an hour ago under one of them; and below them there was a roof-pitch of the same stone, and at the bottom of that a bench, and five people.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:221:"A frightened calf moved them three steps, and that was enough to finish what was left of that plate. If I'm right, I find out with the hill coming down on the cart. If they don't stir, I've learned nothing, since a seed might only have been too small; there is no size of that test that's worth its answer." She turned the pencil over. "So it stays derived, and I write that word on it, and anybody who reads it knows exactly how far to trust me."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:233:He took out the book and wrote it, with the day at the head: the plates he had been counting, and the big animal stepping before any sound had reached it, and that he had looked for one that went first and found none. He wrote her three clauses under that, and her two words under the clauses. It was the first time that anyone had asked him to keep a finding because his hand would make it worth more, and he wrote it as small and as straight as he could.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:5:"It's stuck," he said, low, with his boot on the shale. The plates did not creep under him as they had the evening before; they held, with a small dry crackle. "The cold's glued it. She'll roll on this for an hour, and by noon she'll float on every yard of it. I'd as soon have her on the far side waiting for the word."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:57:They went. He had watched them for five days and had never seen one of them hurry, and they went along that shelf now like barrels loosed down a ramp, all thirty, shoulder behind shoulder, with the calves between; and the clatter of their plates reached him across three hundred yards of slope like hail on a slate roof. They were running from her. They were running east, along a lip of rock that had dropped a slab under one frightened animal at a walk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:115:He gave it no more than a breath's looking, and that was enough. The front had come a third of the way, and it was no longer grey; it had a brown smoke of dust standing over it, and out of the smoke came the sound, a deep steady grinding with the hail of the plates on top. He passed Brom going the other way, and Brom's eyes went to him and to the cart and he did not waste his wind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:133:He could see the animals now, the foremost, with their heads down and their plates flying; and he saw them feel it. The middle of the line broke. The ones dead above the arrowhead's point flung themselves left and right as if the ground there had been snatched away, and the ones beside them went with them, and in the space of two breaths a front eighty yards wide had torn down the middle into two streams. Eleven paces above the rock there was nothing coming at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:143:Compression had the thing measured already. It had waited since a river for a blow worth taking, and it showed him how: a plated shoulder at a run, met square, and sent down through his frame into the slab beneath the shale. He would have stood. Anchor offered him the other way. *There.* One fixed point against the rock under his left boot, and told to stay, and a boy with a box would have been a post that a river goes round.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:157:Then he was in the air, going the way he had chosen, with both arms still round the box. He came down on his left leg in the shale at the foot of her fire, and slid, and the edge of a plate opened his calf along the old line as a thumb opens a pod.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:193:Nothing in the hollow moved. Dust came down on them out of the brown air as flour comes down in a mill, and settled on the boxes, and on Karis's open hands. Above the wall the face of the bay was a different mountain. The roof of plates had gone from a strip eighty yards wide, scoured to the pale slab beneath, and along both edges of that strip the stone was sliding still, a plate at a time, with a sound like somebody clearing a table in another room.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:227:She had almost reached it. She lay on her front in the grey spill of the other stream, with one arm stretched toward the rock and her cheek on the shale, and from the knees down he could not see her. A plate of the mountain lay across her legs. It was as long as a door and as thick as his wrist.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:319:She felt the plate come.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:329:She could not have said how long it was before the boy came. He came along the lower side of the rock, on his feet and hardly so, with one arm shut against his ribs and his left leg dragging; and he was the colour of the dust. He got down beside her by letting the wall take him. His eyes went over her a piece at a time, as they always did. They reached the plate, and stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:5:He said so on the western landing, with the scow rocking at his back and the far drum already groaning. He was the same long sour man who liked animals and nobody else, and he had stood with his arms folded through the whole of Brom's account: the wash, and the well, and a peeled log on a hill of plates, and at the last a gorge with a cart in it. Brom told it as he would have told a debt, in order, leaving nothing out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:161:"Quiet ground's where the sigil goes to sleep. Yours. Everyone's. You walk a road, and there's a place on it where your Arbiter stops — not slows; stops — and you walk on past and it's back. There's a handful of them. Along the void roads, all of them. Carters walk round them, and have as long as there've been carters. The dogs live in them. Stillhounds. Nobody's asked them why. Why any of it, nobody knows and nobody's needed to, because the rule's *don't go in*, and the rule's never once failed anybody who kept it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:169:Cael had heard *yours* and *everyone's*, and he had thought of a place behind his own breastbone, where a small bright thing had gathered once on a morning in a windowless room, and looked at him for eleven seconds, and gone out. It had been dark ever since, and he kept that thought where it was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:179:"You'll know," he said. "Your Arbiter tells you. It stops."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:277:"So do I." She said it low, and she was still looking at the road. "He said your Arbiter tells you. It stops. I've spent three months not doing the one thing I'm good at, on purpose, a stride at a time, and it went off anyway when a ledge fell. I'd like to stand somewhere that I couldn't. I'd like to know what's left when it isn't me doing the *not*."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:135:He had lived for five days on a shelf that he could have thrown a stone across. Now the wall on his left fell back in a long curve and came round again two hundred yards on, and between the wall and the drop there lay a bay forty yards deep, floored from side to side with fine loose shale that had come down the face and spread. The slope above it was the same roof-pitch of plates, but lower; the upper shelf ran across it not three hundred yards up. He did not need to count what was on that shelf, and he counted it all the same, and it came to thirty.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:161:"And then down the other side to a ford, and a man counts out seventy." She had her arms folded. Her right foot was set a little forward, on its whole sole, and she was looking along the ledge as he had seen her look along a painted floor at Norhold before the bell. "It's the best ground on the whole mountain. It's rock from end to end. There isn't a plate on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:3:They got the plate off her with a hedging bill and a man's back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:9:Cael had her wrists. It was all that was left for him to do, and he could do it sitting, with his back against the pale rock. He drew, and she pushed with her elbows, and she came toward him over the plates a foot at a time with her face shut like a fist.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:13:He saw the boot for a breath, down in the dark under the slab, with the lace still tied. Then Brom let the corner go, because he had to, and the plate settled, and the shale ran in round it and over it with a long sigh. By the time the dust had gone there was nothing in that place but hill.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:21:He chose the splints himself, and they were the mountain's own; he found two plates in the bank above the wall, each as long as her shin and no thicker than a slate, and he laid one down either side of the leg. They had no canvas to tear, for the canvas had gone with the cart. Cael's coat had lost its left sleeve at a river. He got the knife into the seam of the right one, and Seln cut that as well, and Brom bound the two plates on with it in three places, one-handed, with Seln's fingers for the knots.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:63:Above the wall, in the dark, a plate let go by itself and slid, and stopped; and a little later another.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:77:"Karis turned thirty animals with a page she'd not tested," said Lira presently. "And Brom carried two boxes he's not allowed to lift. And you went back for the third and stood under one of those things with your hands shut. I saw the marks where you came down." A plate slid, somewhere above. "I ran away from it, and I was the only one who did what I was supposed to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:109:He went for water first. The seep was at the bay's eastern corner, a hundred and thirty yards from the rock, and every yard of the way lay under the stripped face, where the plates were still coming down singly, with no warning but a small dry tick. Cael watched him cross it without once hurrying. He stopped whenever a slide began, and judged its line, and stood still until it had gone by, sometimes within a few feet of him; and he came back in the rain with the skin full and grey grit to his knees.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:293:The shale still lay banked there, in the shelter of the rock, where neither stream had carried it off; and across it, from a point high up down to either hand, ran two straight black lines. The plates along them had been burnt to the colour of iron.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:343:He watched her face, and he knew what it was doing, for he had seen a brass plate in a district hall wait eleven seconds over a boy of fourteen before it lit. He had seen assessors look at a panel with that same small stillness, and a hooded seat, and a clerk with a form that had no box for him. It was what an instrument looked like when it reached for the thing it was made to read, and closed on air.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:353:He waited for the next thing, since he knew the order in which it came. First the instrument failed, and then somebody asked him what he was, and wrote the answer down, and the answer became a word on a file.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:27:"There he had two tales, and I asked for both." Seln looked at the ground while he told them, as he did when he was reading from memory. "The first is his own time. A salt string was going under a ledge herd on the cold road, and a block had come down across the track; and a Force at the tail of the string shifted it with his Path, since that was quicker than unloading. The hill came down, with the herd upon it. They found two mules."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:45:"We're about to drag a cart up a hill of plates," said Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:65:She went up four yards. Then the wheels stopped turning. Cael felt it through the shaft before he saw it; the rims had sunk into the plates to the depth of a hand, and they were no longer rolling over the slope but pushing a wave of it ahead of them. Brom leaned harder, and the wave grew. The cart stood still for a breath, with three people straining at her. Then the stones under her let go all together, and she came back down the four yards with a long sigh of slate, a little sideways, and stopped where she had started.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:73:"Floats. A wheel's for a road. It wants something hard under it to turn on. There's nothing hard in that." He pushed his boot into the fan and watched the plates close round it. "She'll not roll on it. She sits in it, like a boat in a pond, and she goes where it goes, and it only knows the one way."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:93:He took the third box after it, in the same manner, while the light went yellow on the far wall. Lira carried the stores, two steps and a slide, and would not be told that a bound ankle had no business on loose plates. By the last of her journeys the cart stood at the foot of the fan with nothing in it but its own seat board, and looked like a thing that had been robbed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:103:One end went down to a bridle on the cart's axle-tree, where a pull from above would lift her nose. At the other end he put himself. He stood in a loop that went round his chest and under both arms, facing downhill, with his hands behind his back; and when Karis, up at the log, raised her arm, he walked down the mountain. Seln and Cael went down with him, hauling hand over hand, and for every yard that three people descended the cart rose one, nose first, half buried, with the plates parting round her and closing behind as water closes behind a punt.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:125:It was a small slip, a barrowful of plates running out from under the rim, and the cart dropped a foot and snatched. Cael saw the line take Lira. It lifted her off her seat and dragged her a yard up the boulder's face, with her heels scraping, and he saw what happened in her calves, because he had been watching for it for sixty days. Every sinew in her stood ready to be somewhere else. It was the burst, asking, with the whole of her Path behind it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:137:Within a pace and a half of his boots it would have given him every plate on that slope, and which way the whole loose skin meant to go, a breath before it went. He was a surveyor today, by the terms of a slip, and he carried an instrument that no surveyor west of the Line could have bought, and he kept it shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:149:It was a road, or the stone that a road might have been made on. It ran west from the top of the fan along the wall of the gorge, as level from end to end as a shelf in a shop, thirty feet broad where they stood; and no hand, by the look of it, had ever cut it. The mountain rose on the left in a long grey slope of plates, steep as a roof, to a skyline that he had to lean back to find. On the right there was an edge, and beyond the edge blue air, and the far wall three hundred yards off with a white thread of water hanging on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:237:There was ice on the kettle in the morning, a skin as thin as a leaf, the first that any of them had seen past the Line. Lira ran the ninth hundred with her breath smoking, and they drew the cart on after her with Brom leaning into his rope on the up-slope side. Wherever a gully came down the face it had laid a tongue of loose plates right across the shelf, and on those she floated, as he had said she would. There he walked against the strap with his whole body slanted, like a man in a gale.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:249:A quarter of a mile along, and far up the slope, another shelf ran across the face. It was narrower than theirs. Along it, for the length of a long field, the grey plates of the hillside lay in a row, humped and overlapping, each heap about the size of the cart; and there was a gap of bare rock between each heap and the next, and all the gaps were the same.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:49:Cael stood at the end of the sound rock with the others and saw where a girl had been running three days ago. The shelf stopped as if it had been bitten. Beyond the break there was only the wall of the gorge, and along that wall a scar where the fallen stone had been rooted: a strip of raw pale rock four feet wide, tipped outward like the sill of a window, and heaped with broken plates. It ran on for thirty yards or so, and then the ledge began again, grey and level under its roof, as though nothing had happened. Under the scar there was a great deal of air, and he looked at that once.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:81:It took Cael a breath to understand that she had agreed with him. Brom understood it at once. He stood up under the box like a man who has had a long account settled in his favour, and went over the lip sidelong, breast to the wall, placing his big boots on the scratches as daintily as a cat on a shelf of plates.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:93:There was something else that wanted to help him, and it was harder to turn away. The Iron read would have given him every plate on that lip within a pace and a half: which was seated on rock and which on its fellow, and which meant to go. He would have known the ground as he knew his own weight. He had refused it on a fan, when all that hung on it was a map.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:87:"Since the loft. It has three things in it: no Path on a road, no voice, and no fire. I said that people out here keep all three because nobody ever lived to learn which mattered." She looked round at them. "We've lived, and it's on a page; I dictated it. The animal hunts discharge, and a thing struck from a flint and a piece of steel has none. A fire isn't a declaration, and it never was."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:195:He had the sentence ready; it had come to him at once, whole, and it was the worst thing he could have said to her. *Isn't it better?* He had sat in a bar of red light ten days ago and written that no instrument out here could read him, and that he was free; and he had meant every word, and he meant it still. Not one book in this country had a column for a tier or a rank, or for what he was, and it was the best thing that had happened to him since a brass plate went dark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:295:"What does the board pay a man with no Path at all."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:311:"With no Path."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:293:Teague still could not tell what he was. A Force stood one way and a Wind another, and there was no Path in the set of that boy that a man could name, and no want of one. He went down the bank.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:315:He had no argument. The man had not said that they had failed. He had said what the bench was, on a board that wrote down outcomes, and it was that.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:3:He began at a brass plate, since everything else stood on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:5:"I was fourteen. It was a district office on a cold morning, with a chalk circle on the boards for me to stand in. Something woke behind my breastbone, as it wakes in everybody on that day: a small light at the edge of my eye. You'd call it an Arbiter." He kept his voice low and his breath slow, as she had taught it to go. "It looked at me for eleven seconds, and I counted them. There's a plate let into the desk in those offices, which lights with the name of your Path. For eleven seconds it stayed dark."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:287:He knew that beat. He had tried for four days down a mountain to walk at a healer's pace, and had failed, since he went in fits, as he had always gone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:173:She ran along the rim itself. It was a thing no wider than a roof-ridge, round on top, with the white a finger below it on one side and the turf a hand below on the other, and she went along it as he had watched her go along a ledge under a mountain, close and quick, with her arms a little out. The great boot came down every second stride with a slap. There was no burst in it and no Path, since there could be none; it was a girl of thirteen on the lanes at Fenmark, who had only legs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:185:He saw the foot go over. It went outward, past the place where an ankle stops, as it had gone on round stones at a river and under a plate of shale; and that made three times.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:5:"Smallest first," she said. She had ruled a fresh leaf into five rows and given it to Cael, with the pencil. "A one-armed man has told us that nothing can be declared in there, and five of us felt our Arbiters go, and I believe both. I believed in a ledge as well. There are prints in the grass on the far side of this field, and I am not going to learn that he was mistaken by having Lira crack the sky open a quarter of a mile from whatever left them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:107:He was crouched low against a tussock of the pale grass, side on to them, with his face turned down into the crook of his arm and his hands drawn up into his sleeves. He had put himself to the south of them, where the low sun came from behind him into their eyes. There was no Path in it, and no trick that a child could not have understood once it was shown; and it had still taken five people, all of them looking for him, the better part of a breath to find a man in a dark coat on an open hillside at noon.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:211:"Is it Brom?" said Karis. "Forgive me; I must ask it. His Arbiter stopped at that stone, and yours did. If the thing that you read is his current, and his current is shaped by his Path, then perhaps there was nothing in him to read."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:215:Brom stood on the western side of the stone, where he was an Iron Skin with his Path in the next room. He stretched out his left arm. Oryn kept her two hands on it, and she stepped over the stone by herself, so that she stood inside and he outside, with the mended wrist held between them across the line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:97:He had known since a hill of plates what he thought of a reading that nobody else could check, and it had not altered. So he took the pencil and wrote in Karis's table, under her *level*, the only part of what he knew that any one of them could have got with a hand and a pair of eyes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:115:"If I tell her, she may say no. She's a person who says no; I heard her say it to a girl on a mule. And then there is no morning. She goes north in a day or two, and I never lie on that table, and the one time that it could have been tried is gone." He made himself finish. "I want it, and that's under all three. I have been a thing that instruments couldn't read since a brass plate went dark over me, and she's the first that ever tried it with her hands and told me so to my face. On that hill I was the only thing that worked, and I can't tell any of you why. If I had a piece of what she reads with, I might be able to look."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:157:"I'd tell her after," said Cael. "That is what I've built in place of the before. On her table, before I have got up off it, whatever has happened and whatever hasn't; all of it, from the brass plate on, with the nine named for what they are. And then it's hers to deal with as she likes. If she says that I'm never to come within a mile of her round again, then that's the price of it, and I walk south."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:277:It lay exactly over the place where a small bright thing had gathered on a morning in a windowless room, and looked at him for eleven seconds, and gone out. He had gone looking for that place on a hillside five days ago, with five people abreast of him, and had found it dark. Her palm was on it now. She could not have known that; it was only where a healer puts a hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:33:"It ends here," she said. "At about this fence, or an hour beyond; the plate isn't fine enough to say. I have carried this sheet for ninety-four days in case it should be of use, and from this gate on it is a clean piece of paper."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:201:"My Arbiter's gone."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:225:And nobody had turned to him. He saw why, and it was the simplest thing in the world. All four of them knew what he was. They had known for years that a brass plate had waited eleven seconds over him and gone dark, and that nothing had spoken to him out of that dark since. A line that put out a lamp had nothing in him to put out. It had not occurred to one of them to ask whether he had felt it, any more than they would have asked a man without a purse whether he had been robbed. The fifth knew nothing of brass plates, and she was looking at her own two hands.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:237:*Quiet ground. Karis's Arbiter stopped at a line she can mark with a rock. So did Lira's, Brom's, Seln's, Oryn's — same line, same step. Mine didn't. Mine's been stopped since I was fourteen. I don't know what that means. Nobody asked. Tomorrow we go in.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:123:"He says that there is a place on a road where an Arbiter stops. He says it stops at a line, and that a person knows the line by her own. If that is so, then it can be found with nothing but feet, by anybody, and found again in the same place the next day by somebody else. I have been taught that an Arbiter cannot be tested at all; that it is the one thing every instrument we own takes for granted. I should like to stand where one fails." She drew a breath. "I would go for nothing. I say so in order that nobody should count my hand as a vote for the hundred."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:217:*Twelve weeks. Seven contracts. Pike says there's ground out here where the Arbiter goes quiet, and nobody goes in twice. Karis has been waiting her whole life for a sentence like that. Seln says the contract was written by someone from inside. I say it pays. Two days east, through Thornwater, on the day the healer said. For twelve weeks nothing out here has read me. I'd like to see what a place that reads nothing does with that.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:47:Five people had walked up to this place in a row, an arm's length apart. She was an Ember, and Lira a Wind, and Brom an Iron Skin; there was a Shadow among them of a higher tier than any of the rest, and a Tide who had been trained in a healing house. Karis had sat at lectures on what an Arbiter was. She had copied out, in a restricted room, a good many pages on the subject, and on one point every page agreed: it was a person's own. It woke for that one person, on one morning, and named a single gift.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:57:There were six of them on that road, and she had ruled five rows. She considered that. It was right as far as it went; one could hardly enter the stopping of a thing that had stopped years ago, on a brass plate, in front of witnesses. She left a space at the foot of the leaf all the same. She had no idea what she expected to put in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:81:"You keep the stone. Somebody must be standing at the place where we began, so that we know it when we come round to it from the other side. And I should like one of us to be outside the whole time, with the mule, where an Arbiter is an Arbiter."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:91:Brom came up, and drove the hooked point of the bill into the turf, and levered out a square of it as big as a plate. He laid it grass downward. It showed black on the frost.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:105:It was the third line of a new table, and it was of another kind from the sixty-three that he had written on a mountain. There a cord and a clock had shown what anybody could check. Here the only instruments were two people's Arbiters, going out and coming back; he had none to offer, and so he held the pencil for the people who had, and it surprised him a little that he did not grudge it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:185:Nothing moved in the whole of it but themselves. It looked like the plainest piece of country in the world, and five people had just walked the edge of it with their Arbiters going out and coming back at every stumble.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:61:"One: it is a thing that happens, and I have called it the Quieting. Two: five sigils of five Paths failed at one stride, in one way, and came back so. Three: the floor is older than the oldest stone that I can date. Four: the registry has put iron on the edge of it." She shut the notebook on her finger. "And there is a hypothesis, which is mine, and which is not entered anywhere."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:29:"That is the finding. Five sigils, of five Paths and three tiers, failed at one stride, in one way, and were restored at that stride, in one way, with nothing spent and nothing lost. It goes into the box as it stands."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:37:"I was frightened at the stone, on the night that I ruled them, and I entered that I was. I have had eleven days to find out why." She put the pen down. "I sat at lectures on what an Arbiter is, and I copied a good many pages in a room where they counted the pages before I left. On one point every page agrees. It is a person's own. It wakes for one person, on one morning, and names one gift; mine is not Lira's, and Brom's is no kin to a healer's, and not one of them has anything to do with another."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:65:*one dark since fourteen; no change either side of the line; stated by him, not tested by me*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:131:She had gone up the ladder behind the lean man with her right hand and both knees, and had stood up on the planks with the whole valley in front of her. There was the stretch, a mile of pale frozen grass between two shoulders of hill. There was the white stone, a hundred and ten yards down it. And forty paces out from the gate somebody had driven a stake, and hung a sack on it that was stuffed with straw, with a ring burnt on the sacking as big as a plate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:339:She could not answer him, and she looked at her right hand instead, which was sore across the palm from an ash stock, and which had put five of eight into a ring the size of a plate from forty paces.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:31:He could feel her keeping time. He had not expected that, of all things: that he would lie under an instrument and be able to tell that it was a person. A brass plate in a district hall had waited over him for eleven seconds, and he had not felt it wait; an assessor's panel had read him across a floor, and he had got nothing from it but a number on a slate. This one limped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:97:He had been waiting for it without knowing that he waited. He had lain under hands before, and under eyes; he knew to the breath when it came. The thing reaching for him would find that he was not what it had been built to hold, and then it would change. A brass plate went dark. A panel's faces shut. A clerk with a form that had no box for him wrote a word in capitals, because he could find no tier to write; and after that there was a file, and a watcher at a gate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:155:He had said no at the first stride of every road for ninety-six days, and again at the second, on a bar of gravel and a hill of plates and a mile of grass with a grey thing pacing it. He had become so good at the refusal that he had taken it for what he was. He had kept the nine as a person keeps a purse shut; and he knew now what the purse had been kept for, and the relief of it was so sharp that it was nearly grief.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:219:It was in the second that his left boot came down on a stone. It was a round thing the size of a loaf, bedded in the rut's floor; he would have had it a stride and a half off with the read, and he had not got the read. The foot turned on it, and the calf took the whole of his weight slantwise, along a seam that a hound had made at a ford and a plate of shale had opened on a mountain.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:283:She had laid them along the line's inside edge. That was what her feet had been finding since first light: the last hand's breadth of ground where a point would hold, so close to the place where an Arbiter stopped that an animal coming out would meet it with its next stride.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:189:There was *silencing*, which she held longest. It was the right sort of word; it was made out of a verb, and a verb was what she had seen. But it claimed too much. To silence a thing was to stop its mouth, and she had copied a great many pages on what an Arbiter was, in a room where the pages were counted before she left it, and not one of them said that it spoke.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:229:There had been the other reason as well, which she liked a good deal less, since it was no part of her discipline. Four people were asleep over her head, behind a wall of barley. One of them had gone up a hill for her with fire in his fist. He had turned leaves for her for two days, and written what she called, and had not once asked her what she thought. She did not suppose that he had failed to wonder. He had been a sentence in other people's boxes since he was fourteen, and she had spent two years watching what that cost.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:253:"It is a thing that happens. It happens to an Arbiter, at an edge, and it goes on happening for as long as that Arbiter is inside the edge; and then it stops, and nothing has been lost. Yours were the best words for that. I have built on them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:81:"He keeps the fire, and the shelves over it. I split a week of wood for him this morning, and he said I could have apples, and asked me which." Brom looked down into the basket with a kind of wonder. "I didn't know there was a which. At home they came up from the kitchen on a plate."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:7:"It came. There are ten. I told her all of it on her table before I was off it: the plate, and what I am, and where each of the nine came from, by its Path. She had Brom fetch the leaf, and she read it." He looked at Seln. "I gave the ninth its Path and no more. I told her that I'd used it once on that hill, and that I had looked to see where her eyes were before I did."

