editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-05.md:67:*Be it known that the Guilds Compact, by its registry, having convened formal proceedings upon the question of the classification designated [SHATTERED] and its bearing upon evaluation integrity within chartered institutions, hereby files a formal challenge to enrollment basis against the said enrollment.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:117:He thought of Denvash, which he did not often let himself do. A district office on a cold morning, a chalk circle on boards worn into two shallow places where a generation of feet had stood, and a warden-adjunct with tired grey eyes three feet away at a desk with a brass plate let into it. Something had woken behind his breastbone, small, a point of light at the edge of his sight that he could feel and never point to. He had counted eleven seconds while the plate did not light, and then the word had come, very far away and very close, and the plate lighting late with the same word in it, and the light at the edge of his sight going out like a pinched wick.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:121:The leaf said there were two things in that office, not one. There was the reading, and there was something appended to it. And there had been eleven seconds.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-46.md:99:"To find a leaf? In the annex, off the shelf, eleven seconds. I had the assistant time me." She pulled a face. "Out of oilcloth, on my knees, the first day: four minutes and some. And I tore a corner. Yesterday, fifty seconds. I want it under thirty, and I want it under thirty with cold hands, so I've had the window open since noon."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md:119:Cael counted it. He could not help counting it. From the moment the pen touched the first column to the moment it lifted from the second, eleven seconds.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md:121:He filed them where the other eleven lived. A circle of pale wood in an office at Denvash, on the morning of his Kindling, and a brass plate at Pellin's elbow that had stayed dark for eleven seconds before it lit, while a fourteen-year-old boy stood in the circle and did not breathe. He had thought then that eleven seconds was the longest any office would ever take to decide what he was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-59.md:271:"You couldn't read me," he told it, conversationally, the way you tell a fact to an old machine. "Since my Kindling I thought that was the system failing. Turns out it was the most honest thing the system ever did — the one time the layer under you couldn't finish its job, because there was no bin beneath me to file the lie in." He looked at the dark frame a moment longer. "You're the only part of the whole apparatus that never lied about me. You just went quiet. Everything above you did the lying." He shouldered the satchel. "Thanks for the eleven seconds. They were the truest measurement I ever got."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-52.md:35:"I stand in it. Within five weeks, at the district station, with Ilsev seeing to the conduct of it, and she'll see to it properly. I put my hand where I'm shown. It was asked once before, and it took eleven seconds." He did not look at Seln, and he went no nearer to it than that. "We know what it says. Karis told a seat so for fifty-one minutes and nobody contradicted her: *no valid classification.* Then the chapter opens, in its order. One: every enrollment or standing that rests on my being a practitioner is void, and that's this hill. Two: I'm suspended as a practitioner pending a review, and the review is to be begun by the registry at its discretion."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:367:He had been read at fourteen, in a room, in eleven seconds, and since that morning there had always been somebody deciding what he was. A warden had done it, and a provost, and a tournament office; a seat had done it under a hood, and a designation had done it that no office would own. Some of them had been kind, and most had been correct, and all of them had been writing in a column that he could not see.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:59:"Advisory, the same word, on a designation nobody at any height has ever been able to trace to an office." Karis drew a breath. "Cael told me on the third night, when he brought the bread. I asked him about Denvash, and he told me: eleven seconds. The plate lit eleven seconds late, and the word came at the end of them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:63:"Eleven seconds is a long time for a reading," said Karis. "Every other reading on the continent is done before you've finished a breath. I think the function had his reading in front of it for eleven seconds and couldn't find a column to put it in, any column, and couldn't take the margin off because it was all margin. So it did what a good form does when it can't be filled in: it failed safe, and wrote the one word it had for a thing that couldn't be written. Then it marked the file, so that whoever came after would know there was something there it hadn't been able to correct." She laid the slip down. "The mark has no office behind it because no office made it. It made itself, the way a stamp comes down when a lever's pulled. And Ilsev sent it up, at the hearing, twice, to every height she could reach, and asked who'd authorized it. Something answered her each time, something that wasn't anybody."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:183:It did not take long. It had taken eleven seconds once, in another town, and a woman behind a desk had said a word, and he had walked out into a different life. This took perhaps as long as it takes to boil an egg.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:271:He had been fourteen. A room at Denvash, a desk, eleven seconds. Karis had laid it out for him on a night of frost, with her gloves on: something that was not the reading had looked for a column to put him in, and had found none, and had done the safe thing. It had written a word where an older name had been. And it had set a watch on him, at that same instant, before a clerk had so much as ruled a sheet with his name at the top.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md:15:He told the circle chalked on the worn boards of a district office, and the warden-adjunct three feet away watching the brass plate let into her desk, and the thing that woke behind his breastbone and came up to the edge of his sight. He told the eleven seconds he had counted while the plate did not light, and the one word that came at the end of them, very far away and very close, and the light at the edge of his sight going out like a pinched wick and never coming back when he looked. He told the letter that had made Denvash impossible for Hesk's sake, and the road east. He told Lira next, because Lira had come next: six weeks of bouts in a yard at Ardenmere, with her knocking him down on the boards between them more times than either of them had counted. Then one cold night on a cot under a window, with the Log on his knee, something had been written in the dark place behind his breastbone where the light had gone out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:111:"And the fourth," said Karis, and her little finger went down. "I asked. I asked the petitioner what evidence would defeat his petition, and a reading in the frame is evidence, and he told me there was none. He did not say, *let us see what the frame returns.* He is too honest a man to say so, and in thirteen sittings he never has. Nobody has. No officer, no clerk, no witness has stood in this room and told the seat that the frame will return a classification. It was asked once before, at a district station, when he was fourteen, and it took eleven seconds to answer, and its answer is the word on his file."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:115:Cael sat very still behind Withrow's chair. He knew what the eleven seconds had been, and so did she. She had read them to him out of a locked case, with a glass, on a night when she wore gloves; and she had just laid them before a seat as a plain fact from a district's record, with nothing behind them, because that was all she was allowed to lay.
editions/monroe-1.3/OWNER-DECISIONS.md:159:## 50. Book 7 — "Six years I've wanted a face" (OWNER ITEM; needed before Book 7 Movement 7)
editions/monroe-1.3/OWNER-DECISIONS.md:160:The protected line gives Seln six years; edition Book 6 has him carry the case twelve years (fifteen in service). **Default until ruled: the line stays verbatim** and the plan stops calling the case a six-year matter. Options: (1) "Twelve years I've wanted a face."; (2) "All these years I've wanted a face."; (3) keep six and name a separate event six years back that began the wish.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/packets/MOVEMENT-007.md:12:> **[Coordinator note 2026-10-11 — "Six years I've wanted a face" and OWNER-pending #50 (#50 default in force), binding.]** Seln's protected council line stays WORD FOR WORD: "…People have faces. Six years I've wanted a face." Edition canon is that he has carried the case TWELVE years (fifteen in service). Until the owner rules, do not gloss the six, do not invent an event six years back, and do not state the case's twelve years in the same scene or have anyone do the arithmetic. Elsewhere in the movement the case is a twelve-year matter, as the packet now says. (The page already has Seln dating the registry's survey of the circles at "five years ago by the stake, maybe six" in ch34; you may leave that echo to the reader, but do not point at it.) Write so that changing the one numeral later would need no other change.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/packets/MOVEMENT-007.md:45:- **The council, the second honest collision:** Lira for walking away (the fence at forty and the walk at fifty, and pace the third circle on their own time); Brom ("Three hundred's a number a man doesn't reach for." — his father and the horse); Karis ("I would go alone."); **Seln: "Take it… People have faces. Six years I've wanted a face."** [OWNER-pending #50] Cael's sentence to the whole table (protected), then why: the assumption is wrong and cannot be known to be wrong. **Brom quotes Rooke**, the first person he has ever quoted: "Doors open both ways… I don't like it. I'm in." Lira: "I want it entered that I said walk away." The log (EXC-B7-002). Pike marks HOLD unasked and says it to the room. **Teague at the door** (protected): "If you're late back, we'll come look." / "Noted.", the first *Noted* to anyone above them on the wall.

