editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-59.md:153:Sixteen years old. Mid-payment. Under every instrument and every pair of eyes that would ever sit in judgment on his life. And met with a hazard nobody had rehearsed him for, he had answered it *smaller*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:25:"Low," he said. "A sixteenth of an inch, against our own stock. I laid the rule on it in the lodge window." He moved the fingers a fraction. "Everything that comes up the hill from the south mill carries the mark the same distance from the deckle. I've put a rule on perhaps four thousand sheets. That one sat a sixteenth low."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:29:"It's in my book," said Bracken. "Under the seal. *Mark a sixteenth low against house stock.* I didn't know what to make of it, so I wrote it down."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:37:A sixteenth of an inch was nothing. It was the width of a pencil line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:39:It was also a whole story, if you knew how paper was made. The mark in a sheet was a wire. Somebody had bent it into a shape and sewn it onto the face of the mould, and when the pulp was lifted on the mould the wire made the paper thinner there, and the thin place showed against the light. A mould lasted for years. A mark, once sewn, stayed where it was sewn. If the mark on a mill's registry stock had moved a sixteenth of an inch, then the wire had broken, or worn through, and somebody had cut it off and sewn it on again by hand. Nobody sews a wire twice in the same place.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-28.md:41:So every sheet with the mark a sixteenth low had come off the mould after the mending. And the mould had been mended this year, or Bracken would have laid his rule on a low mark before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-49.md:89:She knew what kind of thing it was because she was the subject of one. At home in Auremont's archive there was a whole shelf of her, bound in the house's blue, kept by better clerks than this shop would ever afford, and once when she was sixteen she had sat down and read the lot from the first volume to the last to see whether she could find herself in it. She had found her results, very accurately recorded. It had been like looking at a coat on a hook, holding the shape of somebody's shoulders, with nobody inside.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-49.md:95:It never did, anywhere. Every room she had slept in since she was sixteen had a single pane of thick leaded glass, with a small brass plate on the sill to say what it was rated for, and the rating was always her own assessed ceiling and a margin. The program's facilities office ordered them. She had stopped trying latches years ago.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-49.md:115:There was a page in the registry's book of records with her name on every line. She had seen it once, at a ceremony, open on a stand under glass. The top line was the newest: Gold, Rank Three, at nineteen, youngest. Below it, because the page was kept from the bottom up like a well being filled, were the others. Gold at seventeen, youngest; that ceremony had been full of people from houses she had never seen, who shook her hand one after another and told her what it meant. Silver at sixteen. Iron at fourteen. Every line had the same word at its end.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-49.md:147:She folded the sheet and put it back behind the notebook. On one of the notebook's first pages, when she was sixteen, she had written down a word for herself that she had never said aloud.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-49.md:133:"I put you in this room," said Withrow, to the window, "because a person who's named in a scope reads the scope. That's a rule I've kept all my working life, and I'm not going to break it for an Archmarshal." She drank some of her tea. "I didn't put you here to enjoy it. If it's costing you more than a chair should cost a boy of sixteen, you'll tell me, and I'll find you a reason to be somewhere else for the afternoon, and nobody at that table will think twice about it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-49.md:217:He turned the floor sheet face up again, to where the desk clerk had written the sixteenth's hour, and finished the list in the margin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:7:*MOULD MENDED THIS YEAR. WIRE WENT AT THE TOP OF THE MARK. SEWED IT MYSELF. A SIXTEENTH LOW, I KNEW IT WOULD BE, THERE WAS NO HELPING IT. ONE RUN OFF IT SINCE FOR THE REGISTRY. WE CALL IT THE SECOND. PAID IN FULL.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-39.md:181:It took him some time, because there was a good deal to write. Ephram in the round of sixteen, to the lake house's Shield. Lira a semifinalist, to Zerin, four to nine. Karis the winner of her quarterfinal over Ternhall's best, and then a semifinalist, to the lattice-breaker, five to nine. And at the bottom, in Rooke's big capitals, Brom: *Continental Copper.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md:97:On the sixteenth day Cael took his sheet for the next Fifth-day down to the wing, because the provision's hours were lodged at the counter or nowhere, and the registry did not care what week it was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-09.md:35:"Sixteen," said Lira, under her breath. "If I win every one. If they let me call out every window." She handed the book back. "Thank you."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-09.md:39:"You could have signed a week ago," said Cael. "Same foot, same sixteen."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-09.md:91:So if those fingers closed, it would not exist, and the lean would land on a boy of sixteen with good feet, and the boy would go where he was turned. That was the doctrine's arithmetic, and it was simple. He must not be held at all, so he went on refusing, and kept a little more distance than before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-48.md:7:"Sixteenth," said Cael. "Hall three, the fourth bell, supervised."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-48.md:9:"Sixteenth." The desk clerk took the slip, checked the line against the ledger, and began to write. "Same as ever."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-02.md:149:"We filed in week sixteen," she said. "Brom and Karis and me. Transfers. Did you think we'd let you walk four days to a new gate on your own and find out from a letter which of us had come?" She speared a piece of turnip. "Karis wrote to the registrar. He sent the forms in the fourteenth letter. He said he'd assumed."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-04.md:213:The letter went behind the front board of the observation notebook with the other two that lived there: the folded note that had come in the pine case with the pen, on the eleventh of Sowing, and the sheet that had said *Sixteen suits you.* Three now, in the same hand. He sat with the notebook shut on all three before he put it away, and did not write in the Log what it cost him to have them there together, because some lines in a ledger were better left as a space.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-33.md:161:Ephram's round of sixteen was the third floor's last bout on the afternoon of the sixth day, and the lake house's Shield did exactly what Rooke had said she would.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-33.md:209:"That was the price of admission," he said. "I said so at the draw. The round of sixteen was what I'd pay to get in." He started to unwrap his hands. "Paid in full. I'd like a receipt."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-43.md:39:There was no limp, quite. There was a boy walking as a man walks across a room he knows has a loose board in it, keeping his weight on one side and not letting anybody see him keep it. Vastin had seen a great many people walk like that into a great many rooms, and very few of them had been sixteen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-42.md:5:Cael saw it from the door. He had come down with her before the first bell, as he had every morning that week, and stood where he had stood sixteen days before, with his shoulder against the frame and the two lamps burning over the painted floor. The program was six people in a hall built for sixty. The Force was working the heavy frame at the far end with his broad back to everybody, and the Current girl was walking a line of white crosses on her toes, and the program's clerk sat at her trestle under the first lamp with the red book open and a mug steaming beside it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-42.md:35:Cael stayed at the door for the rest of the hour and watched her walk the count: three lies and a truth, the front foot, the back, the shoulders, and on the fourth a step where the burst would have been. The Stone stood on his crosses and moved when she made him move. She was slow, and a little stiff through the hips from sixteen days of benches, and she knew the drill so well that her hands kept coming up for a burst she was not allowed to spend.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:251:"I have read paper all year for what stood behind it," he said. "A mark a sixteenth low. A lot written above the ruled line, with a mark for a word. A seal that is on no schedule. I have been reading paper that way for a good while longer than a year." He touched the leaf's corner. "This is registry stock from the first mould, and the mark sits where it should. The die has its number in the rim, and the number will be on its line in the cutters' ledger; I should be surprised if it were not. The hand is the seat's own clerk's. I have watched him write it since the fortieth day."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-30.md:295:There was a likely meeting in the round of sixteen with the lake house's methodical Shield, and nothing of his own house anywhere in his quarter of the draw before the semifinals at the earliest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-30.md:299:He looked at it a moment more, and then went on in the voice he used for reading out a bill. "The round of sixteen's what I'm paying to get in. Anything after that, I'm trading on."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-47.md:105:A Silver from one of the eastern houses had come down to the frame that afternoon, long after the register's hour. He was a Five, a big quiet man who had gone out of his bracket in the round of sixteen and had nothing left at the sitting but his number. He had walked up to it with a filing form in his hand, filled in, to be ready for the morning. Half the tunnel had seen it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-20.md:49:"It's six. From twenty-second, if you win every bout you're allowed and every one counts, it's six to the top. Not sixteen. You counted from the ladder book. The ladder book was the wrong book."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-20.md:231:There was one Iron draw this time, sixteen strong, with no split at any rank. It was seeded by formal rank, as the circuit seeded everything, with the foot of the list set against its head. Lira was not at the foot. A host-house Rank One, advanced at the last sitting before the year's turn, had been registered after her, and so stood below her. He drew the top seed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-40.md:167:"One thing more," said Withrow, "and I'd sooner it came from me than from a passage. I took a provision enrollment into this academy. I took advice before I did it, and I read the law myself, twice, and I think the basis is sound. I still think so. If the delegation rules otherwise, it's my ruling they're overturning, not anybody else's, and I'll stand at the front of this hall and tell you so with my own mouth." She looked once along the tiers, slowly, from one side to the other. "Nobody else here carries my decision. Least of all a sixteen-year-old."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-32.md:113:The Copper bracket would run on into the second week, its quarterfinals and its semifinals, and its final before the rest day. Brom was in its last sixteen. Above him on the board, three lines up, stood Marek's name, with a chalk tick beside it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-32.md:167:"Then Karis did the reading," said Rooke, "and you did the paying, and between you it worked. Write that down as well." He sat. "Round of sixteen. The lake house's Shield. We'll talk about her tomorrow."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-03.md:3:The training hall had been swept twice before the first bell, once by the second-years whose turn it was and once by Hobb, who had not been asked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-37.md:15:She stood square in front of the long board with her hands clasped behind her back, the way she had stood at her chalk before the fifteenth session, and she was looking not at her own name but at the top line, where Lira's was. The brass stud sat a line below it, beside *Fiske*, polished that morning; the porter did it on Fourth-days, and today was not a Fourth-day, so somebody else had done it. The date of the final was chalked very small in the corner. Sixteen days to the final, and the semester evaluation the day after it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-37.md:159:*Sixteen. Your grandmother held you the day you were born and said you had watchful eyes. She was right about everything else, too. Write it all down. — H.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-37.md:185:Cael sat with his back against the wall, with Hesk's pen in his breast pocket and Karis's chart folded inside his coat, and watched the good lamp turn the long scarred table into a place where people lived. Somebody who was not Hesk, in a house nowhere near Fen Street, had kept his day for him, and until tonight he had not known that such a thing could happen. He had turned sixteen at some hour before dawn, and gone through a whole day of lectures and floor sheets without its once seeming to matter. Then three people had decided that it mattered, and gone to some trouble about it. There was no column in any book he kept for what that was worth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-37.md:223:*Sixteen. Six fragments. The Compact's best watcher watching out for me instead, and no way to thank him for it that wouldn't burn him. Semester evaluation in seventeen days. Whatever the system sends to watch it — we're ready. We built ready.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-57.md:185:He did it under the records-hall porch, out of the rain, with the list on a board against his forearm and the chests set down in a row in front of him on the flags. There were four of them, and they had come up the bluff road under four seals apiece, and they would go down it under four new ones, pressed that morning at the delegation's working table in the counsel's wax and the senior seat's. His list had sixteen lines, one for each seal, and beside each line the impression's number and the hand that had pressed it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-57.md:189:It did not matter that he had watched every one of them pressed. He had watched, and he had written the numbers as they were pressed, and now he was checking the wax against what he had written. Nobody would ever check after him: the chests would go into the registry's vault at the end of the road, and a vault clerk would sign for four chests, not sixteen seals. The check was his and nobody else's, and so he did it slowly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-57.md:209:He went down the list. Chest three. Chest four. Sixteen ticks.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-57.md:213:"Sixteen?" said Bracken.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-57.md:215:"Sixteen."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-54.md:261:At the rail by the west wall Cael sat with his palms on the cold wood. Two minutes went on hunting down his edges and setting each one back where an ordinary boy of sixteen would wear it. Grudgingly they came, using up nearly the whole allowance, and left behind them an ache behind the eyes like the one the read sent after a long day. An hour and a half still to go: a poor sign.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-21.md:25:It gave a Copper name a bout about once in six days, when the pairings fell kindly. From the posting to the final was a little over a hundred days. Seventeen bouts at the very most, then, and the first of the seventeen was the drawn bout, which counted in the record and moved nobody on the sheet. Sixteen that could move a name. And that was for a name that was never injured, never deferred, never left standing on its afternoon without anybody to fight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-21.md:29:She had sixteen bouts.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md:9:*An Archmarshal looked at you and wrote 'keep looking.' In my day that was called respect. Don't tell him I said so. Sixteen suits you.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-01.md:43:Outside, through the high window, he could see a slice of the gate and the end of the training hall. The man on the bench by the training hall door was there, as he was there every afternoon, with his hands on his knees and his hat on the bench beside him. He was one of five, and like the other four he did not trouble to hide; nobody had hidden since the ruling, and somewhere around the second week of the recess Cael had stopped flinching at it, which he counted as progress.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-01.md:123:*Exhibition bout, full sanction. Faculty supervised, on the record. Presiding: Instructor Wray. Second seat: Assessor Quenna. Eight exchanges on the instructor's count, standings rules. Week twenty, first bell, the training hall.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-01.md:233:She wrote until the lamp guttered and she had to trim it, and then wrote on. In the morning Cael carried the letter down the hill in the inside pocket of his coat, past the man on the bench by the training hall door and the man at the gate, who both watched him go and neither of whom did anything about it. He gave it across the draper's counter to wait for the next coach.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-47.md:195:*Shadow-adjacent, Bronze tier: working ledger. Day sixteen of the ledger; twenty-nine since the stair. Deployment: none.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-01.md:129:*I'm sixteen. Everybody else in this house knows which box the season puts them in. Lira has a bracket. Brom has a bracket. Karis has a bracket. I have a provision and a clause.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-25.md:57:*EPHRAM. The round of sixteen. And: trial caller.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-44.md:245:Cael sat with the book on his knees for the last of the afternoon, while the light went round on the water, and thought about sixteen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-44.md:247:Sixteen had been at Halcenvane, at the end of an ordinary long day, at the house's long table under a good lamp. Three people had decided that the day mattered, and gone to some trouble over it, and he had not known before that night that anybody but Hesk could keep his day for him. That had been the first one, which proved the day could be kept.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-51.md:229:The pen at the table moved after every line. Brom of Halcenvane, Copper bracket, champion of the continent. Lira of Halcenvane, Iron bracket, semifinalist; and a murmur ran along the line at her name, and Lira stood very straight and pretended she had not heard it. Karis of Halcenvane, Iron bracket, a quarterfinal won over Ternhall's first entry. Ephram of Halcenvane, Iron bracket, round of sixteen. And the trial: a semifinal lost on the whole ledger to Auremont, which the office's own summary called, in one dry clause that made Rooke close his eyes, *the closest semifinal on the trial's record*; and third place, won from Rhagen Institute, three exchanges to one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-10.md:205:The meet was held in the mill guild's training hall. It stood at the top of the town above the largest of the mills, a long building of dressed stone with a slate roof, and inside it was one great room. That was the first thing Cael saw when he walked in. There were no pens and no open roofs and no sheep-market cobbles. There was a level floor of hard boards, laid true, under a high timber roof, and a long iron crane-beam running the length of the room overhead on its tracks, where the guild had once lifted millstones for dressing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/STATE_LEDGER.md:12:- **Book 4 runs:** the final Greyvane term (≈5 months; Karis's find in week 3, the Edran rematch in week 20, Quenna in week 21, Prynn in week 22) → four days on the road → arrival at Halcenvane (five months and nine days after the ruling) → the season → **the eleventh of Sowing (Cael turns sixteen)** → the inspection, **the twelfth to the twenty-third of Reaping** (Copper final the 19th; semester evaluation the 20th; interview the 21st; departure the 23rd; file returned the 24th; quarterly drafted the 26th; corridor warning the 27th) → the first week of the month after Reaping (Lira's Arbiter evaluation) → term close → twelve-week recess (Book 5).
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/STATE_LEDGER.md:308:- **Seeding half-sheet (flag).** Returners go first, in last season's closing order (21 Copper returners entered); new names go next by formal rank, then by signing order. Lira's Rank Two sets her 22nd. This reconciles M2's "the foot" / "sixty-fifth line" / "Sixteen" with the packet's twenty-second. Lira: "counted from the wrong book."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/STATE_LEDGER.md:740:| **d178 (Sixth-day)** | **the fourteenth** | Facility review. Morning: the delegation in the wing; **three questions to Gault**. Fourth bell: **hall three**, seven on the gallery, Ilsev's four questions, 54/60, "Read's off". Ledger day sixteen in the bearer yard. Library: Karis's question counts. Sixth bell: the wing's counter; **the case returned unmarked**; Gault tells Cael. Lira and the beam. After supper: **the taxonomy** on the top tier; the porter locks at the eighth. The registry's return comes up by the delegation's courier at the eighth bell; Havel files it at the back of the correspondence |
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/STATE_LEDGER.md:770:- Cael's pages: the clerks; the counsel; Ilsev ("Exactly the person the papers describe"); Havel (gratitude settled; Lira's capitals); the eleventh ("stood still"); the taxonomy; working ledger day sixteen; the numbered Log.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/STATE_LEDGER.md:935:| d187 | First-day | the 23rd | Departure in light rain; Ilsev at the steps; Havel's sixteen seals; Bracken countersigns. Lira's four days off the hip end (fourth bell); Cael's three days end that night. The Log ("Confused systems ask. This one checks.") |
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/STATE_LEDGER.md:815:- **The draw (flag):** sixteen; a host-house R1 at the foot; Lira at the second seed's line (a coastal Mire, R8); the builder (the hill-river house, Force, R7) at the third seed, with a chalked square; the top seed the river academy's first entry (a tall heavy Stone), beaten by Ephram on the figures in the QF. **The confluence fights no bout for third** (both beaten semifinalists stand third). Brom heads a Copper draw of eight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/STATE_LEDGER.md:894:Standings after T4: Lira, Karis and Ephram in the Iron round of sixteen (separate pools for Lira and Karis, meeting only in a final; Ephram's quarter holds neither of them before the semifinals); Brom in the Copper's last sixteen; Cael's exhibition frame has two Silver filings (the Rhagen Shield captain, Silver 6, *Doctrinal interest.*; the lake-house Blade duelist, Silver 4, *The crowd.*), both given the senior exhibition panel; Rhagen's filing countersigned by its delegation head.

