editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-08.md:221:"It doesn't shelve," he said. "It stops. There's a wall of rock under that lip, straight down, and I can't find the floor."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-59.md:113:The pencil on the table had begun its slow journey toward the wall. He watched it go as far as the folded card and stop.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-59.md:145:He had been watching people meet the thing they did not expect for the whole of his working life. A Stone officer in a collapsing gallery who threw up a wall so thick he brought down the roof he was holding off. A Current girl at a qualifying sitting who answered a stumble by pouring out twice her measured strength and burned her own hands on it. A magistrate's guard on a quay at night who met a stranger's sudden movement with a blow that would have stopped a horse, and the stranger only reaching for his papers. Every tier, every service, every age. They gave it more. Surprise came at them like a wall, and they hit the wall harder. It was the steadiest thing he knew about people under strain, steadier than anything in any manual, and he had never set it down anywhere, because something that every man does tells you nothing about any one man.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-49.md:139:There was no slip in her hand; the slips were in the wallet on the table, twenty-five in one bundle and five in the other. But he had heard her say the close three times through, and three times she had stopped after this part, and looked at nothing, and gone on. He had counted the pause in the east hall, and it was four seconds long. She stopped now, and looked at the grey shape above her for four seconds.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-49.md:11:The chart was on the wall before she had stopped breathing hard. The design staff were quick and honest and the chart was both; she stood in front of it with a towel round her neck and read their account of what she had just done, and found that every line of it was true and that it had missed the only part that mattered.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-08.md:155:By then Ephram had a page to himself in the notebook and was well into a second. He had placed him on the Iron board the first afternoon and checked him against the registrar's table on the second: Blade Path, Iron-tier, Rank Six, at the top of the Iron column, which at Halcenvane was the same as saying he was the best competitor on the bluff. Fourteen of the hill's students were Silver and one fellow was Gold, yet the cohort spoke of none of them the way it spoke of Ephram, because no column in the yard carried their names. Here, *best* meant a place on a wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-08.md:169:Ephram came off the wall with his weight forward and stopped two paces out. He sold a strike at the left shoulder, hips turning, the back foot pushing honestly into the oak, and Brom's read took it; Cael saw Brom's shoulders begin to settle into the turn. Ephram was already gone from it. He had pulled the strike back into his own centre before it reached the halfway mark, and stepped a hand to the right, and sold another at the same shoulder from the new angle. Brom's read took that too, because it was honest weight again, and Brom was a man who believed honest weight. The third sale got no turn at all. Brom held, and held, and the strike did not come, and in the instant he let himself believe it would not come at all, it came: full, clean, a flat hand laid along his ribs on the side he had just stopped guarding.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:85:Karis came out along the wall at the second bell after dusk, which was when she always came when she came at all, with her coat buttoned wrong and a volume of the code under her arm and her hair coming down at the back. She stopped beside Brom, and looked at the three of them, and saw at once what they were talking about, because Karis always could.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-38.md:99:"Two," said Karis, "and you can have the half back afterward with interest." She turned and went back along the wall the way she had come, with the code under her arm, toward the lit window at the top of the library.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-18.md:175:Not into carelessness; never that. They slipped by small degrees, and the degrees followed the clock. Three hours into the wing's day, he was still on the best view, but on the edge of his cluster now instead of in its middle. Four hours in, he would sometimes take the place beside the best one: the top of a stair from which most of a floor could be seen, when the rail beside it would have shown him all. And twice, at the end of days when the wing had kept its counter open from first bell to last, he had stood where no watcher stands: in the angle of two walls in the yard's north stair, with a single doorway in front of him, seeing almost nothing of the floor.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:167:The noise was on both sides of them, a river in flood going by either end of a pier. Plates were coming over the top of the wall in the middle and bursting on the floor.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:249:The rock was a gift. It was dry under its roof, and rimed along the outer half where the roof stopped, so she kept in, close under the wall, as she had promised a fire that she would. Her left foot struck and her right came after it. She had the count in her legs. Forty. Fifty. The gorge went by on her right hand, blue and enormous, and she gave it none of her attention; she was reading the shelf five yards ahead of her toes, for cracks and for colour, as she had read a sprung floor.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:309:Twenty yards. The near arm of the fire came down the slope ahead of her and stopped a little beyond the wall's end, and on this side of it, on her side, the western stream was coming.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:329:She could not have said how long it was before the boy came. He came along the lower side of the rock, on his feet and hardly so, with one arm shut against his ribs and his left leg dragging; and he was the colour of the dust. He got down beside her by letting the wall take him. His eyes went over her a piece at a time, as they always did. They reached the plate, and stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-39.md:85:He found her alone under the lamps at the long east wall, in front of the Iron quarterfinals. Her hands hung at her sides. He stopped at the edge of the court, because somebody else was already crossing the stone toward her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-39.md:227:He went down the top stair behind Lira, at her pace, which was faster tonight than it had been. She went down standing now, one step at a time, with her hand on the wall and her right foot set down carefully but set down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-38.md:167:Nine of them stood in a loose line under the lecture range's wall, in coats, and counted the people who looked up at the records hall's windows in the space of a quarter of an hour. Gwen counted forty-one and wrote a large *41* with an eye drawn in the loop of the four, and Cael counted thirty-eight and wrote thirty-eight. Seln took the slates and made a column of the figures in the round copying hand. Then he said, in his flat voice, that the yard had been looking at a building because it believed the building had news in it, and that this was the commonest error in the trade, since buildings rarely had news in them and people nearly always did. Then he dismissed them, and his eyes passed along the line and did not stop anywhere in particular, and Cael had stopped expecting them to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:87:The keeper is invisible. He had been invisible for fifteen years. The boy's people had never been invisible for a day. All year they had walked about the hill in the open and let themselves be counted, the girl on the walls with her chalk and the big one at the frames and the researcher with her lamp lit in the top window every night where the whole valley could see it. Five days ago, at the gate, the house itself had stood in the middle of its own court and read the paper out loud. Nobody on that side had hidden anything. They had simply been there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-29.md:191:He found Cael on the north wall, because it was where Cael always was at that hour, and stood beside him in the rain without an umbrella and gave him the glass. Cael found the bridge. The bridge-ward was at the near end in his oilskin, with a man from the town, hauling a chain across the roadway from post to post. Below them the river was standing up against the middle pier in a long brown hump, and the iron rings let into the stone were gone under it, all but the top two.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-28.md:149:Karis came out to the wall that evening with her own log, which she had kept since the bridge in a narrow book with marbled boards: every change at the ferry and the road's foot that any of the four of them had seen, the hour, the faces where faces could be told apart, a tick for on the bell and a small cross for late. She laid it open on the coping between them and ran her finger down a month of columns. Four late changes in a month, all four by under ten minutes, all four with a reason somebody had seen: a pie, a cart in the road, a dog, and once, memorably, a man who had stopped to help an old woman with a basket of eels.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-12.md:117:It made three shelves in a row, as tall as her shoulders when she sat, with no wall behind them and a barn all round; and a hen walked along the top of the middle one and was removed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md:197:Karis did not come down to watch. Cael looked up at the top of the library, at the reading room's window, and she was not in it. She had gone to sleep in her chair the moment the wallet left the counter, the girl at the outer table told him afterward, with her boots still on and her key on its cord in her fist.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md:207:The north wall was warm under him from the day's sun, and the valley was going blue, when Lira came along the top of it from the east end.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-09.md:247:She went off along the top of the wall toward the residence, quick and narrow in the dusk, and he sat with the rest of his bread and watched her go until she dropped off the coping at the far end and was gone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-53.md:81:He held up his bound forearms. "Everything I ever stopped on these came at me pushing off something. A foot. A wall. The floor. You can't turn in nothing." He lowered them. "She turned. Twice. A little, at the north-west end, and coming in at Karis. Your pea doesn't."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:61:"Everyone I've ever had my hands in, this is the part where I know." She said it as a finding about herself. "Iron Skin, you feel the banks first — dense, walled, everything held. Ember, the banks are a fire's, hot at the edge. Wind you barely feel the banks at all, the water's too fast. But there's always a bank. It's how you know which river." A pause, long, her current going along Ember and stopping at the ignition point and lying there as if it had found a stone in a bed. "I can't find one here. I keep putting my hand where the bank ought to be and there's more water."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-27.md:15:He had sat behind the bell-frame for that. He had counted it as it came back off the walls. Eighty-one words, and his registry name at the top of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:63:Above the wall, in the dark, a plate let go by itself and slid, and stopped; and a little later another.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:157:The clinking stopped beyond the wall's end. Somebody said one word to an animal; and then a woman was standing at the corner of the rock, a stride and a half from where Lira lay, with a cheek-strap in her fist and a brown mule's long head at her shoulder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-50.md:181:He waited. He did not count, for once. The mother sat with the basket and looked at the door. The coat, forty yards back, had stopped by a garden wall and was looking at a row of beans. He had seen a thousand stations, and this one had nothing in it for his column.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:307:They split on it. The foremost flung left and the next flung right, and the rest went after one or the other, as thirty head of horn had gone on a mountain. They ran along the inside of that wall to its two ends. Then they were over the line, somewhere in the dark beyond the light, to the north and to the south; and the last that he knew of any of them was a rushing in the grass that went away along the line's outside in both directions, and thinned, and stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:7:Beyond the stone the slope went up into the dark. At the top of it there was still a faint paleness where the floor lay. Between that and the lamp, along sixty yards of grass, ran a strip of black no wider than his hand, where a wall had stood for a count of six; the frost was coming down on it already.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-07.md:227:Brom had not come over, and sat at their own boards with the jug in front of him, looking up the wall with his lips moving. He started at the bottom and went to the top. Then he did it again, from the top down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:109:Pike had his place on the hearth bench, and he did not sit in it, for he had brought in a roll of sized sailcloth from somewhere, and a boy to hold it. He unpinned the old sheet from the plaster and pinned up a clean length in its place; and then he stood before it with his pen and wrote out the whole wall again from the top, by hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:255:The frost had heaved the top course of the lining at the thaw, and six stones had gone in, and he felt for them with his feet. He stooped for each one, to the shoulder in that water, and got it into the loop, and jerked the rope twice, and it went up past him, dripping, toward the coin of light. He brought up the bucket, and a length of chain; and he brought up something else, which had gone in after water a long while ago and was past being anything, and he sent that up with his face turned to the wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:345:It was the bowman's ash, and the fork at its top had gone pale and smooth where her palm sat. She turned it over once when Pike had done, and looked at it, and then she walked to the inn wall, beside the left-hand post, and stood the stick against the plaster; she set it upright, with some care, as one leaves a borrowed tool where the next person will want it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:9:"With a cart on top of it. I'd sooner say so here than have you find it out from the wall."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-11.md:51:Withrow did not answer at once. She looked past him at the charter on the wall, in its frame, with the glass a little dusty along the top edge where the porters' cloths did not reach.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:95:They slept that night at the top, on the end of the shelf, because she would not have three boxes left alone on a mountain and nobody cared to be the one to tell her that there was no one within a day's walk to steal them. It was too dark by then to see what they had climbed to. He lay with his back against the third box and heard the stream a long way down on his right hand, and a second stream, fainter, that was only the first one coming back from the wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:149:It was a road, or the stone that a road might have been made on. It ran west from the top of the fan along the wall of the gorge, as level from end to end as a shelf in a shop, thirty feet broad where they stood; and no hand, by the look of it, had ever cut it. The mountain rose on the left in a long grey slope of plates, steep as a roof, to a skyline that he had to lean back to find. On the right there was an edge, and beyond the edge blue air, and the far wall three hundred yards off with a white thread of water hanging on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:243:He saw her stop, sixty yards off. She stood quite still for as long as it would have taken to count ten. Then she set no stone on its end; she turned, and walked back toward them along the inside of the shelf, slowly, close under the wall, and her feet made no sound on the rock.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-16.md:133:The girl shut the door. "At the end she said *thank you* in a very small voice and went away. Before that she kept looking at the stair." She swallowed. "Before that, I'd told her about the ninety-one. And the paragraph numbers. And that the next sitting's on the Fifth-day, and that the petitioner's conceded the provision is valid and doesn't allege fraud, and that the Compact's own inspection looked at the provision and it stood." She was going backward through it as Karis had told her to, and her voice steadied as she went. "And at the start I told her he's enrolled under a lawful provision, adjudicated at Greyvane on the public record, under seal, with the grant and two renewals on paper. Because she asked me what the enrollee was *really* like. A Mire girl from the north stair, on the covered walk, after supper. I hardly know her." She stopped. "That was the beginning."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-17.md:85:The west wall came up behind his heels. He felt it before he saw it, the change in the air at his back, and with it something else: the gallery was directly above him now. He could hear the old man's stool creak. If he looked up he would be looking straight into four faces, and they would be looking straight down at the top of his head.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-17.md:175:Rooke came along the wall below them on his way to the gate lodge, and stopped, and looked up, which he did not usually do. He had heard. Cael could tell from his face that he had heard, and that he had expected to hear it, and that he had come along the wall at that hour so that he would.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-48.md:107:Cael came along the rim on his way to the wing with a floor sheet he no longer needed to lodge, out of a habit that had outlived its use, and stopped at the rail. The oak lay four feet below him in the hole somebody had dug for it, sixty feet by forty, pale and dry, with the stone tiers going up empty on three sides. On the fourth side the porter's four black boards stood on their wall above the faculty stand, chalked with the year's names. Ephram's was at the head of the Iron column, where it had stood since before Cael came up the road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:3:Pike wrote the wall out again that evening, from the top, on a clean length of sailcloth, and Lira counted the lines as they went up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:121:He looked at the table. It was bare again already, and somebody had swilled it down; it would stand so for twenty days, scrubbed, with its bench against the wall, and on the twentieth there would be people sitting there before it was light. There were six more like it. There was a child with a chest, three stops on, and a carter with his hand in a rag.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-48.md:189:She had been talking easily while Bracken laid things out, about the charter's binding and the age of the tab. Now she stopped in the middle of a word and read. She read the sheet, and then the two copies at each place the sheet pointed to, and then the sheet again, while Cael, on his hard chair against the wall, counted her breaths without moving his lips, the way he counted feeds.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:99:Brom had the two record cases on his knees, one on top of the other, with his arms round them. He had not let them ride on the roof on the day of the exhibits and he was not going to let them ride on the roof today, in mud. Karis sat beside him with the attestation wallet in her lap and her lips moving. She was saying the form over, the questions the seat's clerk would ask the maker and the enrollee and the advocate, and the answers, under her breath, the way Lira counted her bursts before a bout. Lira was asleep against the window with her coat over her like a blanket and her mouth a little open. She could sleep anywhere in a moving vehicle. She said it was the only useful thing she had learned from guilds.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:107:"I'm saying it so my mouth knows it before the room does." Karis did not stop turning the wallet's leaves. "The clerk asks the maker whether the copies are his and true. Then he asks the enrollee whether the figures are his own, earned on the house's floors. Then he asks the advocate to sign." She looked across at Cael. "That's all it is. Three questions and a pen. You say *They are mine*, and nothing else, and you sit down."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:129:It was a flat chain ferry, broad enough for one coach and its team and nothing else. A great chain ran from an iron ring sunk in the rock on the near side, down under the water, and up again to a winch-house on the far bank, and the ferry hauled itself along the chain by a wheel turned from its deck. The river came through the narrows deep and fast and brown and very loud. But it could not spread. There was no width for it to throw anything about in. It simply went through, as water goes through a pipe, and the ferry sat on top of it on its chain like a cat on a wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-30.md:245:Lira got down at the coach's tail and stood with her weight on the balls of her feet, looking at the four men by the ditch and the parapet, at their feet, not their eyes. Karis came out last, on the north side, and went to the near horse's head. The horse was standing very still. Too still. Cael saw Karis put out her hand toward its foreleg and stop, a hand's breadth short, as if she had touched a wall in the dark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-25.md:87:The Silver was walking it too, from the other end, with his hands behind his back and his guild sash over his shoulder instead of knotted at his hip. He was not fighting again; the exhibitions were done. He stopped when he saw her, and she stopped, and they stood in the corridor between the stacked trestles, a bargeman and a Wind, with the river going past behind the wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-25.md:165:Cael wrote up the meet in the coach, on his knee, while the rain ran down the window and Brom slept and Lira, opposite, slept too, with her head against the window frame and one hand inside her coat. Karis had the fourth sitting's papers open and the production order's duplicate pinned to the top of them with a clip. Every so often she took the plain card out of the wallet's back and looked at it, and put it away again. Seln, in his corner, was awake for once, with his eyes on the wet fields going by. Cael did not ask him what he had seen from the loft that he had not said at supper. He had learned two years ago that the only way to get anything from Seln was to leave a space beside him and wait to see if anything came and sat in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-43.md:77:He did not know what the boy was. He noticed, walking back toward the outer wall through the crowd, that he had not written that down either. It was not a finding. It was only the place where the findings stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-43.md:153:He stopped at the end of the row and counted it. Thirty-one people, standing in a line along the wall of a shut office, most of them with a folded paper in their hands. He knew what the papers were. They were result slips from the Concourse. The tournament made ranks, and the registry wrote them down, and until the registry had written them down they were only a story somebody had told about you in a crowded room. These people had come a day early to stand at a shut door so that they would be first when it opened.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-03.md:179:He let himself be walked back six strides, seven, until the south wall was a stride behind his own heels and the Shield had stopped walking and was setting himself for the last of it. A man setting himself puts his weight down, and a man who has just put his weight down cannot move it again for half a beat, however good he is. The read gave Cael the half-beat, and he spent the second spark in it, at the edge of the plane again, on the knuckles.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-37.md:121:He told it the way he had written it, standing against a wall under a lamp in the covered walk with the notebook held to the stone at the third hour. The archive at Halcenvane on a bad night two years ago, near enough, in the first year he was on the hill. The fire-watch gone the long way round. A man with a bag at the top of the upper flight, in the doorway, with his gloved hand already going into a press he had no business opening. And on the landing above him, in the light, the wing's assistant. He was reading the man a form, one plain line after another, about copying and paper and what a magistrate counted in years, until the man's hand came back out of the press with nothing in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:239:A gully came down out of the right-hand hill, and at its foot the floods of other years had spread a fan of pale stones clean across the valley, a hundred feet wide, with a skin of water shining on it. The ruts ran straight over. The mule's feet rattled in the wet, and the cart came after with a noise like a sack of nails. Out on the right the grey shape came to the edge of the fan and stopped as if it had met a wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-36.md:169:It came back four times more before the hour was up. Four more times it went the moment he reached to check. By the end of the hour Karis had five marks under *NOT* and five little marks beside them, and the sameness of the rows on the wall had stopped being maddening and started being a kind of answer.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-36.md:155:"My fourth posting, twelve years ago." Seln put one hand back on the table, as if the wood steadied him. "A district seat in an old building, a records room on its top floor with presses round the walls, the kind with doors. I was looking for a subject's travel endorsements. They were filed where they should not have been, so I opened presses until I found them, and in the fourth press I found these." He moved his eyes over the rows. "The orders were in bound runs, a few years to a volume, with the records bundled behind them, and the memoranda on the bottom shelf in a box. And at the back of the box, loose, under the oldest, that." He did not look at the leaf. "Nobody had opened the press in years; the dust said so."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-56.md:11:He walked her to the door, because he had nothing else on the hill to do, and it was a strange thing to find out about himself at the third bell of a working day. For two years he had always been due somewhere: at a trestle in wing three, or a table at the top of the library, or the north wall at the changing of the coats. Now his name stood in a book with five words against it, and nobody on the bluff was expecting him anywhere.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:137:She unrolled the paper on the canvas and laid the brass along its top edge to hold it. It was a straightedge, an old one, which she had had from Ternhall. Cael knew the sheet before the firelight had found the whole of it, for it had hung on her reading-room wall on the first day of the year, and she had taken it down in the first week to make room for a code.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-60.md:167:Lira had her own book out by then, and had turned to the page with the capitals. *4 LOADED. 3 LIGHT. STAMPS STOP. 2 GO ON. 1 TURNS.* She had chalked that on a dairy wall once, and copied it out afterward, and carried it through eleven sittings.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-26.md:235:Above it the bluff stood up dark against the morning, with the house on top of it, and the wall where Lira had sat a few nights before, looking north, in the starlight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-20.md:19:He let the read go all the way out and come all the way back, without stopping it anywhere short to look like timing. It brought him Lira's weight half a beat early, as it always did, and in lamplight half a beat was a long time. He used the Wind the way it wanted to be used and not the way the house's book had it. One burst, two, the second off the landing of the first; a third, low, along the wall; a fourth to take him round her lie on the four-count before her fourth arrived. On the boards in wing three he stopped at four, and never let a fifth show. Here he went to five without thinking about it, and then to six, and felt the old easy looseness come into his legs that he had not let himself feel on a floor in front of anybody since Norhold.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-57.md:133:He walked it in the dark at the pace he had taught, which was not fast, and never stopped where there was a wall. The satchel knocked against his hip at each step. He had carried the thing inside it for twelve years, and had never before carried it at night, on a road, toward anybody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-05.md:17:At the gate the lodge-man came out of his door and stood as straight as he stood for the Chancellor's carriage, and for nothing else. Riders on registry business did not stop for lodge-men. They passed under the arch at the same walk, still in the saddle, and the lodge-man stepped back against the wall with his hat in his hand and let them. They crossed the outer court with their hooves loud on the stones in the heat, and every head on the covered walk turned to follow them, and they went on round the corner of the lecture range toward the records hall as if nobody on the hill had a right to stop them, which was true.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-31.md:35:He hit something in the air in front of his chest. It was not a wall, because a wall would have had an edge, and a surface, and a feel. It was a single point, about the height of his breastbone, and it did not give at all. The burst went into it and stopped there, all of it, the way a cart stops when its wheel finds a post in the dark. He went back off it onto his heels. His breath went out of him. The landing beat locked his legs where they stood, the same short pause it had always held him in, and for that pause he could do nothing but stand on the chalk with the point six inches in front of him and know it was there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-23.md:27:Karis drafted in the coach. She had the fourth sitting's papers in a document wallet on her knees and a board across the wallet. She wrote on the board in pencil when the road was rough and in ink when it was smooth, and once, on a long straight stretch above the water meadows, she stopped drafting altogether and took a plain card out of the wallet's back.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-51.md:195:What he felt was not the floor going. It was a corridor ending. He had been walking down it so long that he had stopped feeling the walls, and now there was a last door, with a year's close chalked on it, and behind him nothing whatever that he needed to go back for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-53.md:225:They were not empty now. He counted by tens and by rows, as he had counted benches in a wool exchange, and gave it up at twelve hundred, where the stone ran out and the people did not. They stood along the rim behind the top tier, and sat on the steps, and a row of first-years had got onto the wall of the lecture range and were dangling their legs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:5:It was grey in the hollow still. He had got to his feet by the wall, as he did every morning, to learn what the night had done to him, and he had drawn in air to say something to Brom. At a certain depth, a little short of where the ribs had stopped him the day before, a hand seemed to close across his left side and hold it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:49:Cael stood at the end of the sound rock with the others and saw where a girl had been running three days ago. The shelf stopped as if it had been bitten. Beyond the break there was only the wall of the gorge, and along that wall a scar where the fallen stone had been rooted: a strip of raw pale rock four feet wide, tipped outward like the sill of a window, and heaped with broken plates. It ran on for thirty yards or so, and then the ledge began again, grey and level under its roof, as though nothing had happened. Under the scar there was a great deal of air, and he looked at that once.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-24.md:57:She hit it with her back and shoulders, hard. The new rope did not give. Slacker ropes would have thrown her back off them into the middle of the square. These held her against the post as a wall would, with all her weight and all her speed stopped in them at once, and Cael heard the breath go out of her from twenty feet away.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-45.md:23:Ephram, who had not stopped his set, finished it, and let the long frame's bar come back to its rest with both hands, and went to the rack by the wall where the short frame's iron plates hung on their pegs. He took down two, and looked at them, and hung one back, and took down a heavier one in its place.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-43.md:21:It was the thing she did best in the world, and for three beats of it Cael forgot that she had ever been away. She lied with the front foot, a weight laid on it and left a moment too long. She lied with the back. She lied with her shoulders, a half-turn toward the east wall that pulled the Current girl's whole long glide a yard that way, and the Current girl, who knew perfectly well that it was a lie and had been lied to by it two hundred times, went all the same, because her Path did not stop for knowing. She leaned into her bend.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:69:She was in the chair for her shift a little before noon, with her strapped foot up on a stool and her crutch against the wall and Rooke's red drill-book open on her knee, and she stopped reading and looked at the sill. In the corner of it, where the porter's girl never quite reached with her cloth, a little drift of grey dust had woken up and begun to run along the edge of the wood toward the window frame in a thin stream, as if the sill had tipped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-33.md:91:It came back in the afternoon and stayed. He lay flat through it and let his ears close and open as they liked, while the grey dust ran and stopped and ran again along the wood and the chest in the cellar went the rest of the way along its wall, slowly, scrape by scrape, until it had found the place it wanted and the air went back to being the weight of air.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-06.md:7:Brom, who was sitting on the bench by the wall wrapping his hands, stopped wrapping them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-02.md:101:She was a second-year with ink on her collar and an armful of registry digests, coming down the library's top stair two steps at a time as he went up it after the noon meal with a mug of tea in each hand. The stair was too tight for two people to pass. She flattened herself to the wall to let him by and stared at him the whole time he was going past, and as he climbed on he heard her say to herself, quite quietly, *the left hand, then the right*, the way people say a thing they mean to remember.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-26.md:119:On the four steps outside, in the noise of the market street, Karis stopped and stood with the wallet against her chest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-26.md:183:She did not hurry across the court. She walked to the lodge and stopped beside the lodge-man in his doorway, and before she said a word to anybody, she lifted her eyes to the board on the lodge wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-35.md:111:It was lit the way it was lit every night of the year, a yellow square at the top of the dark building, with the lamp set close to the glass so that you could see it from the north wall and the gate lodge and, Seln had once said, from the ferry landing at the foot of the bluff. The whole valley knew that window. A coat at the foot of the road could have told you the hour by it: lit at dusk, out somewhere between the tenth bell and the eleventh, never once dark before the ninth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-31.md:165:The tiers went up on every side so steeply and so full that they stopped looking like seats at all and became a single sloping wall of faces.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-54.md:31:The betting rows had done something new. The office had let it be known the day before that it did not care for it. For the whole sitting the rows had kept their old frame on the wall, *NO BOOK ON THE DEMONSTRATION BOUTS*, and nobody had touched it. This morning there was a second board beside it. It did not offer odds on who would win. Everybody already knew who would win. It offered odds on the figure. The bands of the Standard were chalked down it in a column, from Iron at the bottom to Gold at the top, cut into half points, with a price against every one. Men were queueing to buy them. Cael read the board through once on his way past, from top to bottom, and saw that there was no line on it anywhere for no figure at all. It had not occurred to anybody to offer one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-54.md:159:He spent his second burst and his third trying to buy ground instead of time, which was what the house had done all year and what had beaten two Silvers. The idea was simple. The oak was the top of the floor. If he could stand somewhere between her and it, anything she laid at him would be climbing. So he went for the slope above her, off the wall, and got a long stride up it, and then another. Then the air behind him went heavy, along the line he had just left, which she had laid while he was still deciding to leave it. It closed. The stride became a pocket. He came out of the pocket on the third burst and landed at the barrier again with nothing bought at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-15.md:57:Its floor was slate, laid in great true flags the size of tabletops, and the walls were slate, and the light came in through tall narrow windows along the south side in long pale bars.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-12.md:135:The Force had learned twice now. He was not going to give her a settle to burst into. He was not going to chase her into a corner where she could go past him along a wall. When the clerk said *exchange* he did not come forward at all. He stamped where he stood, to the left, and to the right, and in front, and behind, a stride out on each side, and as each patch of boards lay down he stamped it awake again, turning slowly in the middle, until he stood in a ring of floor that never stopped jumping. It was like a well with the water always moving at its edges. He stood on the still wood in the middle of it and waited for her to come.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-14.md:273:The first three days were farm country, rolling and wet, with sheep in the hollows and woodsmoke lying flat along the valleys in the mornings. On the fourth day the hedges gave out and the walls began, grey drystone walls that ran up the hillsides and over the tops, ruler-straight. On the fifth the farms got smaller and further apart and the inns got colder, and on the sixth morning there was ice in the horse trough at the waystation for the first time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-33.md:251:Then Cael turned the notebook back to the first page, to her first-round bout, and began to go through it again from the bell. He was not looking for anything this time. He had spent two days hunting gaps in her and had come up with an empty net, so now he simply read, as he might have looked out over a field from the top of a wall, letting the whole shape of the thing come up to meet him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-01.md:5:Halcenvane stood along the top of a bluff above the river Ost, a long grey academy of halls and tiers and covered walks, and for twelve weeks three parts in four of it had been empty. The Crown yard at that hour belonged to nobody. The porter's boards stood bare along the long wall, wiped down for the recess, with the ghost of last season's chalk still grey in the grain of the wood. Mist lay on the tiers. At the far end, by the wash-house, Brom had his shirt off in the cold and was working through his sheet at the striking post, slowly, just as Rooke had written it for him: nineteen exercises, round three, four mornings in the week. He had not missed a morning since the recess began, and he would not have mentioned it if he had been paid to.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-01.md:29:That was the recess's finding, and it had taken him most of the recess to find it. He had come into the twelve weeks meaning to turn six into seven, and for a month of mornings he had gone at the seventh as if it were a wall with a soft course somewhere in it that he only had to find and hit. Every one of those mornings the seventh had been paid for the next day, in a hip that would not take the residence stair without arguing about it, and the eighth had been paid for the day after that. Somewhere in the fifth week he had stopped trying to climb the number and started reading the bill. Six was not a limit at all. Six was what the architecture would sell him at the price it was charging now, and if he wanted a seventh he would not get it by asking harder. He would get it, if he got it, by finding out where the money went.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-01.md:237:The long wall had stood bare for twelve weeks. Now the porter worked along it with his ladder and his chalk and his rag, column by column, from the Copper at the west end to the single name on the Gold. He was copying from a sheet the registrar's office had sent down that morning in Bracken's clerk's hand. He did it the way he had done it every term for longer than anybody could remember: one name, one word, a pause to lean back on the ladder and look, and the cuff of his coat across the letters to polish them. The Mire first-years who had been sent to sweep the oak had stopped sweeping to watch him, and nobody told them to start again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-40.md:11:It was a ring, but not in the way the bracket squares were rings, which were rope on iron stands, put up in an hour and taken down in less. This one had been built to stay for as long as anybody wanted it. The posts stood in the old sockets, one to a socket, plumbed and wedged. Between them ran a barrier of heavy planking, chest-high on a man, with a thick rope laid along its top for a coping so that a fighter thrown against it would meet rope before wood. At the north end and the south the barrier did not run straight. It stepped back from the line for a yard and then came on again, so that each gate was a shallow bay in the wall, a little pocket of floor in its own shadow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-40.md:187:His palm went into it, and that was the strange part. It did not meet a wall. It went in as a hand goes into cold water, and slowed, and slowed again, and somewhere a few inches in it stopped going in at all, with no edge to strike and no surface to push against, only more of the same all the way down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-25.md:135:They worked at it all morning, and every morning of that week. Lira ran Zerin's distances and could not quite reach them. Then she ran Zerin's intervals at her own distances and could reach them, just, three times running and not a fourth. Cael stood off with the read and watched her weight, and called the moment of each burst before it came. He was looking for the thing every Wind fighter has: the moment before a burst when the body draws itself together, setting the hips and loading the foot, like a cat gathering itself on the top of a wall before it jumps. On the oak, in the old days, he had called Lira's gathers a fraction early every time, and it was how the read had first learned her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-24.md:81:Somebody at the gate had seen the wagons on the ferry. By the time they came round the last bend under the wall, the whole house was out along the top of the road in the snow, with lamps.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-24.md:143:The pigeons had been fed. The porter had folded his paper bag and gone in. The court below the window was empty and grey, and the snow lay along the tops of its walls in a long clean line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-10.md:103:An injury had the decency to stop you. Half work let you onto the floor and then stood behind you all morning, saying *no*. She could run the speed line, but only at the pace of a brisk walk. She could stretch, and she did, for an hour. She could not burst, or squat below a chair's height, or spar. On the first morning back on the hill she ran Rooke's line at half pace three times, and then stood at the end of it on the sprung oak with her hands on her hips, looking at the long wall as if it had insulted her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-10.md:197:They left the river on the second day and went up into country Cael had never seen: long bare ridges, sheep on the tops, stone walls running up and over the hills in lines so straight they looked ruled. Every night was colder than the one before. On the fourth morning there was frost on the blankets in the wagons, and Brom woke with his hair stiff with it and complained, at length and with feeling, for the first time anyone could remember. At the waystations Ephram and Cael worked the second-year entry in the yards by lamplight, with Rooke watching from a doorway, and twice the innkeepers' children came out to watch them too and had to be sent to bed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-50.md:33:It was long and narrow, with one tall window at the end looking down into the outer court, and a table of black wood that ran most of its length. What he noticed first were the walls. They were hung, from the wainscot nearly to the ceiling, with framed plans of rings. Some were drawn in brown ink on paper gone the colour of tea. Some were newer, in black and red. Every one was a ring the Concourse had raised on its main floor for a demonstration bout, with its posts and its sockets and its gates, and the cycle written small in a corner, going back further than he could see in the dark at the top.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-27.md:101:The others drifted to the wall to watch. Ephram had his file book out. Karis had nothing in her hands at all, which meant she intended to remember every second of it. Seln was at the yard gate with the travel file under his arm, on his way to the stable to see about the horses, and he stopped there and did not go on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-27.md:143:On the wall, the Stone reserve laughed, and then stopped at once.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-27.md:279:Karis glanced at him and then up at the bar of silver. "Every road in. They always have." She folded the map along its old creases. "It's an old charter, and old charters don't stop at walls. They stop where somebody stopped writing."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-55.md:31:He did it as he had done it on the fourth burst in the hall. He did not shut his eyes; that would have told the whole bowl. He let them go soft instead, as a man does when he reads a page from across a room, and he took his read off her the way you take your hand off a rail. She was still there. He knew she was there, the way you know a wall is there in the dark. But she stopped being loud. And when she stopped, the bowl stopped too, all of it, the eight thousand and the referee's bar and the dais, all of it at once. It was like stepping out of a hall full of people into a yard at night.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-52.md:85:Barrier: posts going in round the edge in fresh sockets, cut deeper than the old ones, and the first length already standing well above a man's head, at six feet, with a carpenter fitting a smooth rounded cap along its top. *Bull-nose*, the foreman called it, shouting. Nothing on it to grip. Breaks: four short lengths of lower wall, at the four points of the compass. Crowd: three rows of the bowl's lowest benches, all the way round, roped off and taken up. He did the sum and wrote it down. Something over a hundred and fifty places. The ticket office would have fought for every one of them and lost.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-13.md:11:Cael's place was the end of the bench by the wall. Nobody had ever said so. It had simply become his, in every room like this one, because he liked a wall behind him and a window where he could see it. He put his left leg up on a stool, because the calf still complained when it hung. Lira, without stopping her thumbs, nudged the oil tin along the hearth with her knee to make room for his foot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-61.md:65:He had to. With that word beside it, the name had no place left among the Copper. He rubbed it out of the top of the column, letter by letter, and climbed down and moved the ladder along the wall, and climbed again, and chalked it at the foot of the Iron column, under Ephram and the rest, with the new word beside it. Down there, under a column of strangers, it looked small. The porter regarded it for a while with his head on one side. Then he climbed down and put the ladder over his shoulder and went away to do the Silver board, and nobody in the yard but Cael had watched him do any of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-61.md:71:She came in by the north arch with her kit over her shoulder, on her way to the session she had on the oak at that hour, and she saw the board before she was halfway across. Cael watched her see it. She stopped. She looked at the empty place at the head of the Copper column where Lira's name had been all season, and then down the wall to the foot of the Iron column, and then at the pinned copy beside the board.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-61.md:89:The yard was nearly bare. The term was almost done, and the porters had begun taking down the season: the brackets were off the long wall, the tiers had been swept, and a man at the far end was coiling the rope that marked the floor's edge on ladder days. On the oak, alone, Ephram was running a Blade drill against nobody, slow, the same six steps over and over, and he stopped in the middle of the fourth and called across.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-61.md:149:Bracken came up the stair behind the cart a little later, as he came behind most things his clerks did, to see them done. He stopped at carrel eleven and looked at the *nil* in the accession book over the inky clerk's shoulder, and at the pale square on the wall where the sheets had hung, and his face did nothing at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-35.md:29:The broadsides had been pricing the bout for two days, and Cael had seen them pasted on the walls of the delegation quarter all the way in, one on top of another where the bill-stickers had run out of wall. Some were about Auremont's perfect product and some about the girl Fenmark had thrown out. The best of them simply printed the two names side by side in large type and left the reader to do the sum.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-51.md:5:That was how it looked from the west gate, in the grey before the morning's inspection: a long low hill of timber and packed earth faced with boards, running across the main floor on the slant from near the north-west corner to the south-east, as if somebody had laid a sleeping animal down the middle of the stone. It was not a wall, like the trial's barrier. It had a back. It rose from the floor at each end at an easy slope, and swelled toward its middle, and then fell away again, and along its top ran a narrow ridge of planking wide enough for one man to stand on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-20.md:7:Copper ran to ninety-one names. Two hundred and nine practitioners on the bluff could have put their names to it, and a few more than half had looked at what a season cost them in hours and bruises and missed lectures, and decided not to pay. Iron ran to sixty-three of its hundred and thirty-eight. The last sheet, headed Silver, held nineteen, gathered from the Bronze and Silver tiers together, all that the top of Halcenvane had been willing to put on a wall. The thinness of that last sheet, Cael thought, was the whole reason a school like this one stayed a school like this one. By the second bell the figures were in the binder.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-37.md:137:"Twenty-two people," said Brom. "I counted them afterward, in bed. Twenty-two people round that table, and every one of them stopped talking. You could hear the candles. My grandmother put down her glass. My mother went white to the lips. And my sister sat there with pear juice running down her wrist into her good cuff, and chewed, and swallowed, and said to the whole table: *it tastes exactly the same.*"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-11.md:51:The low hall was one room, low-ceilinged as its name, with its windows set very high along one wall, so the light came down in long slanting bars and lay across the floor in stripes. The floor was plain stone, swept bare. Two pupils were standing in the middle of it, a fifth-year girl with her hair in a tight knot and a younger boy, and both of them had stopped where they were when the knock came, and had not moved since. Along the back wall a row of pegs held nine grey practice smocks, one for each student of the Path on the bluff, with a name stitched over each peg in blue thread. Below them the stone had been scored with fine straight lines, dozens of them, crossing and recrossing, like the marks a joiner leaves on a bench where he has measured the same cut a thousand times.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-11.md:111:There had been no warning, and that was the thing. His finger was moving, and then it was not, and nothing had arrived on his skin before it stopped: no pressure building, no weight in the air, none of the early signs the read had always laid on him a fraction ahead of a strike. It was not like pushing against a wall, which pushed back. It was more like a sentence arriving at its full stop. His finger had simply been forbidden to go further, and was not going further, and that was all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-11.md:197:Along the flat top of the wall Karis had laid out four documents from carrel eleven to catch the evening light, with a smooth stone on each corner against the river wind. She was reading the third, very slowly, with her finger on the line. Now and then a single word left her, addressed to nobody, and went into the margin.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-14.md:209:He read it as water reads a hillside. Where would people go, and where would they stop, and from where could they be seen stopping? Three halls on the north edge, each with two sets of doors: six streams that ran together for a quarter of an hour at every change of bell and then broke apart again. The assessment wing sat behind a low wall of its own: one gate, then two doors into the building. Excellent, if you worked inside. Very poor for anyone who wanted to stand outside and watch the gate. The lecture range, four floors high, a stair at either end and every landing open along its whole length, so that a man on one landing could be seen from the far stair by anybody who happened to be looking. The quadrangles built round courts, so many windows facing in that nobody ever looked out of any of them on purpose.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-45.md:73:Vastin had heard her through walls for four nights of inns, and he no longer heard the words. He heard the shape. It went on low and level, about the water in the jug and the state of a collar and something a magistrate had once said to her on a stair. Then it stopped in the middle, and there was a silence about as long as a page, and then it went on again from the exact syllable where it had broken off. She had been handed something to read. He had noticed the habit on the second day of the road and had not written it down, because he did not take notes on colleagues. He only noticed that he had noticed, and let it go.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-03.md:5:Cael came in by the side door with his wraps already on and found the gallery full. He had expected that, and it still stopped him in the doorway for a breath. At Greyvane two hundred people was nearly everybody, and nearly everybody was here: on the benches along both long walls, three deep at the rail, sitting on the window ledges with their boots hanging down. The first-years were crammed into the corner by the door, where somebody had told them they could see best and had been wrong. The tout from the river town was moving along the back of the north bench taking wagers in crusts and kitchen duty, quietly, with a great air of not doing anything of the kind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-15.md:117:*Day sixty-three — third bell — lecture stair — second landing, wall side — whole stair, top to foot.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-04.md:83:The four of them were at the gate with their bags. The gate clerk was in the lodge with her list, and the cart that would take their bags down to the stage-house was standing in the road with the carter on the box, and two of the five watchers were where they always were at that hour, one at the gate post and the one who only worked mornings by the wall of the stable. Lira saw Prynn first. She stopped talking in the middle of a sentence and put her hand on Cael's arm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-04.md:137:It was good walking country after the first morning. The cart road off the hill joined the high road at the stage-house, and the high road ran south and east along the top of a long ridge with farms falling away on both sides, sheepfolds and stone walls and the brown of turned fields. The four of them walked it in the order they always walked a road without ever having agreed to: Brom in front because his legs were longest, Karis beside Cael in the middle because she liked to talk while she walked, and Lira a few paces back. She said it was so she could see everything. Cael thought it was so she could see him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-04.md:141:Karis walked beside him with her narrow brown notebook open in one hand and a pencil in the other. She wrote while she walked, which Cael had never been able to do without falling over. At the noon stop they laid the two notebooks side by side on the wall of a sheepfold and compared, line against line, as they had compared the consolidations.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-06.md:173:Until that moment he had not known how many people the bluff held. Standing there he saw he still did not. What the porter had given him was a tally of papers. The Gold fellow was not in it, because the brackets had run out of room above her. And he was not in it either. The brackets had never had a column for him at all. There were two people on this bluff that the boards could not describe, one at the top and one off the side, and neither of them appeared on any wall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-06.md:229:"It changes into everything else," said Cael. "Rooms. You saw the trunks. The instructor's eye, who gets a word after the drill and who doesn't. Which cohort you're put in, and who you train beside. And Karis says the standings feed something bigger, something the academy wants more than any of that." He looked up at the dark hollow of the Crown yard, just visible over the wall at the top of the bluff. "Nobody hands you a purse at the end, because the prize is how they treat you for as long as you're here. Every bout is paid for in where they let you stand next year."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-13.md:207:The passage was full, as it always was at the change of bell, and Cael went along it slowly, because his hip had sent its bill and his right arm would not swing. Heads turned as he went by. Two second-years stopped talking outside the records room. A girl with an armful of practice smocks flattened herself against the wall to let him pass and stared at his arm. Somewhere behind him a man carrying a stack of forms stepped out of the stream, as clerks do, to let the passage go past; Cael did not look at him, because there was nothing in a clerk with forms to look at, and because by then his whole attention had turned inward, to the conversation at the door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-55.md:197:The east wall emptied. The counsel's chair scraped first, and she went out re-tying her ribbons and already talking. Havel tucked his board under his arm with the log sheet squared on the file beneath it and followed her with his eyes on the floor. Ilsev picked up her case and stopped for a breath or two beside the copper pot, where its thread of colour had sunk back to the first line, before she went.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-30.md:97:Footsteps came along the top of the wall, uneven, a long step and a careful one. Lira sat down on the coping beside him with her leg stuck straight out before her and a heel of yesterday's bread in her hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-30.md:211:Jask went past behind him on the way to the Force hall, with his collar up and a book under his arm. He lifted a hand. Cael lifted one back. Jask had turned his dial to the wall for every set now, and had told nobody why, and had moved up two lines on his ledger. He walked the way a person walks who has stopped being the story.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-34.md:49:The building made its night noises round him, and on the third night he knew them all. The stone ticked as it gave up the day's last warmth, a small dry sound somewhere in the wall at his back, like a clock that had lost interest in keeping time. High up in the archive a shutter that had not been properly fastened knocked against its frame whenever the wind came round to the east, three or four soft knocks and then silence for a long while. Once, far off across the bluff, a dog barked twice and was told to be quiet. And under all of it, the whole time, came the river, never stopping, so steady that he only heard it when he listened for it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-07.md:139:There was a case of glass on a stone plinth along one wall, and in it three crowns on three dark cushions with a brass plate under each. The Copper was the plainest of them, and the darkest, and worn bright along its top edge where a great many hands had lifted it. Its plate had been lengthened twice with new brass riveted on below the old.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-54.md:223:The copper was cooling toward nothing when Cael lifted his hand. He rose and returned to the crossing. He kept his eyes off the east wall and still knew what had happened along it, since he had counted rooms ever since Ardenmere and could no more stop than stop his heart. The counsel's pen had run on for a long time and she had turned her page to finish, and then she had leaned along the row and said a few words to Havel, and Havel had made a mark. For the first time that morning Ilsev's fresh point met paper.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md:11:Nothing moved. That was all, and every bit of it was hard. A building full of frightened people, waiting for eleven strangers and a man with no stated business, did not want to stand still. It wanted to sweep its passages and re-ink its ledgers and move its awkward things out of sight. Cael saw the want everywhere. He saw a Stone instructor stop in the second quadrangle with a broom she had picked up from somewhere, and look at it, and look at the flagstones, and then lean the broom carefully back against the wall where she had found it and walk away with her hands in her pockets. He saw two clerks from the bursar's office arguing in low voices at the foot of the lecture stair about whether the lamp-oil ledger ought to be copied fair before the twelfth. Then one of them said *posted day*, and the other said *posted day*, and they went back up the stair together without copying anything.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md:35:He did it properly. He had been trained to arrive at a place properly, and he did it now out of a habit so old it no longer felt like a decision. First the road behind them: the bridge with its toll board, the town, the ferry landing on the near bank with its lamp post and two men in road-grey coats standing under it. He noted the two men, his own service's coats, briefly and without staring, and looked away again, because a records officer who stares at a rotation from a carriage window is a records officer who will be asked by somebody, later, why. Then the road ahead: the bends, the wall, the gate. Then the slope of the hill itself, the roofs above the wall, the long range of grey stone along the top of the bluff with its tiers of seating, open to the sky, standing up against the morning like the side of a ship.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md:71:He did not look at the long grey wall of the Crown yard rising above the courtyard's east side. He did not look at the tiers along the top of it, or at the east end of the upper tier where the sun was. He knew where it was. He kept it at the very edge of the side of his eye, where a thing is not looked at but is still known, and he kept his face toward the door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md:133:Not at the clock over the arch. Not at the roofs. Not at the long grey wall of the Crown yard or any of the tiers along its top. He looked at the corners of the courtyard, one and then the next, the water butt, the arch, the swept empty south side, and then at the door and at Bracken in it, and then at the cobbles, and he kept his chin level the whole way across. Nobody does that in a strange place. Everybody arriving anywhere new looks up at least once, at the roofline, at the height of the walls, at the sky. Havel walked the whole width of the outer courtyard like a man walking under a ladder he has made up his mind not to notice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-62.md:212:Sixty feet along, where the coping dipped and a man might lean his elbows on it without looking as though he had meant to stop, a figure in the plain coat of the assessment office was taking the evening air. He had come along the wall the way any of the staff walked at the close of a term, without hurry and without purpose, and he had stopped, and he stood with his hands clasped behind him and his face turned to the river. The barges seemed to interest him. He had been there a few minutes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-57.md:277:She went on down the passage, one hand on the wall, counting her steps under her breath, and he listened to her turn at the far end and come back past his door without stopping.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-47.md:73:Cael was at the near end of the north bay with his sleeves pushed up, turning his wrists over and back while Brom fed him slow touches on the forearm. He kept his eyes on the floor; he did not need them. Hall three's gallery ran along the north wall above the floor, and its boards were old, and each one had its own small voice. He heard the stair door open at the far end, and he heard people come out onto the boards and spread along the rail, and as they settled he counted the weights: a heavy one, a light one, one that stopped and came on again more slowly, saving a leg.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-22.md:47:Six weeks now Brom had belonged, every evening, to what the residence called Rooke's taking-apart, though nobody could say who had first called it that. It was the hour after supper when the cohort stood round the east hall's walls and Rooke sent them in one at a time to find whatever Brom had not yet stopped doing. They always found it, because Rooke chose who went in, and he chose by what each of them had seen the night before. Then Rooke would stop the floor and have Brom do the thing again, slower, so that fourteen people could watch the exact moment it went wrong. It was, Brom said, like being a lesson somebody else was learning, and having to stand very still while they took notes on you.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-01.md:39:He let his eyes go past her, down the long aisle between the cases, to the second-to-last case against the old wall. The traveller registers stood in their run on the third shelf, crosshatched spines in a row, and between two of them was the space the width of one volume. Nobody had closed it in the months since. Nobody, he was sure now, ever would while Prynn was alive to stop them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-51.md:335:Rooke's real lesson had been smaller and stranger: stop swallowing whole. He took most of each blow on the iron, and simply let the last part of it through, unhardened, into his own body. Whatever he saved by not finishing, he spent on the answer.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-05.md:37:Halcenvane stood along its top. The first thing he noticed was that nothing in it had been inherited. Every school he had ever seen had been made out of something older: a road-house, a granary, a lord's barn with a floor laid in it. This one had been built to be what it was, all at once, by somebody who had drawn it first. The roofs all ran at the same pitch. The stone was the same pale stone from end to end. Along the bluff's north lip ran three long halls with their windows set high under the eaves, all on the side the sun came up. One heavy-roofed building had walls thick enough to carry a great weight of something, and he thought it must be books. A separate block sat behind a low wall of its own. And in the very centre, where everything else on the bluff seemed to lean toward it, there was a great hollow ringed on three sides with tiered stone seats.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-05.md:69:The wall at the top came up no higher than Cael's chest, pale and new and clean-jointed. Nobody could have defended it, and nobody had meant to. It only marked a line on the ground and said *here*. The gate stood open with both leaves folded back. Beside it was a lodge with a glass window, and in the window was a man with a ledger, and in front of the window was a queue: three carts, a woman with two boys and a trunk, a porter with a handbarrow, and a young man in a good coat arguing about something with nobody in particular.

