457:- **Lira's boot.** The carter's spare pair, each "as long as Cael's forearm"; he will not split a pair; two marks. She packs the right with a torn shirt over her taped foot; the left hangs from the cart's tail, is left at Thornwater's barn door by the carter, and goes east on Oryn's mule "against the other one going".
462:- **How the posting came (flag):** by the last mail-ferry, to the landing broker, sealed, with a hundred marks in a bag and "a line of direction"; the broker's clerk brought it up the next morning. Pike said it twice that day to forty people, nobody scratched, and he has kept it under his stone since ("A thing on a tack is a thing I'm offering."). The clerk is the hirer's witness: whatever is brought down is to be seen at the landing, marked, and sent west by the next boat. He does not know what the things are or who wants them.
466:- **Eleven witnesses on the 91st:** Teague and his three; the Oxhollow carter; the hide-woman; the old carter under the ash; the shingle pair; the standing-money boy; the broker's clerk.
467:- **Teague (flag):** he has been round the Fallow Ring eleven times, with carts, by the ruts, at noon, and has never put it to the proof. He once saw a surveyor's kit, "years ago and a long way west". His reason for not taking the paper is his three; his sense that the paper is wrong has no reason, and he tells nobody.
479:- **The floor (flag):** on the crown of a low swell, twenty-six of Cael's strides across by two diameters; colder than the turf; soundless under a boot; its rim rolled over like the rim of a dish, a hand above the turf outside and a finger above the floor within; level under a taut cord and under the brass and thread. Eleven squared grey uprights stand a stride in from the rim, each as thick as a man's body, the tallest twice Brom's height and the shortest knee-high. Round each, at chest height, and round the inner face of the lip, a band a hand wide of fine sunk lines, straight, hooked and crossing. None of Karis's forty-odd chips matches.
481:- **The den:** under the eastern lip, where the slope falls away and the earth has slumped from beneath the rim: a mouth as long as a cart and thigh-high, running back under the floor; a trodden apron; at least four worn hollows in a row; coarse hair on the underside of the lip.
485:*Updated after repair r1 (see `REPAIR-REPORT-r1.md`). Facts changed: the three markers are replaced by the protected units (ch25:43, ch25:131, ch29:169); Teague took a fence on the 89th (the upper inn's paddock, three days, five marks); Seln's locked case is on its strap on him at the ford, out of Thornwater's loft and over the line, shut; all eleven Ring sheets are finished (the tenth torn and done over); the bands run round the foot of each upright and no band on the lip is described; the den is found from above, at the box, and Seln speaks first; Cael's overrun is three quarters of an hour; Cael does not watch Lira's step. Word counts, metrics and line numbers elsewhere in this report are the pre-repair figures.*
491:**Cael.** Eighteen. **Nine confirmed fragments and the uncounted anomaly.** Logged deployment since the Line: one (the ford); the day-96 entry carries no deployment line. **On the 96th, unwitnessed:** the Iron read opened a pace short of the line and carried across it; a foot's worth of Wind evasion twenty strides inside; the Iron read once more on the floor. Shadow is sealed and untouched; it lifted at dusk on the 95th at the turn of the ruts and on the 96th inside the line, and was refused both times. Anchor asked at the stone and at the stake. Ribs: taped, eighteen days from the break, three to go. Calf: bound in Oryn's linen, closing by itself. He has told nobody that anything answered him inside, and has given Karis "It was dark before… It's dark now." for her sixth row. Hesk's satchel holds the log, the old volume, the notebook, Hesk's letter, Pike's lines on bark, and now Ephram's letter and Vell's. He has not answered any of the three.
497:**Karis.** Ember; Iron, Rank Three. Refilled: one point on a wick on the 93rd, "three parts full"; nothing since (roads). Inside she can see every place and set none. Holds: the perimeter table (sixty-two stations, one offset); the six-row table of the trials, with the sixth ruled on his statement; **eleven finished rubbings**, ten in the box and the eleventh rolled inside her coat; the carter's map with three pencilled rings, two marked *guess*; the registry's sheet; the long cord and the short one; the brass; forty-odd chips; the leaf with her nine crossings. She has said "A circle is a made shape." once, and nobody took it up.
499:**Seln.** Shadow; Bronze. Nothing to lift inside. Unmarked. He has read the posting as an office's paper, written by somebody who learned to write inside the Line; the stake as five years in the weather and driven at the far end of a measure; the eastern runs; the den. **The case is on its strap, on him, shut**; it went through Oxhollow's ford on the 85th, left Thornwater's loft with him on the 94th and crossed the line on the 96th, unopened.
501:**Oryn.** Tide; Iron, Rank Four. At Thornwater on the 93rd; one mending there (the carter's hand) and two more that evening. She gave the five her days until the 99th of her own choice, for two reasons. She has taken a surface reading of Brom's wrist on the 94th and at the stone on the 96th; **inside the line she reads nothing and has read nobody.** She has crossed with Brom (twice together, once alone with his arm held across) and with the six. She knows nothing of fragments and has no theory. She cannot mend until she is west of the stone.
509:**Who knows what.** The mechanism: the five, and nobody else. All six know what quiet ground does to a live Arbiter, to the stride, and that a healer's hands stop there. The party knows that the registry's iron stands on the line, numbered thirty-one, some five years old; that no instruments are at the site; that hounds den under the floor. **Nobody but Cael knows that anything answers inside.** Pike, Teague, the clerk and the holds know only that five people took a paper.
511:**Open threads.** Where eight or nine hounds are, at sunset. A lane two hundred yards long and one way out that anybody has walked. A healer who cannot mend east of a stone. A captain's three quarters of an hour. What he has not told them, and why. A posting written inside the Line for a thing that is not there. Thirty-one. Two blanks on a calfskin marked *guess*. "A circle is a made shape." A rope, two holds, three unanswered letters, and no roof.
520:- **Secret/action count:** Cael alone knows the Iron read, foot of Wind and floor read worked. Shadow is unused; deployment since the Line remains one, and the day-96 log deliberately has no deployment line (`manuscript/chapter-29.md:239-299`; `manuscript/chapter-30.md:63-77,219-235`).
531:**Movement 4 CLOSED (2026-10-11).** Same-author repair r1: the seven audit fixes kept and their joins smoothed (Pike speaks unasked, to the open ledger); Seln's case carried on the page (Oxhollow's ford; the east gate; ch30's gear); four tracked passages restaged, the ch30 floor scene re-entered on the bands (Lira sees writing from the turf; the lip found when Brom's cord will not lie down; nine chips rejected and three families tried); ch28's survey proved once; two anchors; a cadence pass. Sol recheck r1: **CLOSE WITH LINE FIXES** — three applied by the coordinator: **Teague's fence is the axe-head hold's longer fence on the east road, forty marks, HOLD** (the repair's paddock job removed), and **Cael's overrun on the 96th is ONE quarter of an hour** (the repair's three quarters and the torn tenth sheet removed). Ruled KEEP: the cut bands run round the FOOT of each of the eleven uprights, a hand above the floor; no band on the lip; eleven finished, numbered sheets. All four tracked passages RESOLVED, no new tracking. Gates 0; overlap 0 unprotected / 36 protected. 37742 words. No markers remain.
537:- **Deployment since the Line: THIRTEEN.** One at the ford (day 53) and twelve in the Ring on the night of the 96th, each priced and billed in the night log: Iron read; Shadow (once, ten strides); Wind; Pressure; Ember (one point on the knife's tip); Blade (an edge on Lira's knife); Anchor (perception only — a deliberate release counts); Storm lanes one, two and three (ten / ten / twelve counts; the body read lost while the air is held); Wind a second time; Compression at contact, late. The two noon tests of the 96th are left out of the number, unexplained. All capability rules held.
538:- **Shadow has been used ONCE and remains sealed.** Only Cael, Lira, Brom, Karis and Seln had the sightline; Oryn was screened and saw him arrive, not come; the pack is no witness. Oryn cannot identify Seln as its source. Seln struck Cael's second reason for silence ("I shall not be a reason… I should sooner have her told what may be done to her than be used to keep her ignorant") WITHOUT offering to be known as the source: Movement 6 tells her sources by Path, not by person, and the seal serves again at the Stair.
540:- **Inside the line** no companion Path was used: Oryn's hands had nothing ("Nothing in my hands"); cloth and knees. Nobody has theorized why the fragments answered. *Quieting* has still not been said. "Compact" has been said aloud once, by Seln. Nothing is known of where the file is, what it says or why; dating stops at "five years… maybe six". Seln's map memory (maps with holes) is open; he asked for the carter's map.
546:All reads on Sol (gpt-5.6-sol): editorial, cold read, source-distance (10 tracked passages; one dense scene, ch34 scene 3; one of the ten is now a restored protected unit), plan / canon / protected-text audit (RATIFY WITH LINE FIXES — 21 applied by the coordinator, including the four protected units the author had left as markers). Same-author repair r1 in progress when this block was written; the CLOSED note follows when the recheck returns.
549:- **The pack came home by the west (flag).** Nine came over the floor's western lip at sunset, up the party's own trodden lane, having gone out by the east at first light. Cael notes that a healer counted to ten on a wrist at that stone at noon with her Path running; "he did not know that it bore, and he put it by for whoever kept findings." It is not entered as a finding by anybody.
557:- **Storm in a channel:** a lane laid down a rut strips the standing grass of its seed and drives it down the channel; the read cannot be under his soles while a lane is held. Three lanes, with bills of ten, ten and twelve counts.
560:- **What the reading is like from under it (flag):** it goes along the length of a person and returns to her other hand, at the pace she walks, favouring the right by a hair. He can tell where she is. As she passes: Wind turns over and settles; his palm warms; Compression does nothing; the read wishes to open; the ninth draws itself in small, unasked; the heaviest lies quiet. She says "You're helping." when his neck hardens.
562:- **Seln's memory (flag):** as a junior he carried a tin with a lead tag from one room to another and counted, without reading, eight fair linen-backed sheets of the country east of the Line: a ruled stroke for the Line, dashed roads carried farther than on the printed sheet, and on some sheets a small round ruled with a compass, empty, with a road going about one; no sign for it in the table at the sheet's foot; one sheet with two. He took them for something guarded. No room, office, year or name is given, and he says that he cannot place the second round.
571:*Updated after repair r1 (see `REPAIR-REPORT-r1.md`). Facts changed: Brom's hurt is ONE hand, four finger-pads; Oryn's rule for it is "Nothing on the string for three days"; Seln strikes Cael's second reason without offering to be known to Oryn; Lira's arm was first bound with Brom's folded cuff and strips of Oryn's shirt, her linen being on the mule; Cael's calf stays in Karis's blanket strip from the 96th until the yard on the 99th and is never bound by Oryn; the hound that Cael throws is let go by and helped on its way, after the jaws are levered open with the hilt of Lira's knife; the second lock is struck from behind, and he goes down onto the left forearm. Word counts, metrics and line numbers elsewhere in this report are the pre-repair figures; the markers it lists are filled.*
577:**Cael.** Eighteen. **Ten confirmed fragments** from the twentieth minute: the tenth is Tide-adjacent, partial, Iron, self and contact, *Acquisition: directed. Engagement: clinical — non-hostile.* It has not been tried, and its cost has not begun. The uncounted anomaly is untouched and unmentioned in this movement. **Deployment since the Line: thirteen** (the ford; twelve in the Ring on the 96th), as his log has it; the noon tests of the 96th are noted in the log as left out. Shadow used **once**. Ribs whole, the tape off on the 99th. Calf closed; it stayed in Karis's blanket strip from the night of the 96th until Oryn undid the knot in the yard on the 99th. Left forearm bruised and working (he came down on it under the hound's weight in the second lock). He knows that refusing would have been right and that the leaf permitted nothing; he chose against it and means to answer for it. He has told Oryn two things: that what she saw on the hill was his doing, and, in the last minute, "I need to tell you what I am." He has told nobody why anything answered him inside, and has not told Lira since when he knew. Three letters still unanswered.
585:**Seln.** Shadow; Bronze. Unmarked. He fetched the bow, opened the jaws, carried Karis to the stone and called "Back". He has said "Compact" aloud once. He has given what he remembers of eight sheets and has refused to guess the file; nobody knows what such a file holds. He has struck Cael's second reason himself ("I should sooner have her told what may be done to her than be used to keep her ignorant") **without offering to be known to Oryn as a source**; the seal stands. **The case is on its strap, on him, shut.**
595:**Who knows what.** The mechanism: the five. Oryn knows that he can do, inside a line, what no Path can; she saw Wind, a light, and heard an edge ring; she did not see the Shadow use and cannot attribute his arrival at Karis. Lira, Brom, Karis and Seln saw it. All six know that the registry's iron stands on the line, that maps were drawn with a blank there, and that the posting was a question which their coming out has answered. Nobody knows where the file is, what it says, or why. Nobody has said why the fragments answer inside.
597:**Open threads.** The telling, on the table, owed that minute. What it cost her, if anything. A leaf in a loft to be fetched and read. *Later*, on Lira's side. Seven hounds. A report going west with six on it. An arm slung until the snow, and an ankle that nobody has mended. Eleven sheets not yet looked at. Two rings marked *guess*. The top gate of a round, eleven days north.
604:- **Counts/seal:** deployment since the Line is thirteen; Shadow has been used once and remains sealed; Oryn cannot identify Seln as its source and must hear sources by Path, not person (`manuscript/chapter-33.md:203-233`; fix 18; `MOVEMENT-006.md:26`).
610:**Movement 5 CLOSED (2026-10-11).** Same-author repair r1: the 21 audit fixes kept and their joins smoothed (Brom's one hand and four finger-pads carried through); **the weighing of the tenth fragment made plain for the Reader Standard** (the four names "had been one name, which was *wrong*"; Cael says aloud what the right thing is and that he will do neither; the leaf "did not make the thing allowed"; on the table he sees the honest way to stop, calls it the right road, and does not take it); nine tracked passages restaged, ch34 scene 3 re-entered; the aftermath's duplicate recitations combined; ch33's long-sentence share brought down. Sol recheck r1: **CLOSE**, no line fixes: every brief item, all nine tracked passages, the Reader Standard, the fragment and rule accounting (twelve in the Ring, thirteen in all; the seal satisfied) and the handoffs pass. Gates 0; overlap 0 unprotected / 38 protected. 36578 words. The author-sourced state sections of the Movement 2–5 blocks above were refreshed from the post-repair reports at this close (tools/ledger_refresh.py), and two stale injury lines corrected.
617:- **The debt:** three readings of himself, on three different days, then all of it to her at once, nobody on the road. OWED; the day is hers to set; NOT paid in this movement. The tenth has been used on nobody but himself.
618:- **Integration cost paid** as the listening cascade (it listens, it doesn't pull): his own current at the edge of thought; the nine heard one at a time; the tenth is "the thing I'm hearing with"; Storm heard resettling. Loudest is "nearer", under the breastbone.
619:- **Deployment since the Line: THIRTEEN, unchanged.** Eleven small Wind leans in yard practice on day 101 are logged as practice, outside the count.
629:All reads on Sol (gpt-5.6-sol): editorial, cold read, source-distance (7 tracked passages; one dense scene, ch40 scene 4), plan / canon / protected-text audit (14 line fixes applied by the coordinator, including the three protected units the author had left as markers; two plan conflicts raised — one already owner-resolved, one logged as #51). Same-author repair r1 in progress when this block was written; the CLOSED note follows when the recheck returns.
632:- **The table, as staged after repair:** he lies still for the telling and for her naming of it; he gets down for what it did and what it costs, and stands by the table for about a quarter of an hour; she gives him his book, tells him to lie down again (the protected line), puts the horn cup in his hand, takes the book back, and sits on the stool with it for a minute before she sends him out.
635:- **The listening (flag):** his own current at the edge of every thought, quick and slow; the nine by sound (Wind, dry grass going over; the read, a millstone through a floor; Ember, a wick ticking; Pressure, a bellows; Compression, hemp under load; Anchor, one held note; Blade, a wet finger on a glass; Shadow, a hush that drops the others; Storm, a sea turning over). It cannot be refused, since it asks for nothing. Three nights. Asked on the 101st where it is loudest, he answers at the breastbone: it is not louder there, it is nearer (the protected line).
636:- **Storm resettling (flag):** heard as one long roll from side to side; afterward the ground arrives half a beat late until dusk, with no lane laid.
644:- **Hesk's parcel:** its absence is found at the cleared board, when Cael puts the bark into Hesk's satchel, which was itself the last parcel, with the last note still under its flap. Nobody speaks of it.
653:- **The mule (C2, flag):** left with the crew on the 110th. She goes on the low side of nothing and the driver on the low side of her; she will not cross moving water with anybody on her near hand; her near hind has been shod since the Oxhollow road; she answers to a click; she has never had a name and is not to be given one. Oryn takes the off pannier on her own back and the spare rope, thirty feet of three-strand, for the next gate's jenny.
656:- **The debt's terms:** three readings of himself on three different days; then all of it to her, at once and in order; nobody read on the road to find out whether he can. "If I'm at a table, you'll sit on the bench and wait your turn."
664:**Cael.** **Nineteen** since the middle of the meal on the 102nd. **Ten confirmed fragments.** The tenth (Tide-adjacent) has been used on himself only: three days of listening, idle by the third night; one self-reading on the night of the 102nd. He has found **a place near the middle of himself that does not flow**, which all ten go round; he knows where it is, that nothing moves in it, and that none goes through; he has not named it, has not gone back to it, and has told nobody what it is. He has told Lira and then the six that there is a thing, and that it is the healer's to hear first. The three standing words are retired; the log reads *Patience is over; observation begins.* **Deployment since the Line: thirteen**; eleven leans of Wind in the yard on the 101st are logged as practice outside the number. Shadow unused; it lifts at dusk and is refused. Body whole: ribs, calf; the left forearm's feeling back to the knuckles on the 100th, a yellow mark. He has said to Oryn that it was wrong; he has not been forgiven. **He owes her:** three readings of himself on three different days, and then all of it at once; he does not know what she would have said at the door and never will; the size of the rest is hers. **The rule** is in his book in his hand, with her note under it. He has told the six that he knew at noon on the 96th. He holds a hypothesis that he has not written. Three letters still unanswered.
666:**Lira.** Wind; Iron, Rank One. No burst. **Left arm slung and under the order until first snow; all eight marks taken at the fortnight (the 110th).** Right tibia released: it may run. **Right ankle taped, not mended; she walks on the whole sole.** She can shoot the hold's yard bow braced on a rail or across her knee, one-armed, when somebody else spans it: four of eight in a plate-sized ring from the rail at sixty paces, and one of one from the knee. She cannot span. She kept the gate-walk alone at dusk on the 110th. "Later" is discharged: she was told first. Her long knife is still blunt.
674:**Oryn.** Tide; Iron, Rank Four. **She knows the mechanism** (told on the 100th, with the nine by Path). Her hands are whole. She has set: on himself first; she is the one he tells; ask, every time; three readings on three days and then all of it; nobody read on the road to find out whether he can. She has not said what she would have answered, and has not forgiven him. She read Lira's arm on the 106th and the 110th. **She has left her mule with the crew** and gone north on foot with one pannier and a spare rope, **sixteen days late out of Thornwater and seventeen at Oxhollow**, to take the next gate's beast. She has said that she goes north at first light, that Oxhollow is seventeen days late, that Thornwater is on her route, and "Don't go in without me." She has heard no theory, since nobody has offered one.
686:**Who knows what.** The mechanism: **six.** That he knew at noon on the 96th that the fragments answered inside: the six. That he has read himself and found something: the six know that there is a thing; none knows what. The Quieting, by that name: the six. The hold, Pike, the carter, the clerk and the west know that six went in and six came out. Nobody has put a verb between a made floor, an age with no ruler, and a boy.
688:**Open threads.** Two more readings, and a telling owed on her day. A question that she will not answer. A place that does not move. A count of kinds that wants a second floor. Two rings marked *guess* and a round that nobody can place. "One at a time, for money." A stake numbered thirty-one. A report eight days gone west. An arm slung until the snow; an ankle that nobody has mended; a bow that she can shoot and cannot span. A mule that is not theirs. Three unanswered letters and a parcel on a ferry somewhere.
693:- **Cael:** nineteen; ten fragments; deployment thirteen; Shadow still only once; the eleven Wind leans are practice; the tenth has read only himself, once; two further self-readings on different days and the whole telling to Oryn remain owed; he may read nobody merely to test the ability; Oryn’s answer and remaining price stay withheld (`manuscript/chapter-39.md:245`; `chapter-40.md:189–211,319–385`; `chapter-44.md:167–209`).
696:- **Knowledge:** all six know Cael functioned inside the Ring and know that his self-reading found something, but none except Cael knows what; Oryn knows the mechanism but not that Seln supplied Shadow; Karis’s hypothesis is neither spoken nor entered; the Quieting name and five findings are shared; nobody has put a verb between the facts (`manuscript/chapter-44.md:11–105,113–117`).
701:**Movement 6 CLOSED (2026-10-11).** Same-author repair r1: the fourteen audit fixes kept and their joins smoothed (Cael's "Then it's owed, and I've heard you say so." before Oryn's restored unit; Karis asks Oryn for a healer's count at the next edge and is not answered; Lira's "And if it's sooner than Thornwater?"); seven tracked passages restaged, ch40 scene 4 re-entered (Gault's line comes back as Brom reads Pike's bark aloud; Hesk's parcel found missing at the cleared board); ch41 now runs forward in time; the research run states its observed-versus-supposed thesis once, at the count on the night of the 103rd. Sol recheck r1: **CLOSE**, no line fixes: every brief item and all seven tracked passages resolved; the telling keeps Oryn's full agency with no retroactive consent or forgiveness; the Movement 7 joins promise neither a sixth place nor a joining. Gates 0; overlap 0 unprotected / 42 protected. 37597 words. Author-sourced sections above refreshed from the post-repair report.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:227:It was not the whole. He looked at that squarely, with the pencil lifted. There were things that he had done at noon, by himself, a cord north of this stone and on a floor, which nothing had seen and which he had written of only to say that he had told nobody. They were not in the twelve. He would not put them into a count that he could not yet explain, and he would not pretend to the page that they had not happened.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:249:It was one word, and she had left him the whole width of it. She did not say which of them, or how many. She did not say that she had been looking at grass on her left hand, in a lane, with nobody in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:269:He could not make it. He had the fact, which was that nine things had answered him in a place where a healer could not count. He had nothing to put after the fact. And he knew what became of a thing that was written down before anybody understood it, since it had been done to him. A clerk had found no tier for a boy, and had put a word in capitals where the tier should go, and from that morning nobody had needed to ask what the boy was. A file stopped the asking. He would not do that to this, and he would not hide it either.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:5:There was no need of it. Nobody in that hold would have come up uninvited; and the one person whom he did not want at its foot had gone to her bed at dusk, across the yard, behind a small window with no light in it, like somebody who has work in the morning. He drew it up all the same. He had asked four people among the barley sacks to hear a thing, and he wished to have done something with his hands before he began.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:11:Nobody moved.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:23:"I've nine, and you know how each of them came. There was somebody with a Path, who used it on me or against me for long enough, and meant it; and there was something that mattered to both of us in how it came out, whether that was a card, or a ruling, or only which of us was left standing. That's the whole of what any of them had in common, and Karis has a page on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:61:"She doesn't know, and I don't, since there's been nothing to try it on. Every one of the nine came out of a fight of some kind. There's never been a person who did a real thing to me for a long time, at a cost, who wasn't standing on the other side of a floor." He touched the second leaf. "If she's right, tomorrow is the first morning of my life on which it could happen with nobody struck. And if it does, I shall have a piece of what a healer reads with."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:113:Nobody in the loft breathed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:141:"Then it's wrong," he said. "I'd not stop you; I'd not know how, and it isn't mine to. But somebody ought to say it who's glad of what you carry, and I am. I was glad of it on a ridge and at a ford, and three nights back, for her arm." He jerked his chin at the sling. "It's wrong to do that to a person who climbed a mountain for five strangers on a carter's word."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:183:He looked at the four of them in the last of the oil. Not one had told him that he might, and not one had offered to stand in a door. Each of them had called the thing by its name, in turn, as Pike said a line to a meadow so that anybody might call it wrong; and the four names had been one name, which was *wrong*, and he had not a word to set against it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:187:"I'm going to do it," he said. "I've heard every one of you, and you're right. The right thing is to cross that yard tonight; or, failing that, to tell her in the morning before she has laid a hand on me. I know that. I am going to lie down on that table and say nothing, and tell her the minute that it's over. I want you to know that I chose it with all of this in front of me. Nobody talked me into it, and nobody was kept from talking me out."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:197:"You could have done it and told us after," she said. "You could have told nobody at all. You carried it up a ladder and let four people call it what it is." She touched the top of his head once, with two fingers, as she might have touched a card for luck. "It's still wrong. Go and write it down."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:207:*The conditions, as I have them. Sustained: twenty minutes at contact is longer than any bout I've stood. Stakes: I'm three days off the Ring and the nine are still settling; she's three mendings down and her route's late at the far end and she's spending a fourth on a patient who's given her nothing four times. Earnest: she'll do the thing she does as well as she does it. Karis: the model says real, not fight. I don't know if the model's right. Nobody does. Tomorrow's the first time it could be tested and I'm the test.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:209:*The price. She doesn't know what I am. She can't consent to what she can't know about — not won't; can't. There's nothing I could put in front of her tonight that she could say yes or no to, because the thing doesn't have a name she's heard. I've decided not to tell her before. My reasons are good, and they're the reasons of somebody who wants it, and the real one is that I don't know what she'd say. Entered.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:223:That was all that the last line meant. A registry had read him for years, and written down what it found, and had never once told him beforehand what it was reaching for or what the reaching might cost him. He was about to reach for somebody else in the same way. The one thing that he had which that office had not was a leaf with tonight's date at the head of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-36.md:225:It was a thin difference, and it did not make the thing allowed. He was exact with himself about that, since he was trying to be exact about all the rest. To cross the yard now and knock would be right. To say in the morning, before she had so much as washed her hands, what those hands might lose, would be right. Four people had shown him both doors. A leaf written first opened neither of them; it meant only that when he walked past them he would be doing it with his eyes open, and could never afterward tell anybody, himself least of all, that he had not known.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:15:He turned that over in the dark, since he had the leisure. He had said once, at a fire in a meadow, that he kept waiting for someone to ask for his report. For fifteen years his reports had been asked for by a slot in a door; for a hundred days after that they had been asked for by nobody, and he had gone on walking the first hour of every road, alone, and saying what he found or not as it bore.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:23:It had been right. A bow at the lip had been worth more than a second knife, and he would have gone again. He noted only that it was the same price each time, and that somebody else always paid it. He had no column for that, and he found, a little to his surprise, that he would have liked one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:31:Seln had not seen him go. Nobody sees that. It is the whole of the craft that there is no instant at which a person is seen to leave; the eye passes over the place and finds it not worth resting on, and goes to something with more in it. He had learned it on a back stair when he was younger than any of them. It had taken him a year to do on purpose, and three more to stop doing by accident whenever a door slammed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:33:He had known it this time from the outside, by two signs. A big man's eye had slid over a piece of air and come back to it. And along the south edge of the lane the grass had leaned, stem by stem, with nothing leaning on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:43:He had wondered, since a night when the boy told him the whole of it in order, what it would be to see his own stair in another person's feet. He had supposed that he would feel robbed. He considered the matter now with some care, standing in frost with a locked case against his ribs, and what he found was nearer to what a mason feels who passes a wall and sees that somebody has laid a course as he would have laid it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:57:He had told the boy that he did not know what the number counted, and that he would not guess. Both of those were true. There was a third thing which he had not said, because it was not yet a thing: something in him had answered to that iron, and it was not the Shadow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:85:There had been eight sheets. They were fair copies, on paper backed with linen, and they were of the east.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:87:He had not read them. But nobody can count eight sheets, corner by corner, and see nothing. He had seen a ruled stroke down the left of each that he took for the Line. He had seen roads beyond it, drawn in dashes, as they were drawn on the printed sheet that lay in a box at the camp; only these had run on much farther before they stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:93:He had counted eight, and said *eight*, and the senior had initialled the docket; and a young man with a satchel had gone down a stair thinking about his dinner.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:97:Seln stayed on his heels by the iron a long while, and went over what he had, strictly, as he would have gone over it for somebody else's signature. He had eight sheets, seen once, by a boy who was counting. He had rounds, ruled, with a road going about one of them. He had a stake under his eyes, of the pattern that is signed for, driven on the edge of a circle that a researcher had paced with her feet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:101:He could not tell, from memory, where on those sheets a round had fallen. He had the look of one: a water crossed, and dashes running on east of it, and then the white place. To say more than that he needed a ford drawn by somebody who had put a wheel through it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:105:The camp was stirring when he came down the line. The healer lay as she had fallen. The girl was awake, with her eyes on the sky, and the boy sat beside her with a book shut on his knee and one arm held in his lap like a thing that belonged to somebody else. Karis was sitting up against the box. She had her hand flat on its lid, and he knew that she had not yet opened it to look at eleven sheets, and why.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:197:"The arm. That was last night, and it was one, and it was the size of two. The throat. The hand. Three mendings in two days." She turned her hands over on her knees and looked at them, and let them lie. "That's a route. Near enough, that's what I carry between a hold and the next one, for the people who feed me and shoe the mule. Three serious hurts and a road's worth of small ones. And I've spent it on a road two days from the nearest roof, on a crew that's not on my circuit, sitting against a piece of iron somebody drove where they'd measured to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:201:"The top gate of my round's eleven days north of here," said Oryn. "That's by the road, at her pace." She tipped her head toward the camp, and the mule. "I'm not telling you for thanks. I've watched you. You count everything that's put in front of you, and that's a figure nobody would have put there."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:213:Seln had laid the calfskin on the turf, with a stone on each corner, where the stake's shadow did not fall across it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:229:Nobody spoke.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:285:"Somebody got it wrong," said Lira. "Somebody thought they'd left them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:287:"A person who writes a sum twice, so that it cannot be altered, does not forget where he has put a brass level." Seln held out his hand for the sheet, and when he had it he held it up unread to the afternoon, as he had held it to a lamp, with the light behind. "I said in that inn that it had been written by somebody who learned to write inside. I said that it was not a reason against going. I should have gone on, and asked what it was a reason for."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:293:Cael saw it then, as he saw a seam in a bout when it was already past mending. A paper had asked whether anybody who went into that ring would come out of it. He had carried the answer down a hill himself, with a light.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:301:"It'll not say how," said Lira. She was looking at Cael, and then she was looking at the grass. "Nobody saw how but us."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:9:"Because a thing that is written twice is a thing that somebody means to use," said Seln. "I have filed a great deal of paper. I know which leaves are drawn out of a box first."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:25:"It is not in the four because nobody in this barn has seen it but one person, once, as a boy, when he was counting corners. I have his memory entered where he gave it, on the leaf of the ninety-seventh, with *entered as said* beneath." Karis touched the roll of thin paper that had the ring of charcoal on it. "That ring lies on mine. It is the best thing that I have to hang a guess on, and a guess is what it stays."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:53:Karis laid the roll in the middle box, by itself, at one end, a little apart from the name and the five rows and the founding stone, as a person sets down on a bench the one tool that belongs to somebody else's trade.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:83:It was said without any bitterness, as she might have told them that a figure had to be carried to the next column whether anybody liked the look of it there or not. She gathered the three leaves, one upon another, with the rubbing on top, and put them into the box, and let the lid down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:93:"The circle's perfect," she said. She was looking at the shut box. "Perfect isn't natural. Somebody made a floor that turns Paths off. It's older than anything I can date. And it doesn't turn you off. Three facts. I'm not putting a verb between them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:105:"You have been a sentence in somebody's box since you were fourteen. There is a word on a file in the west that was put there by a form, since the form could not be filled in, and you have had to walk about under it. I have watched what that cost." Her hand lay on the lid. "There is nothing in this box about you that you have not been told to your face, and there is not going to be. That is all that I kept you for."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:111:"No. You have not, in four days, and I had noticed." She took her hand from the lid. "It is the kindest thing that anybody has done for me since I crossed the Line, and I have entered it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:171:"So that's two boxes on two backs, and one left over, and a case that the quiet one won't put down." She counted it on her fingers. "And a girl who's to fall on her face sooner than put a hand out, on a road that's ice in the ruts. Who steadies her, when the pair of you have a hundredweight apiece?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:181:"I've had her thirty rounds, and she's a mule. She'd as soon carry boxes as panniers. Boxes don't want mending at the end of the day." The healer spoke more quickly than she was used to, and looked at none of them. "There's a beast at every gate on my round. They feed mine, and one of them shoes her. The next has a jenny that's eaten her head off since the barley was in and done nothing for it. I'll have that one off them, and they'll thank me. You've nobody who'd lend you so much as a goat."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:185:"You can't. Nobody's put a coin in my hand since I crossed the Line, and you'll not be the first to try." Her eyes came round to him, tired and level. "And you least. I've a bill against you that I've not finished adding. I'll not have a mule put in on the other side of it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:191:"She goes on the low side of nothing. You go on the low side of her. She'll not cross moving water with anybody on her near hand, and it's no use arguing; she was like that when I got her. Her near hind's been shod since the Oxhollow road, and it'll want looking at by the thaw. She answers to a click." She showed him, with her tongue. "She's never had a name, and she's not to be given one. It's a beast. You name a thing and then you're sorry for it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:215:Nobody had been told; the boy had gone out through the wicket with a mallet, and that had been enough. The steading's woman stood on her step with her arms folded. Her husband had come round the end of the long house with flour to his elbows, and was explaining to two carters from up the valley that the girl shot from a rail, since she had only the one arm to her, and that he had seen worse done with two. The healer had come as far as the door of the back room, and no farther, and leaned there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:229:She put four of the eight into the ring. He counted them, and so did a good many other people, though nobody on that ground said a figure aloud; Karis, behind him, was making strokes in her notebook with the pencil held very small. The boy went out between each pair and came back at a run, with his chin up, as if the bolts had been his own. Not one went over the sack, and not one fell short of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:249:Nobody cheered, since it was not that sort of hold. The steading's woman said "Well," to nobody, and went back to her door, and the small man was heard to remark that he had said so. At the back room the healer pushed herself off the door-post. She had not been watching the sack at all; her eyes had been on the place where a left arm was, behind the planks of the walk, and whatever she had seen there, she went in without a word.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:267:She was alone on the walk, and that was new. The lean man had gone down to his supper while there was still light in the west, which Cael had never known him do; and he had left a girl with one arm sitting against the southern post, and the yard bow lying spanned on the planks beside her knee. She had the whole valley in front of her, grey going to blue, with the frost coming down on a mile of pale grass and a white stone standing in it; and it came to him that in thirty years nobody but one man had sat where she was sitting at that hour.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:281:"Of yours. Not half an hour after you'd stood at that stone and asked for all of it, and been given a step." He kept his eyes on the valley. "And I walked back and gave Karis a row for her table that had two true halves in it and wasn't the truth. I had three reasons. One was the healer, who knew nothing. One was sealed. The real one was that I'd found a thing that I couldn't finish the sentence for, and I've never been able to hand anybody half a sentence."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:307:He had not known how much he had been waiting for somebody to say that it was enough. He had carried a half-made sentence for fifteen days as a thing to be ashamed of, since it had no end to it; and she had taken it out of his hands, and looked at it, and told him that a half was what there was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:311:"Out there I was the thing the system couldn't hold." It came out more easily than he had thought it would, for a thing that he had never said to anybody. "In there I was the only thing that *worked*. I don't know which one's worse."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:325:"There's one thing more," he said. "I read myself, on the night of the birthday. I found something. I can't give it to you. It's the healer's to hear before anybody; that was her price, and I'm paying it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:5:"I was fourteen. It was a district office on a cold morning, with a chalk circle on the boards for me to stand in. Something woke behind my breastbone, as it wakes in everybody on that day: a small light at the edge of my eye. You'd call it an Arbiter." He kept his voice low and his breath slow, as she had taught it to go. "It looked at me for eleven seconds, and I counted them. There's a plate let into the desk in those offices, which lights with the name of your Path. For eleven seconds it stayed dark."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:9:"Then it lit, late, with one word on it, and the word wasn't a Path. Karis found out what it is. It's what the form writes when it can't be filled in, and it stands where an older name used to be written, before somebody took the older name away. After that the light went out. It has been dark ever since, on a floor or a road or at your stone. When the five of you crossed that line, something stopped in each of you; and nothing changed in me, since nothing was lit to begin with."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:15:He gave her the rest as the five of them had settled it in a reading room above Ostrand, a piece at a time, with nothing in it that Karis could not have sworn to. He had never said it to anybody lying down. It was easier than he had feared and a good deal worse than he had hoped, since her palm rose and fell with his breath and she could feel every place where he wished to hurry.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:17:"What came after is the part that concerns you. When somebody with a Path uses it on me, or against me, for long enough, and means it, and there's something that matters to both of us in how it comes out, then a piece of what they do stays in me afterward. They still have all of theirs; I've looked, every time. But I have a piece as well. It's smaller than theirs, and it costs me to carry, and it answers when I ask."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:25:"There's a Wind's, and that one is Lira's. There's an Iron Skin's, which is Brom's, and an Ember's, which came from Karis. You know those three better than I do, since you've had your hands in them. Then a Pressure's, from a Bronze at Ardenmere, and a Compression's, from a fighter on a circuit. A Storm's, from a Gold. An Anchor's, from a hand whose face I never saw. A Blade's, which a friend gave me in a bout that he had asked for." He drew one slow breath. "And a Shadow's. That one I keep shut, for the sake of the person it came from, and I'll tell you its Path and nothing more. I used it once on that hill, for ten strides, in the lane. I looked first to see where your eyes were."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:31:"That's what you saw on the hill, then: the crack was the Wind's, and the fire on the knife was the Ember's. They answer in there, all of them, as they answer anywhere. I don't know why, and nobody does."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:35:"This morning there's a tenth." He kept his eyes on her face, which hung over him upside down and grey. "It wants a real thing done to me for a long time, at a cost, by somebody who means it, and you had told me at Oxhollow what the long reading was: both hands, and all of you, for minutes. I knew when I lay down what it was likely to be. At the end there was a place in me standing open, and the thing that you read with was at the door. I could have kept that door shut. I'm better at shutting them than at anything else I do. I opened it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:39:"When one comes, there are lines. I see them, and nobody else can; they've come every time, in the same place, like a notice pinned to a board." He said them over to her as they stood, six lines from the top, leaving nothing out: what it was adjacent to, and that it was partial, and its tier, and the two things that it was made of, and its range, which was the length of an arm and no farther. He gave her the last line more slowly than the rest.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:107:"I've one rule at this table that I never taught anybody, since I'd not have thought it wanted teaching. Nobody lies down here without being told what I'm going to do to them, and why, and what it's like to cost. I tell a child. I tell a man who's out of his head with a fever, and I say it twice, in case one of the times gets in." She nodded at the plank. "You lay down there this morning knowing what you were going to do to me. The one person in this room who wasn't told was the healer. You turned my table round. That's what you did, and I'll thank you not to dress it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:115:"And your four told you. I've sat with that a minute, and I find it's the part I mind most. It wasn't a thing you did in the dark, by yourself, without anybody to say what it was. You'd been told its name by four people, and you'd agreed with them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:153:It went into him more deeply than anything that she had yet said. He had priced a great many outcomes in a loft: her anger, and her contempt, and a road going north with nobody on it that he knew. He had not once thought that she might simply decline to tell him, and leave the question standing open in him for good, in the one place where no leaf of his could shut it and no price of his could buy it shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:173:"You've been a thing that nobody could read since you were fourteen. I've stood at that door five times and once more for twenty minutes, and I've a list to keep, and I came all the same." For the first time that morning he heard something in her voice that was not arithmetic. "I want to know what you find, and I want to be the one you tell."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:187:"Leave this with me," she said at the end of it. "Get your shirt when the floor's steady under you, and go and eat something, and send nobody in."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:193:She did not keep one. She had told the small pale one so across a fire: a healer who wanted a page to know who was owed had stopped looking at them. It was the truest thing that she knew about her trade, and here she sat on her own stool, in her own room, with somebody else's page open on her knee and her thumb in the margin of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:195:She read the leaf a third time, since there was nobody to see her do it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:199:She had been lied to on that table more times than she could count. People lied to a healer as they lied to nobody else: about how long it had hurt, and what they had lifted, and whether they had kept the arm still. She knew what to do with a lie. She set it to one side and read the body.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:201:Nobody had ever before told her the truth a night too late, in writing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:205:There had been skin, and breath, and a heart that somebody had run hard since it was small. Past those there had been the moving, more than one, with no bank anywhere; she had looked for twenty minutes and she would have looked for forty. That much she had said aloud while she was in, since it was truer said so.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:209:She had not said that part aloud. She had taken it for tiredness. In a long reading the healer goes along the patient and comes home to her own left hand, over and over, and toward the end she begins to hear herself as a person hears her own boots on a road at night. This had been like hearing a second pair. They had fallen exactly with hers, with the same short step on the right, where her heel was raw.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:211:After that they had gone on without her, inside him, and she had stopped, since nobody goes on walking when her own footfall leaves her and carries on down the road alone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:225:That was the difference, and there was no other. Every mending of her life had been given, by a woman who knew the price and said yes. This had been taken off her while her hands were full, by a boy who had thought the price might be high and had kept his mouth shut. Her purse weighed the same. It was the asking that he had had from her; and she, who asked the dying before she laid a finger on them, knew to a hair what an asking was worth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:233:They were on the bench when the shadow of the fence had crossed the well, five of them, as she had left them the evening before.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:249:She did a second after it, a woman's thumb that had gone bad round a thorn, and read the other three and told each of them the truth. Nothing came back short. She had her answer before the shadow of the fence reached the trough, and it was the same that he had given her: it did not show.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:253:The girl was sitting on the well's coping, with her sling, and her foot set flat on the stones. She had been there since noon. She had looked at nobody who went in at that door or came out of it, and she had missed nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:279:He ate it standing, and tasted none of it. Nobody came to him. Brom looked across once from the bench under the window, and got up, and climbed the ladder to the loft. On the platform by the western gate a lean man was watching the valley, as he did at every hour of every day. Cael had been left alone with a bowl by four people who knew when a person was not fit to be spoken to; and when the bowl was empty he went to break the ice for the geese, since it wanted doing, and since it could be done by somebody who was not thinking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:9:She took a sack by the ladder's head, and sat on the edge of it with her boots drawn under her, as she had sat at a board with a goat on it. Brom drew the ladder up, since it had become the custom of that loft, and Karis lit the hold's lantern with a point; and for the first time since a line was crossed there were six of them under one roof with nobody else to hear.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:13:"Lira's had that since dusk. The rest of you have it now. It's fourteen days late, and I'm sorry for every one of them." He looked at the square woman by the ladder. "And there's one thing more that I have, from the night of the birthday, which none of you is getting. I read myself, as she set it. What I found is hers before it's anybody's. I'm telling you that it exists, so that nobody learns later that I sat on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:21:"Somebody had to give one. I was the only person there who knew that it was a different ruling for me." He made himself go on. "I spent a quarter of an hour that I'd said I wouldn't, and I let six people stand at a den believing that all six were alike. If I had told you at the stone, at noon, you'd have planned for it. We'd have been in that lane by two hands, and Lira would have two arms."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:23:Nobody softened that for him, and he would have thought the less of them if they had. Lira's right hand had gone to the knot of her sling, and stayed there, and she met his eye across the lantern without any change of face; she had heard it on a gate-walk already, and had told him what she would have ruled.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:31:"His face," said Lira. "On the floor, a foot from mine, over the dog's head. He was choosing. I didn't know between what. Then in the lane the grass leaned on my left hand with nobody in it. And then the air cracked, twenty feet north, and it was my crack."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:35:"Hands," said Karis, and wrote it as she said it. "On the animal that had me. They had not come. They were there. And at the stone I had nine points set dark along the line, which I had told nobody the places of; and he came down out of the grass and went between the fourth and the fifth without touching either."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:65:"So that's what there is," said Brom. He was sitting on the boards with his knees up, and he went through it on his fingers, as the healer went through a round. "Five that stop and one that doesn't. A floor that nobody can put a year to. A stake." He looked round the lantern. "And there's that clerk's paper. What do they know in the west, when it gets to wherever it's going?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:71:The last word lay in the loft like a dropped coin, and for the length of several breaths nobody stooped for it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:73:Cael waited for one of them to join those things together. He had sat in a good many rooms where it would have been done before the lamp was trimmed: a seat would have found a word for it, and a clerk would have written the word down, and by morning there would have been a file. Here six people sat round one lantern with a made floor, and an age that had no ruler, and a boy; and each of them left the three lying apart, as Karis had left them on a trestle, since nobody in that loft knew what lay between them and nobody was willing to pretend.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:95:"That's how you find a handful. One at a time, for money." She nodded toward Hesk's satchel on its nail, where a broker's cream paper lay folded in a book. "Somebody paid a hundred marks to learn whether a crew would come out of the first, and I do not think that he has stopped wanting to know."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:111:"Don't go in without me. Not because I'm any use inside. Because somebody should be outside the line who can put an arm back on."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:115:"Then you'll find me as anybody finds a healer. You ask a carter which gate she's late at."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:137:Cael stood at the loft door for some time afterward, with the frost coming in round his feet and the yard white below him. A boat had gone west on the hundred and second day with a clerk's fair copy in it, and that was eight days ago. He supposed that by now it lay on somebody's desk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:195:"When you've three. I'm on a round. If I'm at a table when you find me, you'll sit on the bench and wait your turn like anybody."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:203:"Asking's the least of it, and it's not the whole. You put your hand on a person when there's something to read *for*. I've never yet laid a finger on anybody to see whether my fingers worked."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:211:*Hers. Three readings of myself, on three different days. Then all of it to her, at once. Nobody on the road, to find out whether I can.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:219:*Nothing out here reads me. And at a line you can mark with a rock, every Path in this camp stops, and mine doesn't. Karis won't write her hypothesis. I won't write mine. We're going to find the other circles. Pike said nobody goes in twice. Tomorrow makes three.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:221:Behind him somebody was coming down the ladder, heavily, with a box.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:227:It was not there, and she had known that it would not be. She had walked that track thirty times with a small brown head at her right shoulder, and her hand had thirty rounds of habit in it; it went out at every turn of the path, and closed on air, and came back. She let it. A person who has set a great many bones does not expect a habit to knit in a morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:273:There was a piece of her craft in him, all the same. She knew that as she knew a mend of her own on somebody else's leg. She had felt it go along beside her and then go on alone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:279:She had crossed it eight times in the grey, where nobody was to see, and one person had seen and held his tongue for twelve days. She had gone in with her palms up, and with two fingers on her own wrist, and backward, and with her eyes shut; and she had stood in it for a count of a hundred with her arms hanging. Each time it had been the same. On one side of a grey stone she was a Tide, Iron, of the fourth rank, with a reserve like a purse. On the other she was a woman in a dead man's coat who could tie a knot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:285:So she would be at the edge. There would be another of those places; the small one had as good as promised it. Somebody ought to stand on the right side of the stone with her sleeves pushed up.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:293:He would read himself twice more. He was frightened of what he had found, and he would do it regardless, since he had been told to. Then he would sit on some bench of hers and wait his turn, and say it; and she would be the first living person to hear what the thing that she read with had made of the one body that it could not read.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:55:"I was wrong about apples." Karis rested her throat, and went on. "I looked into one basket, on one day, and I said *two*. A man who had kept those trees for twenty-six years showed me three. This is one basket." Her hand went round the eleven strips. "I have one floor. If a hook is cut a little wide on the ninth stone, I cannot tell whether that is a different kind, or the same kind a little wider. Nobody could, from here. So I shall count. And I shall not number the kinds from one site."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:79:She had kept it for the doubtful ones, and there were a good many grains in it. A hook with two bars across the stem, and on another stone a hook with two bars set a hair closer. A long stroke crossed at a slant, and a long stroke crossed at a slant that leaned a little the other way. She had dropped each of those into the bowl, and gone on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:85:She could still taste that apple. A small bald man had been talking about fruit, and it was the best sentence on method that anybody had said to her since Ternhall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:95:She did want to. She had been waiting all her life for a wall that nobody had read. She had crossed a Line for it, and a mountain, with three boxes; she had knelt on that wall with a pencil while the sun went down, and paid for the last two sheets with a quarter of an hour that a friend had no right to give her. It had never once occurred to her that when the wall came she would not be able to count it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:105:Cael had been watching her from the trestle for two mornings, in the breaths between slips. The bench under her window was never empty now. The carts from the upper holds had begun to come down for their barley, a cart or two each day, and every one of them brought somebody who had been saving a hurt for the healer's round: a hand, a back, a child who would not eat. She went along them at first light and again when the shadow reached the well. He had counted five mendings in two days and a good many more that were only looking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:107:She had not spoken to him since the yard. She had sat at a board with him, and had eaten a goat; it was not the same thing, and he did not make the mistake of taking it for one. A person may sit at a table with somebody whom she has not let off, as she may mend a man whom she does not like.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:117:"It isn't too many. It's hide by now." She let the hand go. "You've the hook on your belt. Keep it there. That bow's no good to anybody with its hook in my pannier, and I ought to have known it at noon on that hill."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:129:She put no question about the lines. She had seen a table with somebody else's work on it, and had given it the one look that a person gives to a trade which is not hers; and Cael, who had been read for most of his life by people who could not leave a strange thing alone, thought that he had never seen it done so cleanly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:141:"The same, if nothing breaks. A round never gives a day back." She pushed herself off the door-post. "Eleven's a good deal. I've been worse. I've been late twelve times, and nobody died of it that I know of."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:149:It was slow and dry, and it came from the western gate. He had never before seen that man on the ground at noon without a reason. The bowman crossed the frozen yard as he had crossed it once with a peeled stick, without hurry, and he had something long under his arm in a goatskin with the hair still on it. He came to the barn door and stopped, and his eyes went over the paper ring without the least curiosity and found the person kneeling in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:165:She had been putting it off. She had a leaf to rule for the box, the first fair leaf of all this work, and a fair leaf wants a heading, since it is by the heading that some stranger will one day find it; and every heading that she tried had the wrong thing in it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:209:It was grey in the barn door by then, on the hundred and fifth morning. She wiped the nib on the hem of Seln's coat, which she would have to answer for, and laid the pen in its groove. Somebody's boots were on the ladder over her head.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:289:Cael looked at two words on a leaf, and he knew as well as anybody living what a name could do to a thing. A clerk had once put one on a boy, in capitals, where a tier should have gone; and from that morning nobody had needed to ask what the boy was, since there was a word on a file that answered for him. A name of that sort shut a door. This was the first that he had ever watched being made which had been cut to keep the door open.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:291:She let her own sentence lie where it had fallen; she had said a thing about a word, and no more. She drew the leaf back, and said the name over once more, aloud, exactly as she had said it the first time. Then she wrote it at the head of the fair leaf, and the box had its first heading.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:293:Out in the yard somebody was walking toward the western gate, flat-footed, with one arm tied.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:5:It was the leaf of the trials. She had ruled it fair overnight, in ink, and she held it to the light of the shutter and gave it to them row by row, in the order in which five people had stepped over a grey stone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:11:"A Shadow, Bronze, who sought it and found that it was not there to be asked. A Wind: Iron, first rank, who asked for the whole of it and was given a step." Her voice had lost most of its hinge, and what was left of it creaked only on the long words. "And a Tide: Iron, fourth rank, with a count that stopped at four and nothing in her hands. That makes five Paths, and three tiers, and five people who had never before agreed about anything."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:19:"They read like five because we are five. Take away the words, and look at what is under them. An Ember can see every place and cannot reach; a Skin knows where the thing is kept and has no room to fetch it from; and you could have done that step again exactly, and there was nothing to take hold of." She touched each row in turn. "In every one of them the doing is all still there, and what answers the doing is gone. It is one loss, told from five chairs."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:41:"A personal spiritual entity should not fail the way five looms fail when somebody shuts the sluice on the wheel that drives them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:43:It lay on the cold air among the sacks.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:67:"Is that fairly put?" she said to him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:87:Lira got up off the clay and came out into the light on her flat foot, and stood with her right hand at her side. She did not offer the sling, and Cael saw why: she had been told that the arm was to be let alone, and she had taken that to cover everybody, the healer included.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:105:"It doesn't want hands. It's knitting, and it wants time, and letting alone. It wants somebody by who'd know the day it turned, if it means to turn, since nobody else in this hold could tell that from a bruise." She pulled the wool up again and tied her knot. "A mend that deep is all one thing until it's old. If a corner of it goes on a road, with nobody there to see, she's lost the hand by the snow."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:131:She had gone up the ladder behind the lean man with her right hand and both knees, and had stood up on the planks with the whole valley in front of her. There was the stretch, a mile of pale frozen grass between two shoulders of hill. There was the white stone, a hundred and ten yards down it. And forty paces out from the gate somebody had driven a stake, and hung a sack on it that was stuffed with straw, with a ring burnt on the sacking as big as a plate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:135:It was a bow, and it was not his kind. His own leaned against the fence, as tall as he was. This was short and thick, an ash stock as long as her arm with a bow of horn and wood lashed across the nose of it, and an iron stirrup, and a long lever underneath. It was very old. Somebody had kept it oiled.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:149:So that was how it would be. She would never loose a bolt that somebody else had not bent his back for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:209:"Oldest to the right," she said. "This first is a district hall's, and I had it off the step myself; it was dressed in my grandmother's time. The pale one is from a library that I used to be allowed into, and the honey-coloured is Ostrand's wall. And that is the oldest dressed stone between Denvash and the Line that anybody can put a year to. A mason was re-laying a drain at the foot of the old gate, the one that the city counts its years from. I call it the founding stone, and I have never owned anything older."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:239:She held the knife up to the shutter. The last hair of the point was turned over, and shone.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:259:"The breaks are worse," said Karis. "Every one of those eleven is broken, and I looked at every break that I could reach. There is not a sharp place on one of them. They are as round as river stones, and nothing runs over the top of a pillar twice Brom's height but air."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:271:He had heard that word from her mouth before, in a reading room above Ostrand, and she had shut it afterward like a box. Nobody at the trestle said it after her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:327:"You looked." He had his eyes on the white stone. "Thirty years on this walk. Nobody looks up to see what's done on it. They look to see if I've an arrow on."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:329:"That's not a reason to teach somebody."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:341:*What am I becoming, if nobody's measuring.* She had asked that at a fire, and nobody had been able to tell her. She did not know now. But there had never been such a thing before as a Wind who could not run, and might not catch, and shot another person's bow off a rail with one arm; and she thought that she would like to see what that was by the time there was snow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:23:Nobody proposed to go and fetch it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:49:Nobody said *yours*. It was the one word that Cael listened for through the whole of that day, and he did not hear it from any of them. Lira had said it once, under the stars, and put it on a shelf; and the other three seemed to have agreed among themselves, without a hand raised, that it was hers to take down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:57:*Nobody has asked me anything. I keep waiting to be glad.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:59:*The nine: held. Deployment since the Line: thirteen.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:77:She let herself have that, for the length of a few breaths, since nobody else was going to give it to her. Twenty yards on a thing as round as a rail, in a boot like a bucket, on a leg that she had been forbidden to run on, with two dogs under her heels and one beside her knee; and she had been pulling away from all three. There had been nothing in it but legs. She had felt thirteen. She had felt the lanes at Fenmark come up under her as if they had only been waiting.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:85:She had been looking at Karis. There was a dog on Karis, and Seln's knife was coming up too slowly, and the healer had gone rigid under her arm; and on her left hand, where there was nobody, the grass had leaned.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:87:She knew what that was. She had read it in his book before she ever saw it, in a boy's cramped writing, with *sealed* beside it and a mild man's name left out. She had seen it since, a handful of times, and never once in front of anybody who did not already know. It had gone by her elbow like a draught, and then he was on the dog.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:91:Then he had set his feet. She had watched him set them, as she had taught him to in a lane in Ardenmere when they were both too young to know what he was; and the air had cracked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:97:She tried it for jealousy, as a person tries a tooth with her tongue. It was not that, or it was not only that. It was nearer to what she had felt on a gravel bar when she found out that she was fast: a thing that she had been certain of, about how the world was laid out, was not so, and nobody had asked her leave.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:99:*What am I becoming, if nobody's measuring.* She had said that to four people at a fire. In there she had been legs, and the big fool had been a man with a pole, and the cleverest person she knew had been a small woman with a roll of paper in her coat. And he had been everything that he ever was, and a light besides.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:149:It was also the only thing that he had ever watched that woman do for herself. She had not done it for a table. She had got up before the light to do it where nobody would be looking, and she had found that somebody was, and had said nothing, and gone to see to a mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:163:So she walked like a duck, across a country with nothing in it but grass, at the pace of a small brown animal that never hurried and never stopped. She had hated that pace for four days on a mountain. She found now that she could not have gone faster if she had been let, and it was restful, in its way, to have the matter settled by somebody else's legs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:187:It was not a decision. Lira had caught that small person off a ladder, and out of a chair, and once by the collar on the edge of a wash; her left hand knew the way. It was out of the sling to the wrist before any part of her had been consulted. She felt the wool go slack on her neck and the fingers open.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:221:It was the first flame that any of them had lit on the Fallow road since the way out, and Cael noticed that nobody discussed it. They were a day west of a grey stone; the ground under them was only ground, and a flint was not a declaration. Seln struck it. He had gathered dead stems of the pale grass by the armful, since there was nothing else in that country to burn, and they went up with a hiss and wanted feeding every minute.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:233:They all looked at it. In ninety-eight days of road nobody had seen such a thing come off one of their fires.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:259:It came to him that he was being trusted. That was all the silence was. Lira had said *later*, and three people had taken their time from her; and the fifth asked nobody anything that was not a hurt. They were waiting, without making a show of it, for him to have a sentence.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:273:He had known what the other thing was since a yard at Oxhollow. He had heard her describe it, in a healer's words, and had watched four faces change; and after that he had put it where he put the things that he would look at when there was leisure. There had been a letter to ration, and a line to pace, and a ring. For three days there had been an arm in a sling, and a hill that nobody spoke of. He had been glad of every one of them, and he saw now what he had been glad of them for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:283:They came down to the fork an hour after noon, and up the valley with the smoke of a hold standing over the fence in front of them. The east gate was open. He had gone out by it five days before with five other people and a calfskin, to pick up something that somebody had left lying.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:287:He had no business at that gate. He kept the western one, and in three stays Cael had seen him leave his platform once, to watch six people go out by the east; and he was standing beside the east gate now, with his bow at the length of his arm, like a man who has been coming to the same place at the same hour for five days. He looked at nobody but Lira. He watched her come up the last fifty yards on the flat of her foot, with a strip of grey wool across her and a hand tucked under her collarbone, and he did not lift two fingers from the stave. He took off his cap.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:315:She took the first band at his breastbone, where its end was, and drew it off round his side to the spine in one pull, as she had laid it on. It came with a sound like a page tearing. The other four followed. He had expected to feel something give, and he felt only cold air on a place that had not had any for eighteen days.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:347:"I meant—" He did not know what he had meant. He had lain on a table at Oxhollow and felt an instrument close on air, and he had wanted, with a wanting that he had not written down, for this one to close on something. "Will you get anything?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:351:She was not being short with him. It was only how a healer's table was run: nobody was promised what the hands would find; they were told afterward, whatever it was, to their faces.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:369:They got up, the five of them, and went back to whatever they had been doing; nobody on that bench had ever argued with her about a day. Cael stood by the well with his shirt in his hand and the cold on his ribs, and watched five people give up a morning to him that not one of them knew they were giving.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:371:It was the same yard. He had not looked at it when he came in. There was the sweep of the well, and the cart-yard, and a boy forking straw into the barn under the loft where two boxes waited; the geese went by in a line, and somebody in the long house was scouring a pot. He had gone out of this place five days ago and stood on a floor that matched no stone in the world, and held a hound's head, and carried a light down a hill. Nothing here had altered by so much as a goose, and he found that he had been counting on that without knowing it.

