   271	
   272	### 7.2 Front-loaded teaching (owner formula §4)
   273	
   274	Target ≈58 progression-vocabulary hits per 10k words overall (≈1,740 over 300k), front-loaded about 1.7×: **first third ≈82 per 10k (≈820 hits), middle ≈43 (≈430), last ≈49 (≈490).** The first third does most of the teaching:
   275	- The winter inventory and practice (fragment names, costs, the seal, "deployment notes").
   276	- The board's ranking (eleventh; Teague's four at the top; "the board doesn't ask what a crew was").
   277	- Tiers and Paths in Teague's crew, in Vastin, and in Treswick's machinery (Copper/Iron/Bronze/Silver; passes; tier gates; the smith's two forges; the provision's columns).
   278	- Iron Wall's first showing; the records' grade; the reading inward.
   279	
   280	After the first third, progression language surfaces at turning points: the three notices, the grade, the court's line.
   281	**Measurement caution (as OWNER-DECISIONS #14):** the last third carries **grade vocabulary** (first-, second- and third-tier; the mark; the stroke and bar). Counted as progression vocabulary, it inflates the closing third. Report two counts at each review, with and without grade/taxonomy terms.
   282	
   283	### 7.3 Development cadence (owner formula §5)
   284	
   285	- **Cael:** about 4.5 realization/decision beats per 10k words, ≈135 across the book, spread evenly with a slight middle bump (≈42 / 48 / 45). That is about 15 per movement, so roughly one every 2,200 words.
   286	- **Supporting cast:** many small beats each (§3.D), 0.1–0.7 per 10k per person. No single deuteragonist takes the budget. Karis and Vastin run highest (≈14 each across the book); Pike and the young one run lowest.
   287	
   288	### 7.4 Prose and rhythm targets (formula §2–3, §8–9; measured after each movement with `tools/formula_metrics.py`)
   289	
   290	**Source baseline** (measured 2026-10-01 over all 24 chapters):
   291	
   292	| Metric | Source | Target |
   293	|---|---|---|
   294	| Sentence mean | 21.3 | 14.6 |
   295	| Sentence median | 8 | 11 |
   296	| Spread (sd) | 27.2 | ~26 |
   297	| Sentences ≤5 words | 39.3% | 27.7% |
   298	| Sentences ≥40 words | **19.1%** | 3.3% |
   299	| Paragraph median | **51** | ~18 |
   300	| Paragraph mean | 66.4 | ~26.8 |
   301	| Words per scene | 808 | ~950 |
   302	| Scene breaks per 10k | 10.0 | ~8.7 |
   303	| Flesch Reading Ease | 85.0 | 72.3 |
   304	| FK grade | 6.7 | 6.8 |
   305	
   306	The source's signature is the accumulating catalogue sentence, chained with "and … and … because …". That produces both the 19% of sentences over forty words and the paragraphs of fifty-plus words, while very short beats pull the median and the Flesch score up. **For this edition:**
   307	- Break the catalogue chains into well-built compound and complex sentences of 12–25 words.
   308	- Keep a few deliberate forty-plus sentences per chapter where accumulation is the effect, such as an inventory or a crossing.
   309	- Bring paragraphs down toward a median of 18 by ending the paragraph when the thought turns.
   310	- Keep short punch sentences for landings, not as default (EDITION_BRIEF rhythm calibration).
   311	- Hold a scene break roughly every 950 words.
   312	- Drop dialogue tags where the speaker is clear.
   313	- Adverbs and fillers are to taste: do not chase "that".
   314	
   315	**Logs are exempt from the chain-breaking only where §10 fixes their text.**
