editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:265:Her right foot rolled outward, and went on rolling. It went past the place where an ankle stops, and she heard it go. It made a small wet sound, like a green twig bent double, and she knew that sound. She had heard it once at Fenmark from a girl two places above her on the list, who had not been on the list the term after.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-10.md:281:She had one thing to say, and she picked it with care. There were dogs still on that gravel, and she would not have him kneeling over an ankle.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:5:Brom would not have Cael touch it, and for once there was no arguing with him, since Cael could not have lifted a kettle. So Seln drove the ash haft of the bill under the slab's edge and bore down on it, and Brom knelt in the shale and got his right shoulder beneath the corner that rose. He kept the left arm folded against his chest. The stone came up by the breadth of a hand, grating, and the whole slope behind it stirred.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:73:"Yes." She thought about it. "It's a different hurt from the ankle. That one was a thing I'd done, and this is only a thing that's there."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:91:He had the book, for Hesk's satchel had been on his shoulder when the mountain came, as it was always on his shoulder, and it had gone under him when he fell and taken no harm. He opened it once, in the grey of the morning, and found the page with the last entry. It ended on the word *Sleep*, and above that he had written that the job had no fight at the end of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:153:He heard it then, a small hard clink, and another, as even as a clock. A little after that Seln came round the bend of the ledge, under its roof. He was alone, and he walked no faster than he ever did; but he came straight over the shale to the end of the wall, and for once he gave his report before he had sat down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:159:Her eyes went over the five of them a single time, and it did not take her as long as it takes to count five. He saw them go to the slates on a leg, and to a dark cloth at a wrist, and to the way that he himself was sitting, with one arm shut against his side; and they rested longest on Karis, who had nothing wrong with her that could be seen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:171:"You're not. I can see your leg from here." The woman put the cheek-strap into Seln's hand without troubling to see whether he would take it. "Water, somebody. I've had these on a mule since before it was light, and I'll not put them on that as they are."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:175:He began with the hands, since she was holding them out. They were broad and brown, and the nails had been cut as short as nails can be. She was not tall, but she was square in the shoulder, and she wore a man's coat of some thick brown stuff that had been made for a bigger person and taken in. Her hair was cropped as short as a boy's. He thought that she was perhaps ten years older than himself, and he could not have said why he was sure it was not more. On top of the mule's pack, lashed along it in an oiled cover, there was something with the shape of a crossbow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:215:She said it to Lira, plainly, as a ferryman says that the next step is wet. Then she set one hand above the place and one at the ankle, over the dirty linen, and leaned back with her whole weight, steadily, as one draws a bucket.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-20.md:305:"That isn't mine to mend, for nothing's broken. It fills again or it doesn't, and it fills quicker asleep and fed. Don't ask it for so much as a spark for a week." She got to her feet with a hand on the wall, and her eyes went to Seln. "Those were your scratches, on the lip."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:85:They could go. The near channel was open behind them, twenty yards of green rock under fast water, with a hole beside it. One of them could not stand, and must be carried over that; the man to carry her had one arm; and a third could not close her hands on a sleeve. They would do it in the dark, with their backs to three animals that had just shown how well they could learn, and somewhere on that rock somebody would slip. He knew what Iron Skin did when a load shifted and there was no time to choose, and he had watched a Wind burst go off by itself on a river bar.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:167:He had written it in Hesk's book a dozen times, and had never before stood in it with something coming. *One*, and every fragment he owned went dumb. There were no hands to put down. For that one count he was a boy standing in a river with a knife, who could not have shifted his feet for the forty marks.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:247:Seln was shaking so hard that he could not get his knife back into its sheath. Brom looked at that for some time; then he worked his own coat off, one-armed, with his teeth set, and put it round him, and Seln disappeared into it to the knees.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:299:She unlaced it herself. Lira's face went grey and then greenish, and she held to the edge of a manger with both hands and did not make a sound, while the woman felt along the bones of the foot with her thumbs, and up the ankle on both sides, and took a long time over it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:361:He had Hesk's book on his knee, and the day's heading written, before the woman's lantern had stopped swinging on its nail.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:407:*Brom: left forearm, wrist to mid-arm, fourteen stitches. Lira: right ankle, bound to a stave; not broken, which is worse. Karis: both palms. Mine: left calf, in and out. Seln: nothing, and an hour and a quarter in a river.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-11.md:427:"I put that you had no burst. And your ankle."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:9:Lira had gone on ahead. She had not eaten. She had wound the linen round her right ankle by the first light there was, and tried her weight on it, and walked away along the foot of the wall with her arms swinging loose. By the time the cart was a hundred yards out she was a small dark figure at the place where the bay shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:31:They set the shafts down. Karis took the cord from her shoulder and began to free the first knot, since the ledge would want measuring as soon as it had been run. Seln straightened his back with both hands. It was very still. The stream was only a breath of sound from far below, and the cold had laid a quiet on the hill that Cael had not heard there on any other morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:227:She had almost reached it. She lay on her front in the grey spill of the other stream, with one arm stretched toward the rock and her cheek on the shale, and from the knees down he could not see her. A plate of the mountain lay across her legs. It was as long as a door and as thick as his wrist.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:245:So she bound the ankle in the dark, on the seventy-eighth morning, and ate nothing, as she had eaten nothing before a card. The foot was what it was. It bent less than the other and later, and she had stopped being angry with it somewhere on the bench and begun simply to allow for it, as one allows for a wind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:317:Something the size of a cart went behind her so close that the wind of it turned her half round. She kept her legs. She was three strides from the stone, with her arm already out for it, when the shale that the thing had brought down with it took her ankles from under her like a wave on a beach, and she went onto her face.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:323:It went below the knee, with a duller sound than the ankle had made at a river. She knew at once that it was a different thing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-19.md:329:She could not have said how long it was before the boy came. He came along the lower side of the rock, on his feet and hardly so, with one arm shut against his ribs and his left leg dragging; and he was the colour of the dust. He got down beside her by letting the wall take him. His eyes went over her a piece at a time, as they always did. They reached the plate, and stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:29:He had no Path of his own, and so no tier and no rank, and he said so to himself plainly, because the dark was a good place for plain things. Everybody else he knew had come away from a Kindling at fourteen with one: a single gift, which an Arbiter named and a registry ranked ever after. His own Arbiter had woken too, on a brass plate in a windowless room, and it had taken eleven seconds over him and gone dark, and it had been dark ever since. What answered him now were nine fragments, which was the log's word for them: abilities taken out of other people's Paths, each one inside a fight that somebody else had begun in earnest, and each one written into Hesk's book with a name beside it and a price.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:37:He took Hesk's clock out of its stocking and tilted the face to the embers, and it said a quarter past the hour. It lost, as it always had, and a researcher with her pencil out had ruled two days ago that it was right by definition, being the only clock east of a line. He supposed the watch was over.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:159:Cael had the two letters inside his coat. He had carried them two days in the pocket of Hesk's book, where a Gold's letter had lain for most of a year, and he had not expected to be rid of them before a town. He walked down the bank to her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:197:*Fourth day. The letters to Hesk and to Vell went west by a courier on a mule, a copper each, as far as the turf fire. She showed us the ford by riding it. She did not ask what the letters were. She did not ask what we were either. Entered, because I can't remember the last person who didn't.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:221:The gelding's shoes rang on the stones. It had not mattered on the upland, where there was turf, and Cael had not heard it in the ruts. But the road was hard now, and wherever rock came through it the iron struck, and the valley gave the sound back. He caught Seln listening to it, with his head a little on one side.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-01.md:255:"You have it now. For four strides you had not, and by the second it was both feet." Seln walked on a little. When he spoke again it was no louder, and the others, at the horse's head, could not have heard. "I should say that I know it. I learned it on a stair, as a boy, by wanting very much not to be heard by the person at the top. It took me a year to do on purpose and three more to stop doing by accident. It always came at dusk, when the eye is worst and the ear must do the work."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:11:He was a short, thick man, burnt as brown as a nut, and his cart was loaded to the rails with sacks. He chocked his wheels, saw to the mule, and sat down in the ring with a yard of empty ground on either side of him. Cael had seen him once already that day, at noon, handing a slip across the plank; and he had watched the meadow read that slip all afternoon, crew after crew, and go back to its dinner.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:139:"I shall say where I want each of you, and I should be obliged if nobody argued until we are at the far end." His voice was hardly louder than the wind. "I go ahead, thirty paces, on the left verge. The boy and Lira walk ten paces in front of the mule, one to a rut, and you do not look at each other; you look outward. The carter keeps his beast's head. Karis is at its off shoulder, where the cart is between her and whatever is nearest. Brom is behind the tailboard."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-05.md:157:A little way past that, on the left, something stood up out of the grass that was not grass. It was a cart-shaft. There was only the one, grey and split, pointing at the sky at the angle of a man's raised arm, and whatever it had once been fastened to lay down in the stems where nobody could see it. The carter did not turn his head as they went by. Karis did, and then looked very steadily at the mule's ears.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-18.md:145:"I walked round it this afternoon. The shale is heaped against the upper side of it to the height of my chest, and it has gone round both ends, as water goes round a pier. On the lower side there is bare floor, a piece as large as this camp, with nothing lying on it at all." Seln turned his hands over to warm the backs. "That has not happened once. A slide has come down this face and gone round that rock a good many times, and never yet filled the place behind it. I should have slept there, if it had water. I do not care to carry a skin seventy yards across loose stone in the dark."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:5:She had gone up to the eave on purpose to be first. Cael came after her at his own pace, which on level grass was anybody's pace now, and he took his inventory on the way, as he took it at the start of anything. The calf was a leg again on the flat; he had asked it the evening before, on the saddle road, and it had told him nine strides uphill and then shut. Brom's forearm was closed along the whole of its seam and wrapped by Brom's own choice, and it was to pull nothing. Lira had wound a strip of linen round her right ankle, over the stocking, and she walked on the whole sole of that foot. Karis had two pink lines across each palm and held a pencil for a quarter of an hour at a time. The nine fragments lay in him as they had lain since the ford, idle, each with its rent paid.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:61:The second was that nobody's finger was on it. The last large slip they took had carried a captain's name in capitals. A one-armed man had struck that name with his own pen before a meadow, and they had gone down to a river under the weight of it. This one had been posted for anyone at all, with no hold on it. If they took it they would take it off nobody, and the captain at the top of the sheet could read it when he came down and find nothing in it that had been his.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:69:It was work, besides, that four half-mended people could do. A man with a bitten calf could pace a level shelf and write. A girl with a stiff ankle could tell him whether a hundred yards of it would bear her, and a researcher who could hold a pencil for a quarter of an hour had a cord, and a clock, and a head that kept numbers as a jar keeps water. Nobody would have to be fast, or hard, or to set anything alight; the job asked for no Path from any of them, and for no fragment of his.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:71:He wanted that job more than he had wanted the forty. It surprised him, and he entered it as a want, since Hesk had taught him that a figure left out of a sum did not thereby cease to be owed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:93:"I said it as I say everything." But he had not, quite, and Cael saw Seln's mild eyes rest on the bald brown head for a breath longer than the answer needed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:107:He knew what it would be. A word had been dropped at that plank a quarter of an hour ago, in a voice that was not quite Pike's ordinary one, and Seln collected such things as other men collected debts. He would be asking what a shale-back was, and who had been under one, and what had become of them, and he would get it in fewer sentences than anybody else could have.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-15.md:161:The cart went up to the saddle between two people. It had no beast, for there was not a mule in Lowmarch to be hired for a mountain by a crew that might not bring it back. Seln took the near shaft and Cael the off, and they leaned, and the narrow wheels went round. On the flat of the meadow it was nothing. On the hill Cael counted nine strides and felt the calf begin to close, and at the tenth Lira's shoulder came in beside his without a word, and took the shaft, and he fell back to the tail and walked with his hand on a box until the leg forgave him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:35:At the first reading he saw only that the same three words stood inside and out. Hesk had put them at the head of the sheet as another man would have put the name of a street; it was where Cael lived now, and the old man had written it down without a word of comment and gone on to the next thing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:41:He had never had one. He turned that over, sitting on the bank with the river going past: he was Hesk's ward; it was the second half of the name they wrote on forms, and he had known it since he could know anything. He knew a shop, and a bench under a window, and a clock that lost. There had been no woman in that house, and nobody had ever spoken of one; and it had not occurred to him to ask, because certain doors there had always stood shut in a way that told a child they were not his to try.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:73:*Sixtieth day. Lowmarch. A letter from Hesk, with nothing on the outside but my name and "past the Line". It found me because there is only the one board. He writes that my grandmother crossed the Line once, before I was born. It is the first I have heard that I had one. I don't know her name. I have set down what the letter says, and nothing that I might guess.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:163:Seln was gone before light on the sixty-first day. He came back at dusk, and sat down in his own place, a little behind the rest, and held out his hands to the fire. There was pale dust on his boots to the ankle, the dust of the south road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:195:Brom had been forbidden to pull anything, and nobody had said that he might not let a thing down. He took the candle on its string, and Seln lit it with his flint, at a halting-ground, on a road. Brom lowered it over the curb with his right hand, and called the knots as they went through his fingers.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:359:She had been trying the foot without it since the well, a few steps at a time, behind a cart or at the river's edge, wherever she judged that nobody could see. Nothing had mended. The hold's woman had told her how the ankle would be, and it was being so; and to run on it was a risk, which she had looked over as she looked over a draw, and taken.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:377:"That's all it is. It still does what an ankle does; it does rather less of it, and it's a beat behind." She scuffed the gravel with the foot in question. "I know what that would cost me on a card. Iron, Rank One, with a foot that's a beat behind. I can work it out to a tenth."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:443:*Mine: the calf is a leg on level ground and gives nine strides uphill. Lira: no stick since the sixty-fourth. She runs. The ankle bends less, and later; those are her words, and I have not counted since the first run. Brom: shut, not sound, and wrapped. He has carried nothing for sixteen days and I think it is the hardest thing he has done past the Line. Karis: a pencil, yesterday. Seln: nothing to report, which he reported.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-14.md:449:*Hesk's letter is not answered. No roof.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:39:"I asked him that in those words. He said, the kind a man would not be on a road." Seln spread his hands a little. "He means all three, and he does not know which. It is the rule that Thornwater keeps, for the reason that Thornwater gave us."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:47:"Then we'll do it when there's nothing above us. He's looked." He turned to Seln. "You have looked?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:53:"With an arm," said Karis. "One arm up is *walk*, and both is *hold*. I can't pull on anything, so I shall stand where everybody can see me and be the voice." She looked at her two pink palms. "It's the first use I've found for them since a river."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:59:It did not slide. The whole surface gave a little, with a dry clinking, as a shingle beach gives under a boot, and settled again about his ankles. He stood in it with his arms out. Then he climbed ten yards, slowly, and each step sank and slipped back half its length, so that he seemed to be wading upstream.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:93:He took the third box after it, in the same manner, while the light went yellow on the far wall. Lira carried the stores, two steps and a slide, and would not be told that a bound ankle had no business on loose plates. By the last of her journeys the cart stood at the foot of the fan with nothing in it but its own seat board, and looked like a thing that had been robbed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:155:"Four, and a piece," said Karis, with Hesk's clock in her hand. "I heard it land, and then I heard it land again." She looked at the far wall with respect. "I shall have to take the first."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-16.md:163:Cael said nothing to that. He had stopped counting her runs on a gravel bar, and he did not begin again; but it cost him something to hear her set her own ankle against a mountain as though it were a price she had already agreed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-24.md:143:Cael had no other word for it. He had watched that face for eighty days, and it was never still; it looked at a thing and then looked again, and something in it went on working like a mill between the two looks. Now the one arm lay on the book and the brown eyes rested on a sheet of cream paper, and nothing in the face moved at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:57:Oryn had loaded the mule in the hollow. Two of the boxes hung from the pack-saddle, one on either flank, and her own panniers were lashed on top with the crossbow in its cover. She took the animal by the cheek-strap and walked it to the edge of the break, and there she changed her hand and put herself on the outside.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:71:Lira got up. She sat the pack-saddle as she would have sat a fence, sideways, with her new leg hanging and her bare foot turned in; and she fixed her eyes on the roof of rock over the far ledge and kept them there while she was taken across between a wall and a healer. When Seln lifted her down she said something short to him that Cael could not hear, and Seln bowed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:155:She saw the ankle from below, walking, on the second morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:183:Lira looked straight in front of her, over the long brown ears, and Cael watched her take it. He had seen her take a result before, on a painted floor, with a sheet of figures to tell her how far she had fallen short. There was no sheet here. There was a square woman with tired eyes, walking below a mule, who had spent what she had on the leg above that ankle, and had said to her face what the rest was worth and to whom it was owed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:191:They halted at noon, on a floor of turf beside a stream, with the road in sight half a mile off, two pale ruts going north-west; and there she showed her. It was a craft, like any other, and Cael watched it as he would have watched a new guard. One turn went round the shin above the ankle, for an anchor; then a strip ran from the inside of that, down under the heel, and up the outside, pulled hard.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-21.md:309:"You'll be the Lowmarch crew that took my paper. A carter said the one-armed man had given it to five." He looked past them, at the empty road, and at the river. "Where's your cart?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:129:He did not try to drag it off her, since a thing that is pulled bites down. He had Lira's knife, and he could not spend the blade either, with two small fists lying under those teeth. He gave it the other end. He put the horn of the hilt in at the back of the jaw, crosswise, where Seln had put a flat blade on a white floor, and bore down on it as on the handle of a pump.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:163:The landing locked. *One*, and everything that he owned went dumb; there was a boy standing in a field with a knife, who could not have shifted a foot to save his life. He had priced that count before he spent it. The thrown one was on the south side, finding its legs, and the rest were strung along sixty yards of lane. The nearest of them was a good deal more than one count from his ankles.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-32.md:215:*One. Two.* For ten counts after a lane, everything that he had answered him a beat late. He had written it so in Hesk's book, on a road, from a bout. It was a different thing to run in. His foot came down, and then he felt it come down. The flame leaned, and then he saw it lean.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:5:She said it from the tail of the carter's cart, where she sat with the ash stick across her knees and her bound foot propped on the left-hand box. The cart was empty but for folded sacks and three boxes under their canvas, and it stood in the yard at Thornwater with the mule in the shafts and noon coming on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:97:"The hold stands," he said. "For everything any of us carries, on any road, and I'm not changing a word of that." He turned to Seln. "It doesn't cover a tinderbox any longer."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:191:Brom's mouth opened, and shut again. He had a question of his own that was first cousin to hers, and he had been carrying it since a jug; Cael could see him hold it up beside hers and conclude that it did not fit. Karis could have given an answer by method, with a table in it, and she had the sense to know that it was not what had been asked. Seln looked at his hands.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:203:He took out Hesk's book instead, since it was the one honest thing he had by him. He wrote the day, and the place, and under them her question, word for word as she had put it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-13.md:249:"I do not know. I should think that there is a reason, and that it is not a pleasant one." Seln reached up and drew the tack, and it was the first slip that Cael had ever seen him touch. "It is half a day off, on level ground. The work at a well is done sitting, for the most part, or else hanging; and we are a crew with three good legs among five. I am the one of us who can hang."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:27:Cael weighed it. Thirty animals stood three hundred and fifty yards up a slope of loose plates, and the five of them had a cart, and a rule in a one-armed man's words. To go back was to go down the fan, and to go on was to do for one afternoon what carters did every year.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:63:They gave it half an hour by Hesk's clock, with the fire built up to the size of a hat, and thirty beaks rasped on in the dark overhead.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:81:Cael sat on her left with the notebook, and Lira lay on the rock at her right, on her stomach again. Brom stayed under the roof with the cart, and Seln had gone on westward before any of them woke. The sun had not yet reached into the gorge, and thirty heaps of grey stone lay along the upper shelf with frost on their backs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-17.md:231:"The other one I gave you because I couldn't hold a pencil. This I can write myself, and I have. But it's derived, and a derived thing in one hand is an opinion." She was looking at the leaf, and not at Hesk's book, which she had never read. "You had the far end of the line. If you'll put down what you saw there, in your own words, then it's two people's."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:5:She did not go alone, since Seln had made a rule and she had lost no time in seeing the sense of it. Lira had the watch with her. Lira sat down in the grass ten yards short of the place, with her back turned and her knife across her knees, to look at everything that was not a ring of stones; and Karis went on the last ten yards by herself, with a notebook, and a pencil, and no lamp.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:183:From the south he had the whole of it in one view, for the first and only time. The grey stone was away on his left hand, with a small brown mule beside it. Beyond it the ruts swung out round the north in their wide curve. The floor lay in the middle of everything, with its eleven broken stones, and on the far side, where no turf had been cut, there was the low place in the grass where Seln had knelt.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-28.md:229:"I do not know what the number counts, and I shall not guess." Seln rose. He stood with his hands behind him and looked from the stake to the ring, and back along the line of turfs to the stone, as he would have looked over a room that somebody had left in a hurry. "It stands a boot's width from where she stopped. She found that place with her feet, this morning, knowing nothing. Whoever drove this found the same place to the same boot's width."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:185:He saw the foot go over. It went outward, past the place where an ankle stops, as it had gone on round stones at a river and under a plate of shale; and that made three times.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:247:He was thirty inside his time. He came over the lip with a long thing under one arm, out of its cover, and no breath to speak with. He gave the bow to Brom, and stood holding his own knees.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:283:The jaws had not opened. Seln knelt, and put the flat of the narrow dull knife between the teeth at the hinge, and Cael took the upper jaw in both hands, and it was done as a trap is opened. Lira made one sound while they did it, and it was not loud.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-31.md:291:Brom carried her the four strides, on one arm, with the bow in the other hand, and Oryn was on her knees in the grass before Lira's back had touched it. Both her hands went round the arm, above the place and below it, as they had gone round a shin on a mountain, and her eyes shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:51:He got onto the ash by stages. The light was going yellow, and the others had drawn in without being asked; Karis sat on the end of the bench with her notebook shut, and Brom stood behind her, and Seln was a darker place against the gate-post. Lira had not moved from the trough.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:115:She did the rest of them in the time it took to load a mule. She looked at Lira's foot, and at how the long strip crossed in front, and said that it would do. She had Brom's wrist out of its cuff and into it again. She told Karis to eat, and to ask nothing of herself for three days more, not so much as a spark to see by. To Seln she gave a nod, as one gives it to a man of one's own trade, and Seln returned it exactly.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:205:She left that hold at first light on the ninety-first, with two days of road in front of her and the mule going well. And somewhere on the first of those days, between a ford and a stand of the red trees, she found that she had been doing a sum for the better part of a morning without having set herself to it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:291:The boy was sitting on the gravel at the water's edge, alone, with a book on his knee. He held one arm in a little against his left side, and under the shirt, when he breathed, something drew a line across his ribs. There was a healer's tape on him. That meant ribs; and ribs, by her rule, were three weeks under linen, counted from whichever day the mountain had done it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:327:*Mine: three ribs taped, twenty-one days from the seventy-eighth; the calf bound in her cloth. Lira: a leg that is new, an ankle that is taped and will not be mended. Brom: mended, and not to pull. Karis: empty, and filling. Seln: nothing.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-22.md:335:On the far bank four people and two mules were going up out of the river into the dusk, quiet, a crew's length apart. He turned his head the other way. The north road climbed the brown hill behind the hold in one long slant, and a mile up it, small and steady, there was a mule, and on the low side of the mule a woman, walking at a pace that she could keep until dark.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:37:He put them both inside Hesk's book, between the leaves, where a cream sheet had lain the night before. It was a small thing to have decided; but he had gone a long time with nothing in front of him except what he was holding down, and he liked the weight of two unbroken seals a good deal more than he would have liked knowing what was under them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-26.md:143:"And here, a long way south-east, among his water. Strokes all round, and a round of bare skin in the midst, with no road within a day of it." She had ringed both. "Three blank circles. One I can source, since a one-armed man has named it. The other two are a man lifting his hand from a skin. I have written *guess* beside each, and I shall not call them anything else."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:85:"Yes; and a one-armed man who said *a handful*."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:95:"That's how you find a handful. One at a time, for money." She nodded toward Hesk's satchel on its nail, where a broker's cream paper lay folded in a book. "Somebody paid a hundred marks to learn whether a crew would come out of the first, and I do not think that he has stopped wanting to know."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:119:It lay there for as long as it takes to count two. It was the first time that Cael had ever seen her touch that man, who had not been hurt since he crossed the Line and had given her no occasion; and Seln stood quite still under it. Then she took the hand away, without a word, and went down the rungs into the dark. Cael could not have said whether a tired woman had steadied herself at a ladder's head, or whether it had been something else. He did not guess. He had heard her say, not half an hour before, what she did with a thing that she could not read.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:147:He had not slept. He had come down the ladder with Hesk's book inside his shirt, and she was there already, a square shape against the white of the yard, filling a flask from the dipper. The mule stood at the trough with a blanket on her. There was a pannier on the coping with its strap buckled, and a coil of rope beside it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:9:Seln had the locked case as well, shut, on its strap across his chest. He had looked once at the mule's pack, and once at the stone, and had kept it on him, as he had kept it on every road. Everything else that they owned was either in a loft two days west or under the feet of a mule.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:13:So they went in as they had gone round. Brom held one end at the stone, and Karis walked the other out until it came tight, and stopped; then he came up to her, and she went on. The other four walked behind, in file, in the track that the cord left. The grass stood to Cael's hip. It closed over each of them as they passed and did not quite rise again, and when he looked back from the third length there was a lane trodden through it, as straight as a ruled margin, with a grey stone and a small brown mule at the far end.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:167:She numbered the last corner and rolled the sheet, and for once she did not stop to look at what she had got. Brom had set the box at the foot of the tallest stone, on the eastern side, an hour ago, to keep ten sheets of paper out of the north wind. He knelt there now with his back to it and reached behind him for the strap, and Cael went to steady the weight onto his shoulders, since that was a thing that could be done with one arm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-30.md:245:He did not write it. It would not have been true, and he did not yet know what would be. He shut the book on an entry that stopped short, for the first time since he had crossed the Line, and put it away in Hesk's satchel.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:29:Lira had a boot in each hand. One was the carter's left, which she had found standing outside the barn door at first light. She hung it on the mule's pack by its laces, with great care, against the other one going.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:45:They had halted on a level of short turf where a spring came out. Brom had been helped off with the box and lay on his back beside it with his arms spread. The others had gone to the water. Lira had not; she had sat down next to Cael, and stretched out both legs, and was looking at Hesk's book where it lay on his knee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:57:*Filed with your record. It reads: left in good standing, undefeated on paper. I've kept this ledger forty years and never had a line like it. The session still stands. The road still runs through Ardenmere. — V.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:73:He had thought about that on the forty-ninth night, and it came back to him now on a turf bank two days past a fence. There was a woman in Ardenmere who kept a straight ledger of a fighting yard, and a one-armed man in a meadow who kept a straight book of a plank; and neither had the least idea that the other was alive. He was written in both. He did not think that any registry on the continent could have said as much of one of its own.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:107:There was grass. That was nearly the whole of it. It began at the height of his ankle, on the turf by the spring, and by the middle of the afternoon it stood to his knee, and before dusk to his hip: pale stuff, the colour of dry straw, with a seed-head that hissed against his coat. No stock had cropped it. There was not a fence in sight, or a smoke, or a tree, only long slow swells of ground going away to a sky that seemed to begin at his boots. The wind came over it from the north with nothing in its way, and laid the whole of it over in one direction, like the nap of a cloth under a hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:179:"Then we shall have walked to a ring of stones, and a one-armed man has been wrong for thirty years."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:181:They left the mule at the turn, with her rein under a rock. Brom knelt out from under the box and set it beside her. Then the six of them spread across the old line, in a row, and Cael took the right-hand end. Lira was next to him, and then Brom, and Karis, and the healer; Seln had the far end, on the north.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-27.md:185:He counted strides, since somebody would want the number. And he did what a one-armed man had told them to do, though it had not been said to him. He turned his attention inward, to the place behind his breastbone where a small bright thing had gathered on the morning of his Kindling. He had gone looking for it a few times since, in the first months, on bad nights. He looked now. It was as it had always been: a dark place, like a niche in a wall where a lamp once stood lit.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:31:There was one thing more to do before she went under. It was Karis's to do, and Karis could not; she sat in the grass with her two hands at her throat and her eyes on him. She held, as a one-armed man at a plank held, that a thing not said was not a finding; and so he said it for her, aloud, as Pike read a line before he wrote it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:63:Lira did not speak. She had been told once, on a mountain, to lie still and let it, and she lay still, and her right hand was the only part of her that told him anything. At first it had hold of the grass, a whole fistful, with the knuckles standing. A little later it left the grass and found the strap of Hesk's satchel, where that hung from his shoulder beside her, and took two turns of it round the wrist. By the time the oil stood halfway down the fourth finger the strap had gone slack, and the hand lay open on her own breast.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:107:"One arm. From here to the snow."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:113:"The ankle's a tape and a season. I've told you that before."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:119:She got one hand to the ground, to push herself up, and the arm folded. She did not so much lie down as stop being upright. She went over onto her back in the trodden grass, on the old line of the road, with her broad hands open at her sides and her mouth a little open; and she was asleep before Seln had come in out of the dark to put a blanket on her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:147:Then she shut the book. She did not lie down. She was looking at his left leg, which lay stretched toward the lamp with the cloth black from the knee to the boot, and he watched her think it through as she thought through everything. The healer was asleep and would be asleep till morning; Seln had the south and Brom the north, and Lira had one arm. That left a person with two skinned hands and no voice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:151:She bound the calf as she would have ruled a table. He supposed that she had watched a healer do it under a rock, and had kept it, as she kept everything; she was not gentle, and she was very exact, and when she tied it off the knot lay flat. She patted the leg once, absently, as she had patted a box on Brom's back. Then she went and lay down on her side against that box with her back to all of them, in Seln's coat, as she had lain on a mountain.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:185:Karis had looked at the lantern's glass before she slept, and held up three fingers, which was what an hour had left in it. He set it on the lid of the box and opened Hesk's book to the page that had stopped short. The entry there was in his own hand and a few hours old; it said that the dogs were inside, and that he had told nobody, and he read it as he might have read the writing of somebody he had known as a boy.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-33.md:193:*Lira: left forearm bitten through and held; mended at the line, an hour, by lantern. One arm till first snow, by order. Right ankle gone a third time, and not mended. Karis: throat; no voice. Brom: four fingers, on a string. Seln: nothing. Oryn: spent to the bottom, and asleep on the road.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:63:He stood in front of them for as long as it took to be sure what he felt. He had asked for that word once, on a ford, and had it struck with one line while he was on a ledge with a string of salt; and he had never grudged the striking, since a hold cannot wait on a captain's convenience. Now two holds had come down to a ferry and asked a one-armed man who they ought to have. That was the board doing its work. If he minded it, he would have to mind arithmetic.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:201:Brom was looking at his left wrist. Lira had her eyes on the great boot, and on what was taped inside it; she had asked for that ankle once, and been told what it was worth and to whom it was owed. Seln studied his cup. And Cael had stood by a well with a sentence ready, six days ago, and had found that he had no right to it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-25.md:211:There were five. He drew the cream sheet toward him, and folded it once along its old fold, and put it inside Hesk's book for the night.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:7:It was the start of something, and so he took the inventory, as he took it at the start of anything. Three ribs were broken on the left side, low; they had been taped on the eighty-first day, and the tape was to come off on the ninety-ninth and not before. The calf was bound in a healer's cloth and was closing by itself, as his hurts did. Lira had a shin that was four days old, and an ankle that no healer on this road was going to mend, and one boot. Brom's left wrist wore a linen cuff, and he was to hang nothing from it until the middle of the eighty-seventh day. Karis had been told to ask nothing of herself for three days more, and she was asleep against a box.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:19:"Where else is there grain? I'd meant him to go over the top, when the dry spell came, and be home in six days." The brown eyes came round. "You've told me what's over the top. So he goes round by the one-armed man's ferry, the same as his father went, and that's eight days out and eight home, and the frost won't wait on me while I think about it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:127:He knew, since he could hear her. She had been told a month, and it was plain that she had taken the month as she had taken the mule, as a thing agreed to and not to be discussed. She set the great boot down on its whole sole at every step and looked at the country. There was a good deal of it to see. The road went south down a broad valley of alder and rough pasture, with the grey mountain shrinking behind their left shoulders, and twice a day it forded some small brown water where the mules drank below the crossing. She named nothing and timed nothing. Once, in the middle of the second afternoon, she said across the cart that she had never before walked anywhere slowly enough to see what was growing on a wall, and that it was mostly moss.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:135:He walked behind the cart with his hands hanging, as he had walked behind another cart up a saddle, and every little while he turned his left wrist over and looked at the cuff. He ate his bread with the right hand. When the carter's off mule cast a shoe-nail in the middle of the morning and stood on three legs, it was Seln who held the hoof, and Brom stood by with both arms folded so tightly that the knuckles showed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-23.md:197:She went out along the logs. She did not run them, and Cael, who had watched her run sixty-two hundreds of a mountain, found it stranger to see her walk these. She set the packed boot down in the middle of each log and leaned on it, and waited, with her arms a little out from her sides; and at every one that gave she said "Soft," without turning her head, and stepped over. Seln followed three logs behind with a lump of chalk that the carter kept for his tallies, and drew a cross.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:49:He saw the six days then, all at once, as he saw a bout when it was over. A girl with one arm tied had gone off behind a barn each evening with another man's flint-bag full of pebbles, to teach five fingers the work of ten, so that when the day came she could pay for it herself and ask nobody to hold a purse open for her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:59:"You don't. You know what number it is." She faced him squarely, on her flat foot, with her one arm at her side. "I've had it written in the route book since the shelf, and I've been counting toward it across a mountain. You'd have let it go by. You always let a thing go by when you think that you've not earned it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:191:It was only a number. He had been given a great many, by a great many people, and most of them had been wrong; this one was merely true. A line in some register moved down a row. The last time that he had felt it move, he had been in a room with a brass lock on its door and a house's seal on every paper in it. There was no seal here. It had been said to him across a goat, in a grain hold on no map, by a girl with one arm, who had counted toward it over a mountain so that he should not let it go by.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:203:He took Hesk's book out of the satchel. He had to turn past a leaf to reach a clean one, and he knew that the square woman at the end of the bench had seen which leaf it was. He wetted the pencil, and wrote, with the firelight on the page and five people watching his hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:229:"From the one-armed man, same as before," the carter had said. "You're to read it, and if it's wrong you say so when you're down."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:237:Gault had signed a figure for him every week for two years, to the decimal, and had ended by writing that no instrument would have such a thing to measure again. Pike had one arm and a horn ink-pot, and had never owned an instrument in his life. Somebody had told him that some were lying at a place, and that a crew had gone to look; and he had put down that they were not there, and that the crew was. Cael thought that he would have liked to see the two men at one table.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:245:"He has that," said the carter. "The one-armed man said it under his eave, before and after, with the meadow standing; and the broker's clerk had his own off the plank and away down to the landing while the ink was wet. There's a boat goes west today." He turned his hat round. "Is it wrong, then? I'm to carry the answer."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:257:Seln passed it, and she read it for herself, once, and gave it on to Karis. Cael had written a healer down as a stop, in pencil, a minute before a one-armed man's line came in at the door to say that the country had already entered her otherwise. A clerk's fair copy of that same count was in a boat by now, going west to whoever had put the question.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:309:The steading's woman cleared the board herself. She took six bowls on one arm, and left what was not hers to clear: a basket with three brown apples in it, and a book, and a scrap of bark. Cael drew Hesk's satchel up from under the bench to put the bark away with Pike's other lines.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:355:That was what brought the cold out on him. There was a leaf in the old volume of Hesk's book, the oldest unexplained thing that he owned, with a session's number at its head. Something had once come out of him in a practice yard that was none of his fragments, and it had come from under the breastbone, near the middle of him; a big hand had been laid on him afterward, and its owner had frowned and said that there was a quiet in there, under the noise. He had written the place down. He had turned that leaf over at every inventory since, and added nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:365:He got up, and took Hesk's book to the loft door, and wrote there by the stars, with the frost coming in round his feet and the pencil cold between his fingers.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:387:*Daeva's rematch, Reydan's answer — both ride east. Nineteen. Ten fragments. Hesk's package is on a ferry somewhere. The last one said mind the shape. I'm starting to.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:5:"Smallest first," she said. She had ruled a fresh leaf into five rows and given it to Cael, with the pencil. "A one-armed man has told us that nothing can be declared in there, and five of us felt our Arbiters go, and I believe both. I believed in a ledge as well. There are prints in the grass on the far side of this field, and I am not going to learn that he was mistaken by having Lira crack the sky open a quarter of a mile from whatever left them."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:9:"You did not crack it on purpose the last time. That is rather my point." Karis looked round at them. "So I go first, with one point, unlit. If it sets, I shall know it, and nobody else will, and we stop there and think. If mine does not, then Brom. Then Seln. You are last of the four, and you go only if the three of us have had nothing."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:95:"There is nothing to lift," said Seln. "I looked for it as one feels for a banister. It is not that it resists. It is not there to be asked."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:131:He did not watch her go. If a one-armed man had been wrong for thirty years, the answer to it would come out of the grass on the eastern side, a quarter of a mile off, where three runs crossed a line; and somebody had to have his eyes there when the air broke. He fixed them on that place and kept them on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-29.md:245:Cael watched it happen. Five people had each lost a thing, a stride from where they stood, and had it handed back, and every one of them had turned inward to look at it. Oryn was hobbling the mule, with her lips moving in a count that she did not seem to know she was keeping. Brom knelt by the middle box. Karis sat with her eyes shut, and Lira walked up and down on the great boot, a little apart, and Seln stood looking east.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:169:She began instead with what other people called it. A carter's wife had given her *quiet ground*, across a yard, in three sentences. A one-armed man had said the same two words under an eave, and had told them where the sigil went to sleep. She had those entered as said. They were good words for what they were, which was a warning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:179:So she did not want the name of a place. There was a second reason for that, and it was on a sheet of thin paper in Seln's hand: a ring of charcoal, and beside it the memory of a sheet that had carried two. A one-armed man had said *a handful*. If she called this thing by the name of the Fallow Ring, then on the day that she stood at another she would have nothing to call that one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:247:"It's got a name," said Brom. "Quiet ground. The carter's wife said it, and the one-armed man, and that old keeper at the ford would have, I dare say, if we'd asked him."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:267:"Do we give it to the one-armed man?" said Lira. "He'd say it under his eave twice, to forty people."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:293:Out in the yard somebody was walking toward the western gate, flat-footed, with one arm tied.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:141:"I can't span it. Not with one arm. I'd want a foot in that and both hands on the string."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:297:It was her bad foot. She saw that at once, and she saw in the next breath that it did not matter, since the right foot was to be set down whole, heel and toe together, and to roll nowhere and push at nothing, exactly as a healer had ordered it at a stone. It was the first thing that she had been asked to do with that ankle in twelve days that the ankle was good for.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:341:*What am I becoming, if nobody's measuring.* She had asked that at a fire, and nobody had been able to tell her. She did not know now. But there had never been such a thing before as a Wind who could not run, and might not catch, and shot another person's bow off a rail with one arm; and she thought that she would like to see what that was by the time there was snow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:61:"There's a hook for this in her near pannier. I'll ask her for it when she's on her feet, and not before; she's had enough taken off her this morning." He hung the bow on his shoulder. "And the carter went down light at first light, with Karis's four lines in his hat for the one-armed man, and the hold's mark under them. So that's gone, and it says six."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:67:He lay between two sacks with Hesk's book under his hand. Lira had brought it up the ladder at dusk inside her coat, and with it one sentence, said exactly as a healer had said it: that it was a true page, and that true was not the same as paid. He had written nothing in it since.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:127:So Brom took two sacks at a time, one on each shoulder, and went up the rungs like a man going upstairs to bed. Seln took one, and made no sound with it. Karis sat on an upturned keg at the ladder's foot with the hold's slate on her knee, and cut a stroke for every sack that passed her. Lira had been given nothing, since there was nothing that a person could do with a sack and one arm. She sat on the well's coping with her foot flat on the stones and counted the strokes on Karis's slate, aloud, to annoy her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:129:Cael carried one at a time. It was the first load that he had had on his back since a mountain, and the ribs took it without a word. He went up past his own blanket at each turn, and past Hesk's satchel on its nail, and set the sack down on a heap that had begun level with his knee and now stood as high as his chest; and by the tenth it had come to him that he was being walled into his own bedroom with other people's breakfast.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:143:He knew that lag, for he had run up a ditch in it with eight hounds behind him. Whenever the heaviest thing in him had been used, everything else answered him a beat late for ten counts or twelve, and he had entered that in Hesk's book as a bill and learned to wait it out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:243:On the platform by the western gate the bowman had turned round. It was the one hour of the day when nothing had ever come up that valley, and he seemed to keep it for looking inward, at the hold. He had watched a boy fall about a yard eleven times, and get it right, and he stood with his bow at the length of his arm and gave no sign that it was any business of his. Then his eyes went to the girl on the coping, who had seen a fault from thirty feet with one arm tied, and they stayed there a good deal longer.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:245:Cael wrote one thing in Hesk's book before he went back to the sacks, since he wished it to be in his hand and dated.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:11:Karis had the leaf ready, and she read it standing, as a one-armed man read a line under an eave.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:45:Seln had not moved from the trestle's end. His hand had gone, as it went a dozen times a day, to the strap across his chest, where a locked case hung that he had carried shut on every road past the Line.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:85:The others went up; and Brom took the lantern's fellow from its nail and lit Lira up the ladder ahead of him, a rung at a time, with his big hand held ready under her heel and never touching it. He had been told what that arm might not do, and had decided for himself what the rest of her was likely to want. Seln went last and made no sound.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:145:She went round the rest of them at her stride and a half, in the time that it took to fill a bucket. She turned Brom's hand to the light and gave it back to him. She laid two fingers on Karis's throat, and told her that she had her voice, and that she was not to lecture with it for a week. To Seln, who had not been hurt since he crossed the Line and seemed half ashamed of it, she gave the nod that she gave to a man of her own trade.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:215:Nobody had been told; the boy had gone out through the wicket with a mallet, and that had been enough. The steading's woman stood on her step with her arms folded. Her husband had come round the end of the long house with flour to his elbows, and was explaining to two carters from up the valley that the girl shot from a rail, since she had only the one arm to her, and that he had seen worse done with two. The healer had come as far as the door of the back room, and no farther, and leaned there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:267:She was alone on the walk, and that was new. The lean man had gone down to his supper while there was still light in the west, which Cael had never known him do; and he had left a girl with one arm sitting against the southern post, and the yard bow lying spanned on the planks beside her knee. She had the whole valley in front of her, grey going to blue, with the frost coming down on a mile of pale grass and a white stone standing in it; and it came to him that in thirty years nobody but one man had sat where she was sitting at that hour.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:75:He was back before she had moved from the sill, with Hesk's book held in both hands and a straw shut into it at the place. She did not take it at once. She looked at the hand that held it, as she looked at everything of hers first.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:125:"I can read. It's on your leaf." She shut Hesk's book on the straw. "Say it to me again when it's cost you something."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:141:She had brought Hesk's book with her from the sill, and she tapped its cover with one thick finger.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:183:He lay down. She put the horn cup into his hand, with water in it, and waited until he had drunk. Then she took Hesk's book back and sat down on the three-legged stool herself, for the first time since he had come into that room, with the book on her knee.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-37.md:5:A man asleep draws twelve to the minute. He had timed Brom against Hesk's clock once, on a road, for want of anything better to do, and had kept the figure. Twelve to the minute made sixty in five, and two hundred and forty in the twenty that she had named; and he lay on a scrubbed plank with a healer's hand over his breastbone and began at one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:105:The camp was stirring when he came down the line. The healer lay as she had fallen. The girl was awake, with her eyes on the sky, and the boy sat beside her with a book shut on his knee and one arm held in his lap like a thing that belonged to somebody else. Karis was sitting up against the box. She had her hand flat on its lid, and he knew that she had not yet opened it to look at eleven sheets, and why.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:139:That had been a little while ago. She had been grey when she woke, and she was grey again; a Tide on a road had two in her, and she had spent the whole of yesterday's on one arm.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:197:"The arm. That was last night, and it was one, and it was the size of two. The throat. The hand. Three mendings in two days." She turned her hands over on her knees and looked at them, and let them lie. "That's a route. Near enough, that's what I carry between a hold and the next one, for the people who feed me and shoe the mule. Three serious hurts and a road's worth of small ones. And I've spent it on a road two days from the nearest roof, on a crew that's not on my circuit, sitting against a piece of iron somebody drove where they'd measured to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:277:He took the cream sheet out of Hesk's book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:287:"A person who writes a sum twice, so that it cannot be altered, does not forget where he has put a brass level." Seln held out his hand for the sheet, and when he had it he held it up unread to the afternoon, as he had held it to a lamp, with the light behind. "I said in that inn that it had been written by somebody who learned to write inside. I said that it was not a reason against going. I should have gone on, and asked what it was a reason for."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-34.md:307:"What does he write? The one-armed man. How many?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:7:"It's a dog," said Brom. He had both hands tucked under his arms, like a man kept in from a harvest. "It weighs what a calf weighs. I could have it under one arm."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:29:He could not cut it. He sat on a pannier with his hands on his knees and told Seln where to put the knife, and Seln cut, without expression, as he would have taken dictation. They had three loaves of Thornwater's, and a cheese of the healer's, and five onions. It was two days to the fence.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:63:That night, the ninety-seventh, Lira lay on her back with one arm tied across her chest and found that she could not get comfortable round it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:81:She left the ankle alone. She had asked a healer once, from the back of a mule, and had been told what it was worth and to whom it was owed; and last night that same healer had watched her not ask and had answered regardless. That made twice told. She would not make it twice asked.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:221:It was the first flame that any of them had lit on the Fallow road since the way out, and Cael noticed that nobody discussed it. They were a day west of a grey stone; the ground under them was only ground, and a flint was not a declaration. Seln struck it. He had gathered dead stems of the pale grass by the armful, since there was nothing else in that country to burn, and they went up with a hiss and wanted feeding every minute.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-35.md:311:He got it over his head with one arm and the other, which had come back to him on the road as far as the wrist. He stood by the well's coping in the last of the sun with five bands of linen round him that he had hated on the eighty-sixth day, and measured on the eighty-seventh, and since a certain evening had not once had the leisure to think about.

